window.RIOT = {"generated_for": "riot.brussels", "preview": false, "note": "Brussels-Capital Parliament 2025-2026 — roll-call (nominal) votes from the plenary CRIs, aggregated to political groups. Cards English, source French.", "n_decisions": 42, "sessions_in_table": ["PB_2024-25_00003", "PB_2024-25_00006", "PB_2024-25_00008", "PB_2024-25_00011", "PB_2024-25_00013", "PB_2024-25_00016", "PB_2024-25_00018", "PB_2024-25_00022", "PB_2024-25_00023", "PB_2024-25_00027", "PB_2024-25_00029", "PB_2024-25_00031", "PB_2025-26_00014", "PB_2025-26_00019", "PB_2025-26_00020", "PB_2025-26_00021", "PB_2025-26_00023", "PB_2025-26_00024", "PB_2025-26_00025"], "parties": [{"token": "MR", "name": "MR", "color": "#1356a0", "logo": null}, {"token": "PS", "name": "PS", "color": "#e6002d", "logo": null}, {"token": "PTB", "name": "PTB", "color": "#aa0022", "logo": null}, {"token": "LE", "name": "Les Engagés", "color": "#00a99d", "logo": null}, {"token": "Ecolo", "name": "Ecolo", "color": "#66a821", "logo": null}, {"token": "DEFI", "name": "DéFI", "color": "#e6007e", "logo": null}, {"token": "GRN", "name": "Groen", "color": "#8cc63f", "logo": null}, {"token": "TFA", "name": "Team F. Ahidar", "color": "#6a4ea3", "logo": null}, {"token": "OVLD", "name": "Open VLD", "color": "#2aa0e0", "logo": null}, {"token": "NVA", "name": "N-VA", "color": "#f0a500", "logo": null}, {"token": "VOOR", "name": "Vooruit", "color": "#ff5c39", "logo": null}, {"token": "VB", "name": "Vlaams Belang", "color": "#8b5e3c", "logo": null}, {"token": "CDV", "name": "CD&V", "color": "#f47a20", "logo": null}], "decisions": [{"id": "BXL-2425-00003-v5", "date": "2024-10-04", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00003", "point": 5, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Housing renovation", "headline": "Demand that the government keep paying out the \"Rénolution\" home-renovation premiums without interruption, after the scheme ran out of money and froze new applications.", "title": "Proposition de résolution relative à la continuité du versement des primes « Rénolution » (A-9). Le texte demande au gouvernement d'assurer la poursuite du paiement de ces primes de rénovation. Deux amendements au dispositif, portant sur le tiret 3 et le tiret 4, ont été déposés.", "raw_outcome": "Proposition de résolution relative à la continuité du versement des primes « Rénolution » (A-9). Le texte demande au gouvernement d'assurer la poursuite du paiement de ces primes de rénovation. Deux amendements au dispositif, portant sur le tiret 3 et le tiret 4, ont été déposés.", "source_brief": "Brussels pays \"Rénolution\" premiums to help residents insulate and renovate their homes, but the pot ran dry and the Region stopped processing new applications, leaving households who had already done the work waiting to be paid. This resolution is a formal instruction to the government to guarantee those payments continue.\n\n- **A demand, not a cheque.** A resolution carries no budget of its own; it asks the government to find the money and resume processing the backlog of premium claims.\n- **Who it touches.** The premiums are meant to make insulation and renovation affordable for ordinary households in an ageing, energy-hungry housing stock, not just owners who can front the cost.\n- **Amended on the floor.** Two extra demands were proposed for the operative part of the text.\n- **The rub:** almost no one argues against paying people who already renovated in good faith, so the real question is whether a resolution with no funding attached changes anything, or just records Parliament's wish while the caretaker government decides.", "deep_facts": "- The text is the \"proposition de résolution relative à la continuité du versement des primes « Rénolution »\" (A-9), tabled by Aurélie Czekalski, Isabelle Emmery, Christophe De Beukelaer, Aline Godfrin, Cécile Vainsel, Mounir Laarissi, Imane Belguenani and Pascal Smet (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- A resolution is a non-binding instruction from Parliament to the government; it does not itself open or release credits, but formally requests action on the continued payment of the Rénolution renovation/energy premiums (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- Two amendments to the operative part (\"dispositif\") were tabled: amendment no. 1 on the third indent (\"tiret 3\") and amendment no. 2 on the fourth indent (\"tiret 4\") (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- Context from the same legislature: the Rénolution premium scheme suspended processing of new applications on 16 August 2024 for lack of budget, leaving households unable to be paid for works already carried out, before additional credits were later released (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- Roughly 60% of Brussels housing dates from before 1970 and is often old and energy-inefficient, the renovation of which the premiums are intended to support (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- A resolution is the cheapest possible lever: it commits no money and binds no one, so the substantive choice is whether Parliament should put pressure on a caretaker government that has no full budget to act with, or wait for the incoming majority.\n- Tabling it days before communal elections, with sponsors drawn from several parties, reads as a cross-party signal to anxious renovating households that the premiums will not be abandoned.\n- The two amendments to specific indents suggest a fight over exactly what the government is being asked to commit to, and over how far the finer obligations should reach.\n- A 'no' here is less opposition to the premiums than a statement that resolutions without funding are empty gestures.", "proposed_by": "MR, PS and Vooruit members (Czekalski, Emmery, De Beukelaer, Godfrin, Vainsel, Laarissi, Belguenani, Smet)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00003/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "PTB": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "VOOR": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "NVA": "for", "CDV": "for"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00003-v10", "date": "2024-10-04", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00003", "point": 10, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance", "topic": "Low Emission Zone", "headline": "Postpone the next phase of the Low Emission Zone by two years, pushing the planned 1 January 2025 ban on Euro 5 diesel and Euro 2 petrol cars back to 1 January 2027.", "title": "Proposition d'ordonnance visant à fixer la mise en œuvre de la prochaine phase de la zone de basses émissions (LEZ) au 1er janvier 2027 (A-10). La prochaine étape, qui devait entrer en vigueur le 1er janvier 2025 et viser les véhicules diesel Euro 5 et essence Euro 2, est ainsi reportée de deux ans. Trois amendements à l'article 2, élargissant les dérogations ou prolongeant l'accès de certains véhicules, ont été déposés.", "raw_outcome": "Proposition d'ordonnance visant à fixer la mise en œuvre de la prochaine phase de la zone de basses émissions (LEZ) au 1er janvier 2027 (A-10). La prochaine étape, qui devait entrer en vigueur le 1er janvier 2025 et viser les véhicules diesel Euro 5 et essence Euro 2, est ainsi reportée de deux ans. Trois amendements à l'article 2, élargissant les dérogations ou prolongeant l'accès de certains véhicules, ont été déposés.", "source_brief": "Brussels' Low Emission Zone tightens step by step, banning ever-older, more polluting cars from the city. The next tightening was scheduled to take effect on 1 January 2025 and would have shut out Euro 5 diesel and Euro 2 petrol vehicles. This ordinance pushes that deadline back two years, to 1 January 2027.\n\n- **What changes.** Cars that were due to be banned in weeks get a two-year reprieve to keep entering the zone.\n- **The social case.** Owners of these vehicles, often lower-income drivers and small tradespeople, are told they were facing the cost of scrapping a working car or buying a new one on short notice.\n- **The health case.** The vehicles targeted by the delayed phase are described as responsible for 40% of nitrogen-oxide emissions while making up a small share of the fleet; air quality breaches EU and WHO norms across most of the city.\n- **The rub:** the choice is whether to spare tens of thousands of car owners a costly switch right now, or hold the line on a seven-year-old, publicly announced clean-air calendar that doctors and environmental groups say is already overdue.", "deep_facts": "- The text is the \"proposition d'ordonnance visant à fixer la mise en œuvre de la prochaine phase de la zone de basses émissions (LEZ) au 1er janvier 2027\" (A-10), tabled by Anne-Charlotte d'Ursel, Jamal Ikazban, Mounir Laarissi, David Weytsman, Ibrahim Dönmez, Sofia Bennani, Imane Belguenani, Leila Agic, Elhadj Moussa Diallo and Olivier Willocx (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- The next LEZ phase was due to enter into force on 1 January 2025; the ordinance sets its implementation date at 1 January 2027 instead, a two-year postponement (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- The vehicles concerned by the delayed phase are Euro 5 diesel and Euro 2 (and older) petrol cars; Euro 5 diesels are described as the vehicles at the centre of the dieselgate scandal, able to pollute as much as Euro 3 diesels already banned for five years (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- These vehicles were stated to make up 9.3% of the fleet while causing 40% of nitrogen-oxide (NOx) emissions (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- On the current (un-delayed) schedule, diesel cars could still enter the Region for six more years and petrol cars for eleven more years (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- Air-quality figures cited: since the LEZ was introduced, Brussels air quality improved by 30–40%, more so in disadvantaged areas; 98% of the population lives where air fails the WHO norm of 10 µg/m³; the EU PM norm of 40 µg/m³ is exceeded at four sites, and the future EU norm of 20 µg/m³ would be missed at nine of thirteen measuring stations (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- Health context cited: respiratory problems are among the most common reasons for doctor visits and sick leave in Brussels; some 200,000 residents have respiratory problems and 80,000 suffer from asthma; life expectancy is markedly lower in Saint-Josse-ten-Noode than in Woluwe-Saint-Pierre (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- Existing accompanying measures were referenced: the Bruxell'air premium of more than 1,000 euros; the LEZ premium of up to 16,000 euros for professionals with a utility vehicle; retrofit support to replace a combustion engine with an electric one; electric-vehicle leasing; charging-point installation; and existing derogations for persons with reduced mobility and school transport of disabled children (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- Three amendments to Article 2 were tabled: amendment no. 1 (de Patoul) widening derogations on socio-economic grounds, for low-mileage vehicles under 3,000 km/year and for holders of a medical certificate; amendment no. 2 (Rittweger de Moor / Michaux Maimone) granting Euro 5+ diesel and Euro 2+ petrol vehicles automatic, free LEZ access until 31 December 2034; and amendment no. 3 extending the same postponement to taxi-sector vehicles (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- Once adopted, the ordinance is submitted to the government for sanction (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).\n- The proposal was debated jointly with three other ordinance texts on the LEZ (A-11 De Smedt establishing a moratorium on the LEZ phases; A-12 Smet/Mouani postponing the access restriction; A-13 Ahidar postponing the restriction on Euro 5 / Euro V diesel vehicles) (CRI, 4 Oct 2024).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The vote pits an immediate, concrete cost on a defined group of car owners against a diffuse, statistical health benefit for the whole population, the classic shape of a clean-air fight, sharpened by being decided weeks before communal elections.\n- Changing a seven-year-old calendar three months before it bites is framed by opponents as a governance problem in itself: people who already scrapped or replaced compliant-by-2025 cars get no reprieve, while those who waited are rewarded.\n- The amendments map the alternatives on the table: a narrow social carve-out, a near-total exemption to 2034, and a taxi-specific delay, set against a clean two-year slip for everyone rather than targeted relief.\n- Joining four competing texts into one debate frames a choice between the mildest option (a fixed two-year delay), a full moratorium and the status quo.", "proposed_by": "MR, PS and Vooruit members (d'Ursel, Ikazban, Laarissi, Weytsman, Dönmez, Bennani, Belguenani, Agic, Diallo, Willocx)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00003/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "PTB": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "against", "GRN": "against", "Ecolo": "against", "CDV": "abstain", "VOOR": "abstain", "NVA": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00006-v4", "date": "2024-12-06", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00006", "point": 4, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Proposition d'ordonnance", "topic": "Taxi zero-emission", "headline": "Push back to 1 January 2027 the deadline forcing Brussels taxis to buy zero-emission (electric/hydrogen) vehicles — a two-year delay from the 1 January 2025 date set by decree.", "title": "Proposition d'ordonnance visant à fixer au 1er janvier 2027 l'échéance, pour les services de taxis, d'acquérir des véhicules zéro émission. Les signataires présentent ce report de deux ans comme nécessaire pour un secteur confronté à des coûts fixes élevés, un manque de bornes de recharge rapide et une autonomie insuffisante des véhicules électriques. Près de 700 véhicules atteindront la limite d'âge de sept ans et devraient être remplacés d'ici fin 2025.", "raw_outcome": "Proposition d'ordonnance visant à fixer au 1er janvier 2027 l'échéance, pour les services de taxis, d'acquérir des véhicules zéro émission. Les signataires présentent ce report de deux ans comme nécessaire pour un secteur confronté à des coûts fixes élevés, un manque de bornes de recharge rapide et une autonomie insuffisante des véhicules électriques. Près de 700 véhicules atteindront la limite d'âge de sept ans et devraient être remplacés d'ici fin 2025.", "source_brief": "A regional decree currently requires Brussels taxis to switch to zero-emission vehicles when they renew their licence, with the obligation biting from **1 January 2025**. This ordinance moves that deadline to **1 January 2027**, giving the sector two more years before the rule applies.\n\n- **Who it touches:** about **700 taxis** hit the seven-year age limit and would have to be replaced by emission-free vehicles by end-2025; the active fleet is **3,250**, of which only **240** are already electric.\n- **The cost argument:** a thermal taxi can be bought for around **€30,000**, while a comparable-capacity electric one runs two to three times more (one group cited **€60,000–80,000**); drivers are mostly small self-employed operators who receive no public purchase subsidy in Brussels.\n- **The counter-argument:** an official task force judged the public charging network adequate, and opponents warn that fine particles and CO2 from road traffic harm residents' lungs — delaying the switch keeps higher-emitting cars on the road two more years.\n- **The rub:** is a two-year reprieve a fair fix for a struggling sector facing infrastructure gaps, or a retreat from air-quality and climate commitments without a contradictory hearing of the experts?", "deep_facts": "- The text sets at **1 January 2027** the deadline for taxi services to acquire zero-emission vehicles; it is the ordinance route (A-38), given priority over a competing resolution (A-20) under the hierarchy-of-norms principle, which thereby became without object (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- The obligation originally applied from **1 January 2025** under an arrêté; the proposers present the two-year postponement as necessary for a sector facing high fixed costs, a shortage of fast charging points, and insufficient electric-vehicle range (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- The obligation does not hit all taxis at once: a driver is bound only at **renewal of their authorisation**. Near **700 vehicles** reach the seven-year age limit and would have to be replaced by zero-emission vehicles by end-2025 (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Fleet figures cited: roughly **240 electric taxis** are already running out of a total of **3,250** active taxis in the Region; some operators installed charging points or solar panels with no public aid (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Price figures cited: a vehicle suitable for paid passenger transport can be acquired for about **€30,000**, whereas a good-capacity electric vehicle 'often costs double, even triple' that amount; Team Fouad Ahidar cited a purchase cost of **€60,000 to €80,000** as a brake on electrification (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- A government condition stated the obligation could apply only if the Region had a sufficient number of charging points by 2025. In September, the electric-charging-points task force presented a study to the government concluding the network was adapted to the sector's needs (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Proposers contest that study: they say it was done without the sector's collaboration, relies on manufacturers' range data (range drops sharply with AC/heating), assumes most drivers charge at night, and that Belgian public-charging tariffs are among Europe's highest (third for slow charging, behind Croatia and Denmark, per a Transport & Mobility Leuven study) (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Bruxelles Mobilité took position in the dossier asking to postpone the obligation set for 1 January 2025, citing inadequate and insufficient electrical infrastructure; there are no hydrogen stations in the Region and no charging points exclusively reserved for taxis (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Positions stated in committee (26 Nov): PS, MR, PTB, N-VA and Team Fouad Ahidar favourable to the postponement; Les Engagés, Groen and Ecolo opposed; DéFI favoured a one-year rather than two-year delay (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Amendments tabled: a PTB amendment for an intermediate review in September 2026 to verify accompanying measures and fast-charging deployment; a DéFI amendment for a one-year postponement (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Les Engagés argued the arrêté of 6 October 2022 already allowed a postponement if charging infrastructure was deemed insufficient, but the task force gave a favourable opinion on the initial measure; they proposed either auditioning the task force or rejecting the text and following the initial deadline (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Ecolo argued the existing **7,000 charging points** suffice and that electrification is profitable long-term via lower operating costs; Groen stressed fine-particle harm to residents with pulmonary/respiratory conditions (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Procedural sequence: two amendments to Article 2 were tabled, followed by Article 2 non-amended and then the ensemble; once adopted, the ordinance is to be submitted to the government for sanction (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- The seven-year age limit on taxis is cited as a guarantee that thermal vehicles in service are modern and low-emitting; the sector states it is not against decarbonisation but judged the 1 January 2025 date unworkable (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The split is not left-vs-right but environment-vs-livelihood: a PS–MR–PTB–N-VA bloc lined up behind the sector, while the green family (Ecolo, Groen) plus Les Engagés held the air-quality line — an unusual cross-ideological coalition that signals taxi-driver constituencies weigh heavily across parties.\n- Choosing the ordinance over the resolution, and the choice on the PTB 'review in 2026' and the DéFI 'one year' amendments, decides whether the delay is a clean two years or a conditional, checkpointed one — the unconditional reprieve is what the sector wanted and what opponents most distrust.\n- Much of the floor speech attacks the task force study's methodology; functionally this pre-empts the 'the network is ready' objection, since if the official adequacy finding stands, the legal basis for delay (insufficient infrastructure) weakens.\n- The framing of taxis as small self-employed operators from working-class neighbourhoods reframes an emissions question as a social-equity one, raising the political cost of voting against the delay.", "proposed_by": "Jamal Ikazban, Ahmed Laaouej, Ibrahim Dönmez, Leila Agic, Amin El Boujdaini, Sevket Temiz, Aline Godfrin, Sadik Köksal, David Weytsman, Loubna Azghoud (PS, MR e.a.)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00006/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "OVLD": "for", "VB": "for", "LE": "against", "NVA": "for", "DEFI": "abstain", "CDV": "against", "GRN": "against", "Ecolo": "against", "VOOR": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00006-v5", "date": "2024-12-06", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00006", "point": 5, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Proposition d'ordonnance", "topic": "Public-domain tax", "headline": "Confirm in law that public- and private-domain buildings used for general-interest missions stay subject to regional taxes (unless an exemption is expressly granted) — closing an interpretation gap that could otherwise have widened tax exemptions from 1 January 2025.", "title": "Proposition d'ordonnance modifiant l'ordonnance du 4 avril 2024 portant le Code des finances publiques de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale, afin de clarifier l'article 175. Elle précise, sans ambiguïté, que tous les biens du domaine public et privé affectés à des missions d'intérêt général sont bien soumis aux taxes et impôts régionaux, sauf dérogation expresse, préservant ainsi les recettes fiscales de la Région.", "raw_outcome": "Proposition d'ordonnance modifiant l'ordonnance du 4 avril 2024 portant le Code des finances publiques de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale, afin de clarifier l'article 175. Elle précise, sans ambiguïté, que tous les biens du domaine public et privé affectés à des missions d'intérêt général sont bien soumis aux taxes et impôts régionaux, sauf dérogation expresse, préservant ainsi les recettes fiscales de la Région.", "source_brief": "When the Region's Code of Public Finances was overhauled in April 2024, its Article 175 — due to take effect on **1 January 2025** — turned out to be open to several readings, one of which could have broadened tax exemptions for public-domain property. This ordinance rewrites it to state plainly that such property is taxable unless an exemption is expressly granted.\n\n- **What is at stake:** the Region's ability to keep taxing buildings on the public and private domain; the change is presented as preserving regional tax revenue and avoiding losses of fiscal receipts in 2025.\n- **The legal anchor:** it codifies settled **Court of Cassation** case-law (public-domain goods exempt from *communal* taxes only) and aligns with the **Conseil d'État** opinion given in early 2024; proposers stress it changes nothing in current policy and never intended a general exemption regime.\n- **The rub:** a 'merely technical' clarification that nonetheless decides whether public-interest property can be broadly shielded from regional tax — a precaution worth locking in, or a fix being rushed through just before the article takes effect?", "deep_facts": "- The proposition modifies the ordinance of 4 April 2024 bearing the Code of Public Finances of the Brussels-Capital Region, specifically clarifying **Article 175**, whose entry into force is set at **1 January 2025** (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- After publication of the new code in the Moniteur belge, several interpretations of Article 175 appeared possible; the stated object is to specify, unambiguously, that all goods of the public and private domain assigned to general-interest missions are subject to regional taxes and levies, **save express derogation** (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Scope: the rule concerns the domanial goods of the Region as well as those of other public entities (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- The clarification was introduced via an amendment in committee; it is described as deleting a provision that merely confirmed the established Court of Cassation case-law under which public-domain goods are exempt from **communal** taxes (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Stated rationale: when the ordinance was approved in 2024 there was no intention to institute a general tax-exemption regime that would prevent the Region from taxing buildings; the change aims to secure the legal certainty of the Region's fiscal revenue and avoid losses or risk of losses of fiscal receipts in 2025 (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- The modification is stated to conform to the initial opinion rendered by the **Conseil d'État** in early 2024 on the draft new code (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- Process: examined by the Finance and General Affairs committee on **2 December 2024**; in plenary, Articles 1, 2 and 3 were taken without observation before the vote on the ensemble (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).\n- The text is to be submitted to the government for sanction (CRI, 6 Dec 2024).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- Despite the 'technical' label, the item is a revenue-protection move: left unfixed, the ambiguous Article 175 risked being read as a broad exemption, which would have eroded the Region's tax base — so the vote is effectively for or against keeping public-interest property taxable.\n- The cross-bench authorship (Open Vld, DéFI) suggests broad consensus that this is housekeeping rather than a contested policy choice; any reservations are likely to read as concerns about substance-by-amendment or timing rather than opposition to the principle.\n- Acting before the article's 1 January 2025 entry into force is the point: fixing it after the fact would have meant litigating exemption claims, so the urgency is about pre-empting interpretation disputes, not changing policy direction.", "proposed_by": "Imane Belguenani (Open Vld), Stijn Bex, Jonathan de Patoul (DéFI)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00006/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "CDV": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "GRN": "for", "VOOR": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "NVA": "for", "PTB": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00008-v22", "date": "2024-12-19", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00008", "point": 22, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance", "topic": "Public finance", "headline": "Authorise the Brussels-Capital Region to keep operating without a real 2025 budget by opening provisional \"twelfths\" credits to fund the administration through January, February and March 2025.", "title": "Projet d'ordonnance de finances ouvrant des crédits provisoires pour les mois de janvier, février et mars 2025 sur le budget de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale pour l'année budgétaire 2025. Faute de gouvernement de plein exercice, la Région doit fonctionner avec des douzièmes provisoires, lesquels assurent la continuité des services. La Cour des comptes, n'ayant disposé que de cinq jours ouvrables, a exprimé une opinion défavorable sur ce texte.", "raw_outcome": "Projet d'ordonnance de finances ouvrant des crédits provisoires pour les mois de janvier, février et mars 2025 sur le budget de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale pour l'année budgétaire 2025. Faute de gouvernement de plein exercice, la Région doit fonctionner avec des douzièmes provisoires, lesquels assurent la continuité des services. La Cour des comptes, n'ayant disposé que de cinq jours ouvrables, a exprimé une opinion défavorable sur ce texte.", "source_brief": "Six months after the June elections the Region still has no new government and no full 2025 budget, so this text keeps the lights on a different way: it releases \"provisional twelfths\" — monthly slices of last year's budget — to pay salaries, services and bills for the first quarter of 2025. Vote it through and the administration keeps running; reject it and the Region has no legal basis to spend in January.\n\n- **Budgeting by stopgap.** Instead of a proper 2025 budget debated line by line, the caretaker government asks for permission to spend by reference to the 2024 budget, three months at a time.\n- **A watchdog cried foul.** The independent Cour des comptes was given only five working days to review the text and issued an unfavourable opinion, saying the three-twelfths rule was exceeded in places without proper justification.\n- **Against a heavy backdrop.** Regional debt was cited in debate as having climbed from 4 billion euros in 2017 to about 14 billion euros now.\n- **The rub:** voting yes guarantees public services don't stop — but it also signs off on spending with weak oversight and lets the political deadlock continue without a real budget being put on the table.", "deep_facts": "- The text is the \"projet d'ordonnance de finances ouvrant des crédits provisoires pour les mois de janvier, février et mars 2025 sur le budget de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale pour l'année budgétaire 2025\" (A-47) (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- It allows the Region to spend during the first quarter of 2025 by reference to the 2024 initial budget, in the absence of a full 2025 budget; the caretaker government is in affaires courantes following the June 2024 elections, with no new government formed more than six months later (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- The Cour des comptes (Rekenhof) issued an unfavourable opinion on the text, stating it had only five working days to analyse it, which it deemed insufficient; it noted the three-twelfths rule (règle des trois douzièmes) was exceeded in numerous places and that appropriate justification notes were missing (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- Debate referenced an attempt to subject the Cour des comptes' audits to a steering committee including representatives of the Brussels finance/budget minister, later withdrawn at the Court's request (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- Regional debt was cited as having risen from 4 billion euros in 2017 to about 14 billion euros at the time of debate (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- The Region's A+ rating with stable outlook was referenced, along with a debt-ratio threshold of 210% noted as already exceeded (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- Amendments tabled would reduce several provisional credits back towards the standard three-twelfths: for the Parliament's credits (programme 100, mission 34) to 3.6 twelfths instead of fourteen; for provisions management (programme 043, mission 36) to three twelfths instead of twelve; for the titres-services scheme (programme 048, mission 44) to four twelfths of the 2024 initial budget; and for the Fonds régional bruxellois de refinancement des trésoreries communales (programme 253, mission 40) from twelve to a little under eleven twelfths (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- The amendments were stated not to affect public-service operations, public-sector salaries, the employment of domestic-help workers (aides ménagères / huishoudhulpen) or the functioning of approved titres-services firms (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- The Kanal-Centre Pompidou cultural project was cited in the wider debate at a cost of at least 172 million euros, with 185 million euros referenced as the contractual figure, 180 million engaged and 164 million liquidated to the Fondation Kanal (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- The project of ordinance, once adopted, is submitted to the government for sanction (\"soumis à la sanction du gouvernement\") (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- Provisional twelfths are the legal life-support of a region without a government: they guarantee continuity but freeze policy, since no new spending choices can be made — only last year's pattern, sliced monthly.\n- The Cour des comptes' five-day window and unfavourable opinion are the real flashpoint: a 'yes' is partly a vote to spend with oversight the watchdog itself called inadequate, which is why several groups back the credits but distance themselves from the underlying budget management.\n- The amendments pulling credits back towards three twelfths read as a discipline move — an attempt to limit how far ahead a caretaker government can pre-commit money before negotiators form a real majority.\n- Naming Kanal and the debt trajectory in a routine continuity vote turns the stopgap into a proxy fight over whether Brussels can still afford its flagship projects.", "proposed_by": "Brussels-Capital Government (caretaker / affaires courantes)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00008/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "VOOR": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "PTB": "abstain", "CDV": "abstain", "NVA": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00008-v25", "date": "2024-12-19", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00008", "point": 25, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance", "topic": "Housing & energy", "headline": "Release extra budget credits to restart and keep paying the Rénolution home-renovation and energy premiums to Brussels households, after the scheme ran out of money and stopped processing applications in August 2024.", "title": "Projet d'ordonnance portant mise en vigueur de la délibération budgétaire n° 02 concernant la poursuite des paiements des primes Rénolution. Les primes ont été victimes de leur succès et le traitement des demandes a été suspendu le 16 août 2024, faute de budget ; 72 millions d'euros supplémentaires sont injectés, répartis entre Bruxelles Environnement et urban.brussels, pour répondre aux demandes introduites.", "raw_outcome": "Projet d'ordonnance portant mise en vigueur de la délibération budgétaire n° 02 concernant la poursuite des paiements des primes Rénolution. Les primes ont été victimes de leur succès et le traitement des demandes a été suspendu le 16 août 2024, faute de budget ; 72 millions d'euros supplémentaires sont injectés, répartis entre Bruxelles Environnement et urban.brussels, pour répondre aux demandes introduites.", "source_brief": "Brussels pays \"Rénolution\" premiums to help residents insulate and renovate their homes — but the pot ran dry and on 16 August 2024 the Region stopped processing new applications, leaving thousands of households who had already done the work waiting to be paid. This text frees up additional money so those payments can resume.\n\n- **What it funds.** A budget delegation injecting extra credits, split between Bruxelles Environnement and urban.brussels, to clear the backlog of premium claims for 2024 works.\n- **Who it touches.** Roughly 60% of Brussels homes pre-date 1970 and are energy-hungry; the premiums are meant to make renovation affordable for ordinary households, not just wealthy ones who can front the cost.\n- **A recurring failure.** Debate noted the budget had run out before year-end in both 2023 and 2024 — the scheme is repeatedly \"a victim of its own success\".\n- **The rub:** few argue against helping residents renovate — the fight is whether this money is actually enough and well-targeted, given the government's own cost estimate was challenged as unrealistic and questions over which households qualify went unanswered.", "deep_facts": "- The text is the \"projet d'ordonnance portant mise en vigueur de la délibération budgétaire n° 02 du Gouvernement de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale autorisant l'engagement, la liquidation et le paiement des dépenses, à imputer au budget général des dépenses de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale pour l'année budgétaire 2024 concernant la poursuite des paiements des primes Rénolution\" (A-44) (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- Under this budget delegation, 72 million euros in additional credits are injected, split between Bruxelles Environnement and urban.brussels, described as sufficient to meet the premium applications introduced (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- Processing of premium applications was suspended on 16 August 2024 for lack of budget, after which households could not continue their renovation works (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- Debate stated the Rénolution premium budgets had been exhausted before year-end in both 2023 and 2024 (\"victimes de leur succès\") (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- A figure of 18 million euros was cited as provided for premiums destined to households (\"primes destinées aux ménages\") (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- On the related provisional 2025 credit, the budget was said to be provisioned at 66% of the 2024 budget, corresponding to a liquidation credit of 29.6 million euros for 2025 Rénolution premiums managed by Bruxelles Environnement (mission 041) (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- The government's cost estimate for the measure was cited at 29 million euros, contrasted in debate with 140 million euros spent in the first half of 2024, a comparison one member called unrealistic (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- Debate flagged an unresolved ambiguity over eligibility — whether the claimant's invoice had to be merely emitted, or also paid, by 31 December 2024 — with a stated risk of back-dated invoices (factures antidatées) (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- It was noted that close to 60% of Brussels housing dates from before 1970 and is often old and energy-inefficient; concern was raised that renovation can push up rents and that mainly wealthier households benefit from the current premium system (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).\n- The application portal was said to remain open in 2025, with a call for clear communication to citizens on eligibility conditions; the ordinance, once adopted, is submitted to the government for sanction (CRI, 19 Dec 2024).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The vote is less about whether to help renovators than about cleaning up a budgeting failure: the scheme was stopped mid-year because demand was under-budgeted, and this text retro-funds the gap.\n- The unresolved \"emitted vs paid by 31 December\" question is the real lever — it silently decides which households actually get paid and how big the bill is, which is why members pressed for clarity and warned of back-dated invoices.\n- The 29-million-euro estimate set against 140 million euros spent in H1 2024 suggests the credit may again be under-sized, setting up a repeat of the mid-year freeze.\n- The structural critique — that premiums favour households able to front the cost, and that renovation can raise rents — frames a citizen's choice between keeping a flawed-but-real subsidy flowing and demanding the scheme be redesigned before more money goes in.", "proposed_by": "Brussels-Capital Government (caretaker / affaires courantes)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00008/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "VOOR": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "NVA": "for", "CDV": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00011-v5", "date": "2025-01-31", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00011", "point": 5, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance (projet d'ordonnance)", "topic": "Energy / horeca", "headline": "Ban outdoor gas patio heaters for Brussels horeca from June 2026, while keeping electric terrace heaters permanently allowed — carving them out of the regional energy-sobriety ordinance.", "title": "Le projet d'ordonnance transpose dans la loi bruxelloise des mesures de sobriete energetique prevues dans le plan air-climat-energie de 2022 : fermeture des portes de commerce, extinction des enseignes lumineuses et interdiction des chauffages de terrasse. Un amendement adopte en seance pleniere a exonere les chaufferettes electriques de l'interdiction, ne maintenant que le ban sur les appareils a gaz a partir de juin 2026. Le ministre Maron a souleve un risque juridique d'inegalite de traitement entre commercants selon le type d'equipement.", "raw_outcome": "Le projet d'ordonnance transpose dans la loi bruxelloise des mesures de sobriete energetique prevues dans le plan air-climat-energie de 2022 : fermeture des portes de commerce, extinction des enseignes lumineuses et interdiction des chauffages de terrasse. Un amendement adopte en seance pleniere a exonere les chaufferettes electriques de l'interdiction, ne maintenant que le ban sur les appareils a gaz a partir de juin 2026. Le ministre Maron a souleve un risque juridique d'inegalite de traitement entre commercants selon le type d'equipement.", "source_brief": "A Brussels ordinance transposes energy-sobriety rules from the regional Air-Climate-Energy Plan into law: shop doors must be closed, illuminated signs turned off at night, and outdoor heating on commercial terraces banned. At article-by-article discussion, a cross-party amendment (Vooruit.brussels + MR) stripped the words \"de l'électricité ou\" from the ban, exempting electric heaters from it indefinitely while the gas-heater ban from June 2026 stood.\n\n- **What the ordinance does:** codifies into law a 2022 royal decree requiring energy-saving behaviour from Brussels businesses — closing entrance doors, shutting off illuminated advertising overnight, and banning all terrace heaters.\n- **The flashpoint amendment:** the Fédération Horeca Bruxelles lobbied for electric heaters to be excluded; supporters argue gas combustion produces 40 times more greenhouse gases than nuclear electricity and 490 times more than renewable, making the parallel with electric vehicles coherent.\n- **Minister's counter:** Minister Maron warned of a likely equal-treatment legal challenge — electric heaters consume at least as many kWh as gas ones, so banning gas but allowing electric ad vitam aeternam undermines the ordinance's energy-reduction purpose and may be struck down by the Constitutional Court.\n- **The rub:** supporters say the amendment protects a struggling horeca sector with negligible climate impact; opponents argue heating open air with electricity is the same ecological absurdity as heating it with gas, and that Paris is the only city to have banned such heaters at all, suggesting the whole provision is largely symbolic.", "deep_facts": "- The text is a projet d'ordonnance modifying various provisions of Brussels energy, climate and waste law; its core aim is to give legal force to energy-sobriety measures first introduced by a 2022 arrêté under the Plan air-climat-énergie, covering illuminated signs, open shop doors and all outdoor terrace heating (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- Articles 9 and 10 of the ordinance, as originally drafted, banned 'de l'électricité ou du gaz' for terrace heating on commercial premises; amendments no. 1 and no. 2, tabled by Pascal Smet (Vooruit.brussels) and co-signers from MR, deleted 'de l'électricité ou' from each article, making the ban gas-only with no sunset date for electric (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- Amendment mover Smet argued that only one city in the world — Paris — has banned electric heaters, and that even there controls are not strictly enforced and the ban is circumvented; he said the measure was 'purely symbolic' as electric heater consumption is negligible in total Brussels energy use (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- MR speaker Clémentine Barzin cited the Fédération Horeca Bruxelles as having itself requested the amendment, arguing gas combustion produces 40 times more greenhouse gases than nuclear electricity and 490 times more than renewable electricity, and that the analogy with banning fossil-fuel cars while allowing electric ones was correct (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- Minister Maron rebutted the car analogy: the LEZ aims to improve air quality by phasing out the most polluting vehicles, whereas this ordinance aims to reduce total energy consumption regardless of source; electric heaters consume at least as many kWh as gas ones, making any ban on gas-only heaters incoherent in energy terms (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- Maron also warned of a 'risque juridique évident' — an equal-treatment challenge: how can merchants who invested in gas heaters be required to remove them while those who invested in electric heaters may continue ad vitam aeternam, given both consume the same energy? (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- Ecolo speaker Soiresse Njall argued the amendment offered 'no perspective' — electric heaters would remain allowed even after the horeca sector recovered, sending no signal on energy sobriety; heating outdoor air, whether with gas or electricity, is 'une absurdité écologique' (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- Groen speaker Stijn Bex, himself a lawyer, questioned whether the gas/electricity distinction had been sufficiently demonstrated to justify different legal treatment, and noted Parliament had already seen one law — the LEZ amendment — fail before the Constitutional Court since the elections (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- Amendment no. 1 to article 9 passed 43 yes / 16 no / 17 abstentions; the whole ordinance as amended passed 41 yes / 18 no / 17 abstentions (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The amendment's real logic is economic, not environmental: in a period of coalition formation and with horeca under financial stress, allowing electric heaters hands a business-friendly coalition partner a visible, low-cost win — the climate argument is a post-hoc justification for what is structurally a sector-relief measure.\n- Maron's equal-treatment warning is technically credible but strategically useful to the majority: framing a likely Constitutional Court loss as the opponents' problem lets the majority claim it acted in good faith if the provision is later struck, without having to reverse it themselves.\n- The 'only Paris did this, and Paris doesn't enforce it' move reframes legislative boldness as naïve exceptionalism, a recurring technique to normalise inaction by pointing to lax enforcement elsewhere rather than to the policy objective.\n- The ordinance nonetheless retains its core architecture: gas heaters banned from 2026, shop doors closed, illuminated signs off at night — the electric-heater carve-out is politically visible but energy-marginal, which is precisely why the coalition could afford to grant it.", "proposed_by": "Projet d'ordonnance tabled by Minister Alain Maron (Ecolo), government of the Brussels-Capital Region; amendment no. 1 (art. 9) and amendment no. 2 (art. 10) tabled by Pascal Smet (Vooruit.brussels), Aurélie Czekalski, Clémentine Barzin, Gaëtan Van Goidsenhoven, Geoffroy Coomans de Brachène, Loubna Azghoud, Ludivine de Magnanville, Olivier Willocx, Anne-Charlotte d'Ursel, Ilyas Mouani (MR/Vooruit.brussels coalition)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00011/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "VOOR": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "PTB": "against", "NVA": "against", "MR": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00011-v9", "date": "2025-01-31", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00011", "point": 9, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Housing / homelessness", "headline": "Demand that Brussels social housing bodies prioritise homeless people in allocations, strengthen Housing First, and better coordinate housing and social-health policy to fight street homelessness.", "title": "La proposition de resolution demande a la Region bruxelloise de mobiliser les societes de logement social (SISP, SLRB) et les agences immobilieres sociales en faveur des personnes sans abri, d'ameliorer l'acces aux garanties locatives via le Fonds du logement et de mieux articuler les politiques du logement et celles du social-sante. Un amendement visant a porter progressivement a 33 % d'ici 2030 la part des attributions reservees aux sans-abri, inspire du plan directeur de Bruss'help, a ete rejete.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition de resolution demande a la Region bruxelloise de mobiliser les societes de logement social (SISP, SLRB) et les agences immobilieres sociales en faveur des personnes sans abri, d'ameliorer l'acces aux garanties locatives via le Fonds du logement et de mieux articuler les politiques du logement et celles du social-sante. Un amendement visant a porter progressivement a 33 % d'ici 2030 la part des attributions reservees aux sans-abri, inspire du plan directeur de Bruss'help, a ete rejete.", "source_brief": "Brussels counts at least 10,000 people sleeping rough or in precarious shelter, against a backdrop of more than 50,000 households on the social-housing waiting list. This resolution, prepared over the previous legislature, asks the Brussels Region to better arm its social-housing operators (SLRB, SISP) to prioritise homeless people, involve communes and CPAS more actively, and hard-wire Housing First logic into allocation policy.\n\n- **What it asks:** that social housing companies (SISP) progressively increase the share of allocations bypassing the waiting list in favour of homeless people, that the Fonds du logement improve access to rental guarantees, and that housing and social-health policy be formally bridged.\n- **The contested amendment:** Ecolo tabled an amendment drawing on article 11 of Bruss'help's master plan, targeting 33 % of annual SISP allocations (roughly 2,000 units) for homeless people by 2030 — nearly double the current derogation rate; it was rejected.\n- **Who abstained and why:** a broad spread of groups — MR, PS, Team Fouad Ahidar, PTB, N-VA, Les Engagés, Open Vld, Vlaams Belang, DéFI — abstained on procedural and substantive grounds: the base text was seen as outdated (its original version dated from February 2024), several measures had already been implemented by the outgoing government, and critics argued the quota approach risked pitting two groups of poor people against each other in a market already short of supply.\n- **The rub:** supporters argue that without a Housing First anchor in allocation law the Brussels homeless count will keep rising; opponents counter that reserving a fixed share of scarce social housing for one vulnerable group, without new supply or clear support budgets, simply organises competition between the poor.", "deep_facts": "- The latest Brussels homeless count (carried out November 2024, figures not yet published at the time of debate) was expected to exceed the approximately 7,000 recorded in the previous official count; multiple speakers cited the figure of at least 10,000 people sleeping rough or in precarious situations (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- More than 50,000 households were on the Brussels social-housing waiting list at the time of debate, with average waiting times exceeding twelve years according to several speakers; N-VA placed the figure at up to 60,000 (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- The outgoing Brussels government had in April 2024 adopted an arrêté raising the minimum quota of social housing allocated to homeless people to 3 % from January 2025, 5 % in 2026, and 6 % in 2027; the resolution was designed to support and reinforce that trajectory (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- Ecolo's amendment, drawn from article 11 of the Bruss'help master plan, sought a target of 33 % of annual SISP allocations for homeless people by 2030, equating to roughly 738 of the 2,214 social housing units allocated per year; opponents noted that of those 2,214 annual allocations, only about 1,000 actually become free for waiting-list families — the rest going to internal rotations and existing derogations (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- A ULB applied-economics study cited in debate concluded that accompanying homeless people into durable rehousing is economically more efficient than maintaining emergency-shelter systems; this was cited as the academic foundation for the Housing First approach in the resolution (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- Glasgow was cited by Ecolo as having instituted an 80 % social-housing allocation quota for homeless people despite having a lower social-housing stock than Brussels, as evidence that ambitious quotas are operationally feasible (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- PS speaker El Yousfi objected that the resolution was 'almost identical' to a text tabled by former member Pierre-Yves Lux in February 2024 without updating it for regulatory changes that had since taken place, including the April 2024 arrêté; she called the text 'depassé' (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- Les Engagés noted that several measures in the resolution had already been enacted, others had been rejected by the sector, and the remainder would require budgets for the next government to allocate; the group's abstention was 'not a rejection of principle but an exigency of method and efficiency' (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- DéFI cited the SLRB's own managing director as having warned of 'situations explosives' from placing a particularly vulnerable population into housing structures already occupied by people in precarious situations, without guaranteed support resources (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).\n- Vlaams Belang tabled two amendments to the resolution: one limiting social-housing access to holders of a legal residence permit, and one requiring SLRB and SISP to systematically check whether tenants held undeclared property abroad; both were rejected 1 yes / 73 no / 2 abstentions (CRI, 31 Jan 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The near-universal abstention on the base text is not indifference: it reflects a peculiar Brussels alignment where even groups sympathetic to the goal — PS, PTB, Les Engagés — declined to hand opposition Ecolo a legislative win before a new government is formed, particularly on a text they regard as stale and partly superseded.\n- The 33 % quota amendment is the resolution's only genuinely new operative element; its rejection is therefore the real political event — it signals that a cross-partisan majority is unwilling to bind the incoming government to an allocation formula that could generate court challenges from waiting-list families displaced by it.\n- The 'competition between the poor' framing, deployed by Team Fouad Ahidar, PTB and others from very different political traditions, converges to protect the status quo against a supply-side critique: if everyone agrees the root cause is too few units, a quota debate becomes a diversion unless paired with a construction plan, which the resolution does not include.\n- Ecolo's explicit framing of the resolution as 'a signal to our successors' rather than an executable programme reveals the text's true register: it is politics-of-record, staking out a position for coalition negotiations rather than legislating — which is precisely why most groups felt comfortable abstaining rather than voting yes or no.", "proposed_by": "Farida Tahar (Ecolo) and Kalvin Soiresse Njall (Ecolo), with original work by former member Pierre-Yves Lux (Ecolo); rapporteurs Martin Casier and Louis de Clippele", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00011/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"GRN": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "PS": "abstain", "TFA": "abstain", "MR": "abstain", "PTB": "abstain", "OVLD": "abstain", "LE": "abstain", "VB": "abstain", "DEFI": "abstain", "VOOR": "abstain", "NVA": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00013-v13", "date": "2025-02-14", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00013", "point": 13, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Foreign affairs", "headline": "Cancel every Brussels licence for the export or transit of weapons (and dual-use goods) to Israel, and have the Region press the federal government to back the World Court's Gaza genocide case, oppose any forced expulsion of Gazans, and push for sanctions.", "title": "La proposition vise à instaurer un cessez-le-feu permanent à Gaza et à activer les leviers propres de la Région : résilier toutes les licences d'exportation ou de transit d'armes et de biens à double usage lorsqu'un usage militaire ne peut être exclu. Elle demande au gouvernement fédéral de réaffirmer son soutien à la procédure devant la Cour internationale de Justice relative aux allégations de génocide à Gaza, de s'opposer à tout déplacement forcé de la population gazaouie et de plaider pour des sanctions contre les responsables, tout en condamnant les actes terroristes du Hamas et en exigeant la libération des otages.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition vise à instaurer un cessez-le-feu permanent à Gaza et à activer les leviers propres de la Région : résilier toutes les licences d'exportation ou de transit d'armes et de biens à double usage lorsqu'un usage militaire ne peut être exclu. Elle demande au gouvernement fédéral de réaffirmer son soutien à la procédure devant la Cour internationale de Justice relative aux allégations de génocide à Gaza, de s'opposer à tout déplacement forcé de la population gazaouie et de plaider pour des sanctions contre les responsables, tout en condamnant les actes terroristes du Hamas et en exigeant la libération des otages.", "source_brief": "A ceasefire took hold in Gaza on 19 January 2025, but the proposers call it fragile. This resolution turns the Brussels Region's own levers and its weight as Europe's capital toward the conflict: cancel every regional licence to export or move weapons and dual-use technology where military use can't be ruled out, and push the federal government to reaffirm Belgium's support for the genocide case at the International Court of Justice, oppose Trump's announced plan to expel Gaza's population, and plead for sanctions against those responsible for the bloodshed. The text was reworked in session with a long string of amendments.\n\n- **The Region's direct lever** — end the export or transit of arms and of dual-use goods to Israel when it can't be certain they won't be used militarily; this is the part Brussels can act on itself.\n- **What it asks the federal level** — reaffirm support for the ICJ genocide proceedings, oppose forced displacement of Gazans, summon the US and Israeli ambassadors, and plead in Belgium and the EU for sanctions against the political and military leaders responsible.\n- **It cuts both ways** — the same text condemns Hamas's 7 October attacks as terrorist acts and demands the release of the remaining hostages, alongside its condemnation of Israel's conduct.\n- **The rub:** is a regional parliament right to wield its arms-licensing power and its diplomatic voice to take a hard line on Gaza — or does a Brussels resolution on a federal foreign-policy matter, leaning heavily on one side of a contested war, import the conflict into local politics without changing facts on the ground?", "deep_facts": "- The base text (the committee's text, then amended in plenary) seeks an immediate/permanent ceasefire in Gaza and measures against Israel; the proposers frame the Region as able to act through its own powers as 'capital of Europe' (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; doc A-16).\n- Direct regional lever (amendment n° 3): in response to a UN General Assembly resolution, cancel all licences for the export or transit of weapons and their technologies — whether for military or civilian use — and of dual-use goods and technologies where it cannot be established with certainty that they cannot be used for military purposes (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; am. n° 3, A-16).\n- Operative asks to the Brussels Government and (via it) the federal government include: ask the federal government to reaffirm support for the proceedings before the International Court of Justice on the allegations of genocide in Gaza; use all the Region's own powers to prevent further escalation; invite the federal government to work within the EU for peace and a negotiated two-State solution; mobilise additional humanitarian resources; plead within Belgium and the EU for sanctions against the political and military leaders responsible for the bloodshed in Gaza and Lebanon; insist on the release of the remaining Israeli hostages; maintain sanctions against Hezbollah and Hamas (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-16).\n- Amendments tabled in plenary added: authorising the dispatch of independent investigators to Gaza to establish whether the acts constitute genocide under Article II of the Genocide Convention (am. n° 6); recognising, per the ICJ order of 26 January 2024, the plausible risk of genocide in Gaza (am. n° 7); recognising and supporting that ICJ order (am. n° 8); supporting the actions of the ICJ and the International Criminal Court and calling on the federal State to execute their decisions (am. n° 9); categorically opposing US President Donald Trump's announced plan to forcibly expel the Palestinian population from Gaza, taking all legal and diplomatic measures and envisaging sanctions (am. n° 10); summoning the US ambassador over Trump's plan and the Israeli ambassador for violating the ceasefire (am. n° 11) (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-16).\n- The text also condemns Hamas's violence against civilians as terrorist acts and flagrant violations of international law, and condemns the disproportionate use of force by Israel in Gaza as possibly amounting to war crimes (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-16).\n- Cited figures (stated, attributed to UN/speakers): more than 47,000 dead in the Gaza Strip including nearly 15,000 children, and close to 4,000 dead in Lebanon (UN figures cited); 1.9 million people in Gaza displaced repeatedly and 1.3 million in Lebanon and Israel; 95% of schools destroyed; UN had at that stage only 3.6% of the estimated funding needed for the first 42-day ceasefire phase; in the West Bank since 7 October 2023, 806 Palestinians killed and 7,138 injured (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-16).\n- Legal/diplomatic references (not enacted by this text): ICC arrest warrants of 21 November 2024 against Israeli PM Benjamin Netanyahu and former defence minister Yoav Gallant for war crimes and crimes against humanity in Gaza between 8 October 2023 and 20 May 2024; the Israel–Hamas ceasefire concluded 15 January 2025 (three phases) and entered into force 19 January 2025; the Israel–Hezbollah ceasefire of 27 November 2024; a US presidential decree of 4 February 2025 announcing US withdrawal from the UN Human Rights Council (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-16).\n- The resolution decides to transmit the text to the European Commission, to the EU High Representative for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy Kaja Kallas, and to the Belgian federal government (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-16).\n- Process note: the text originated from competing PTB and Ecolo proposals; a global amendment was tabled in commission along a PTB-Ecolo-PS axis; the version brought to the plenary was further reshaped by amendments tabled in session, including new points responding to Trump's 4 February statements (CRI, 14 Feb 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- **The one hard lever:** almost every ask is addressed upward (federal government, EU, international courts) and is therefore symbolic — except the arms- and dual-use-licensing cancellation, which the Region can in principle execute itself. That single point is where the resolution stops being a position statement and becomes an act with material reach, even if the volume of such licences is contested.\n- **What it enables:** by recognising the ICJ's 'plausible risk of genocide' and authorising independent investigators, the text tries to convert a contested characterisation ('genocide') into an administratively endorsed premise that the Region then asks the federal level to act on — agenda-setting through naming.\n- **The wedge:** competence and even-handedness. Opponents read a regional parliament legislating foreign policy it cannot execute, on a text they see as marked by one party's framing, as 'importing the conflict'; supporters read Brussels' status as Europe's capital as precisely the reason its voice carries. The in-session amendment blitz (responding to Trump's expulsion plan days earlier) is also a procedural flashpoint, criticised as reshaping the text on the floor.\n- **Balancing moves:** the condemnations of Hamas as terrorist and the demand to free hostages function partly to broaden the coalition and pre-empt the 'one-sided' charge — the genuine values split is less about condemning violence than about whether to attach Israel-specific licensing measures and sanctions language.", "proposed_by": "PTB, PS, Ecolo, Groen, Vooruit.brussels, Team Fouad Ahidar (El Mokadem, Ikazban, Tahar, Groothedde, Busselen, Mouani, De Smedt, El Yousfi, Parga Vega, Khattabi — replacing Emmery)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00013/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "PTB": "for", "GRN": "for", "TFA": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "MR": "abstain", "OVLD": "abstain", "LE": "abstain", "CDV": "abstain", "VB": "abstain", "DEFI": "abstain", "NVA": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00013-v14", "date": "2025-02-14", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00013", "point": 14, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Foreign affairs", "headline": "Call for suspending every military and commercial agreement with Rwanda while it backs the M23 and occupies Congolese territory, demand the immediate withdrawal of Rwandan forces from eastern Congo, and push for asset freezes and EU travel bans on those responsible.", "title": "La proposition concerne la situation dans l'est de la République démocratique du Congo. Elle appelle à un engagement diplomatique et financier des partenaires internationaux pour exiger la suspension de tout accord de coopération militaire ou commercial avec le Rwanda tant qu'il soutient le M23 et occupe le territoire congolais, ainsi que le retrait immédiat des forces rwandaises de la RDC. Elle soutient la plainte du Congo contre l'exploitation illégale de ses ressources naturelles, demande le renforcement des sanctions contre les responsables, y compris les dirigeants rwandais (gel des avoirs et interdiction de circuler dans l'Union européenne), et le déploiement d'une aide humanitaire d'urgence.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition concerne la situation dans l'est de la République démocratique du Congo. Elle appelle à un engagement diplomatique et financier des partenaires internationaux pour exiger la suspension de tout accord de coopération militaire ou commercial avec le Rwanda tant qu'il soutient le M23 et occupe le territoire congolais, ainsi que le retrait immédiat des forces rwandaises de la RDC. Elle soutient la plainte du Congo contre l'exploitation illégale de ses ressources naturelles, demande le renforcement des sanctions contre les responsables, y compris les dirigeants rwandais (gel des avoirs et interdiction de circuler dans l'Union européenne), et le déploiement d'une aide humanitaire d'urgence.", "source_brief": "War has gripped eastern Congo for three decades, and in early 2025 the city of Goma fell to the M23 rebels, backed by Rwandan forces. This resolution turns Brussels' diplomatic voice toward the crisis: press international partners to suspend every military or commercial agreement with Rwanda for as long as it supports the M23 and occupies Congolese territory, demand the immediate withdrawal of Rwandan forces, and tighten sanctions — asset freezes abroad and an EU travel ban — on those responsible. It frames the war as driven by a scramble for Congo's minerals and points to a 'double standard' compared with Western sanctions on Russia.\n\n- **The headline demand** — suspend military and commercial cooperation with Rwanda while it backs the M23, and demand Rwandan troops leave Congo.\n- **The minerals angle** — back Congo's complaint over the illegal exploitation of its natural resources and the trade in 'blood minerals', the trade the text says fuels the violence.\n- **The harder edge** — reinforced sanctions on those backing the M23, including Rwandan leaders: freezing their foreign assets and barring free movement within the EU.\n- **The rub:** should a regional parliament push to cut economic and military ties with a foreign state and name its leaders for sanctions — a clear stand against an occupation and a resource war — or does a Brussels resolution reach beyond its powers into federal and EU foreign policy, with the real decisions resting elsewhere?", "deep_facts": "- The resolution concerns the situation in the east of the Democratic Republic of Congo; it was cosigned by ten political groups and examined in commission with its amendments (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; doc A-76).\n- Stated objects: international solidarity to condemn the violation of Congo's territorial integrity; to demand the withdrawal of the M23 and Rwandan troops from eastern Congo; to condemn the massacres of civilians, rape used as a weapon of war and exactions of all kinds; and to provide humanitarian aid (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-76).\n- Operative asks set out by the proposers: a diplomatic and financial engagement of international partners to suspend any military or commercial cooperation agreement with Rwanda, and to demand the immediate withdrawal of Rwandan forces from the DRC; support for Congo's complaint against the illegal exploitation of its natural resources, notably its minerals; reinforcing European coordination to channel humanitarian aid, notably via the Congolese diaspora; immediate deployment of humanitarian assistance for displaced populations; reinforced sanctions against those responsible for the military support to the M23, including Rwandan leaders — freezing their foreign assets and barring free movement within the EU (save for strictly peace-related missions); facilitating fundraising and material collection by the diaspora and Brussels associations (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-76).\n- Amendments (two, by Mandaila): support the establishment of an international criminal tribunal; study the possibility of asking for the transformation of the UN observation mission (MONUSCO) into an intervention mission; and exhume (resurface) the report of the UN Mapping project (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-76).\n- Cited human-toll figures (stated, attributed to speakers/UN): at least 10 million lives lost over the conflict's course (a separate figure of more than 6 million dead over three decades was also cited), with millions displaced; more than 400,000 people displaced since the start of 2025; the fall of Goma to the M23 (backed by Rwanda) cited as having caused 3,000 victims, with saturated morgues, overwhelmed hospitals, generalised pillaging including of schools, cut water supply, and bodies recovered from Lake Kivu (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-76).\n- Cited minerals/financial figures (stated): per a UN group of experts, at least 150 tonnes of coltan illegally exported since April 2024 from the Rubaya mines (cited as among the largest tantalum mines in the world); Rwanda — without a single coltan mine — described as the world's leading exporter of the mineral; the European Commission cited as having signed a memorandum of understanding with Rwanda on raw-material supply, and the EU as having paid €20 million to Rwanda in support of its military operations (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-76).\n- Context references (not enacted by this text): the strategic airport of Bukavu reported, hours before the session, as having fallen to the M23 and Rwandan troops; regional ceasefire appeals at a summit in Tanzania; the Luanda and Nairobi peace processes and social pacts initiated by the Catholic and Protestant churches of Congo; a European Parliament resolution adopted the previous day on eastern DRC, aimed at suspending the EU–Rwanda partnership on raw materials and calling to halt arms transfers and freeze military assistance to Rwandan forces (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-76).\n- The proposers stress respect for the competences of the different levels of power and the international legal framework, framing Brussels' role through its status as 'capital of Europe' (CRI, 14 Feb 2025; A-76).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- **Breadth of sponsorship is the headline fact:** ten groups across the spectrum (from Les Engagés/MR/N-VA to PTB/Ecolo) cosign — signalling a deliberate consensus-symbolism play, partly anchored in Brussels' large Congolese diaspora. The cross-bench buy-in lowers the cost of voting yes and makes abstention, not opposition, the dissenting move.\n- **What it enables:** like most regional foreign-policy texts, its asks are addressed upward (federal government, EU, UN) and are not self-executing — its force is agenda-setting and pressure. The most concrete-sounding levers (suspend agreements with Rwanda, freeze assets, EU travel bans, transform MONUSCO) all require federal or EU action the Region cannot itself take.\n- **The framing wedge:** the 'two weights, two measures' argument (sanction Russia but not Rwanda) and the 'blood minerals' line tie the war explicitly to European complicity and supply chains — a framing that pushes the text from humanitarian appeal toward an indictment of EU and corporate mineral policy. That framing is where a values split could surface even amid broad agreement on condemning the violence.\n- **Timing:** the fall of Bukavu hours before the vote and the European Parliament's resolution the day before gave the text momentum and an external anchor, making a 'Brussels is on the right side of history' framing easy to assert.", "proposed_by": "Les Engagés, MR, Vooruit, Ecolo, PTB, N-VA, cd&v and others (Kazadi, Willocx, Luhahi, Soiresse Njall, Agic, Mandaila, Parga Vega, Busselen, Mouani, Dalle) — ten groups cosigning", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00013/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "TFA": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for", "OVLD": "abstain", "VB": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00016-v1", "date": "2025-03-21", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00016", "point": 1, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Amendment (to a finance ordinance)", "topic": "Political pay", "headline": "Cut deputies' pay by reducing the parliament's operating subsidy by 5% for April 2025, as a signal of accountability after nine months without a Brussels government.", "title": "L'amendement n° 1, déposé par Mme De Smedt et M. Busselen (PTB/PVDA), réduit d'environ 5 % la subvention de fonctionnement inscrite à l'allocation de base 34.100.10 (mission 34, programme 100 'Financer le Parlement de la RBC') pour les crédits provisoires d'avril 2025, avec les ajustements correspondants à l'article 6 et à l'annexe 1. Le débat s'inscrit dans le contexte d'un blocage institutionnel de plus de neuf mois sans gouvernement de plein exercice et d'un déficit structurel régional dépassant 1,6 milliard d'euros. L'amendement a été rejeté par 32 voix pour, 38 contre et 12 abstentions.", "raw_outcome": "L'amendement n° 1, déposé par Mme De Smedt et M. Busselen (PTB/PVDA), réduit d'environ 5 % la subvention de fonctionnement inscrite à l'allocation de base 34.100.10 (mission 34, programme 100 'Financer le Parlement de la RBC') pour les crédits provisoires d'avril 2025, avec les ajustements correspondants à l'article 6 et à l'annexe 1. Le débat s'inscrit dans le contexte d'un blocage institutionnel de plus de neuf mois sans gouvernement de plein exercice et d'un déficit structurel régional dépassant 1,6 milliard d'euros. L'amendement a été rejeté par 32 voix pour, 38 contre et 12 abstentions.", "source_brief": "Brussels has been without a fully empowered government since the June 2024 elections — for more than nine months at the time of this vote. The Region is operating on provisional credits while running a structural deficit of over 1.6 billion euros, and parliament has been urged from multiple sides to show it grasps the gravity. This amendment targets the operating subsidy that finances the parliament itself, cutting it for April 2025 in a way that would constrain deputies' take-home pay.\n\n- **What the amendment does:** It lowers the parliament's budget allocation (allocation de base 34.100.10, mission 34 Chancellerie) from 17,250 to 16,603.50 thousand euros for April 2025 — approximately a 5% reduction — and adjusts the totals in Article 6 and Annex 1 accordingly.\n- **The context:** Multiple speakers noted that Brussels deputies earn roughly 5% more than their counterparts at the Federal Parliament and other Belgian assemblies. Supporters say reducing the subsidy would force the parliament's Bureau to translate that into lower remuneration, saving an estimated 600,000 euros a year if applied year-round.\n- **A rival proposal:** Les Engagés announced simultaneously that they had tabled a separate text — scheduled for the parliamentary Bureau on 2 April — proposing a 40% pay cut for deputies and ministers for as long as the government-formation deadlock lasts; they abstained on this amendment, calling it technically flawed and insufficient.\n- **The rub:** Supporters argue it is symbolic but concrete accountability: elected officials should not earn more than federal colleagues while the Region drifts without a government. Opponents and abstainers counter that cutting a dotation does not automatically reduce salaries — the Bureau must still decide — and that the reduction covers only three months, making the headline saving figure approximate and the symbolic gesture hollow.", "deep_facts": "- Amendment nr. 1 was tabled by Mme Françoise De Smedt and M. Jan Busselen (PTB/PVDA) to Article 6 (tableaux budgétaires) of A-86, the projet d'ordonnance de finances ouvrant des crédits provisoires pour les mois d'avril, mai et juin 2025 sur le budget de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- The amendment cuts allocation de base 34.100.10 (subventions de fonctionnement non neutralisées en SEC au sein de la RBC, mission 34 Chancellerie, programme 100 'Financer le Parlement de la RBC') from 17,250 to 16,603.50 thousand euros in both liquidation and engagement credits; the April 2025 provisional-credits column drops correspondingly from 34,500 to 33,853.50 thousand euros (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- In plenary debate, Stijn Bex (Groen) stated that Brussels deputies earn roughly 5% more than their counterparts at the Kamer, the Flemish Parliament and other Belgian assemblies, and argued that the Bureau could simply decide to align remuneration — saving an estimated 600,000 euros per year (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- PVDA/PTB tabled the amendment citing the Region's severe fiscal situation: a structural deficit exceeding 1.6 billion euros, provisional budgets instead of a proper annual budget, and a hiring freeze already in place; they invited all groups to support the amendment as a signal of collective accountability (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- DéFI (Jonathan de Patoul) announced his group would vote in favour, saying it was a just and responsible measure that DéFI had defended before (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- Les Engagés (Alain Deneef) abstained, arguing that reducing the dotation does not automatically reduce salaries — a Bureau decision is still required — that the amendment covers only the first quarter rather than the period under discussion, and that the stated 650,000-euro saving figure was approximate and poorly justified; they pointed to their own separate text, scheduled for the Bureau on 2 April 2025, which would immediately reduce deputies' and ministers' salaries by 40% for the duration of the government-formation deadlock (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- N-VA (Gilles Verstraeten) stated before the amendment was formally tabled that he did not believe parliamentary salaries could be reduced by a budget amendment — it was a matter for the Bureau élargi — and that any salary comparison should be comprehensive and Belgium-wide before adjustments are made (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- The caretaker government had been in office since the June 2024 elections; at the time of the vote, Les Engagés characterised Brussels as having been 'sans gouvernement depuis plus de neuf mois', making it a prolonged constitutional and budgetary impasse (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- A separate issue was flagged in the same debate: Standard & Poor's was expected to deliver a credit-rating verdict on Brussels in June 2025, and the caretaker Finance Minister Gatz warned that the Region's fiscal trajectory was under close scrutiny (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The mechanism chosen — cutting the parliamentary dotation rather than mandating a direct pay reduction — reveals the legal constraint: parliament cannot legislate its own members' salaries by ordinary budget amendment; only the Bureau can. So the amendment is, in effect, a pressure move rather than a direct executive act, which is exactly what Les Engagés attacked as amateurish while proposing the same idea through the only vehicle (the Bureau) that could actually deliver it.\n- The split between PTB's 5% cut and Les Engagés' 40% cut reflects two different political calculations: PTB's figure aligns Brussels deputies with other Belgian assemblies (framed as fairness, not punishment), while 40% is explicitly framed as coercive — a penalty designed to force parties to the government-formation table.\n- DéFI and Groen supporting the amendment, and Les Engagés abstaining rather than voting against, signals that no group wanted to be seen defending the status quo salary premium during a fiscal emergency — the disagreement is entirely about the right instrument and the right cut, not about the principle.\n- The coexistence of a 1.6-billion-euro structural deficit and a 5% pay premium for deputies is a politically combustible combination; whatever the legal route, the trajectory of the debate points toward a Bureau decision aligning Brussels salaries with the federal level, making the amendment's defeat less a rejection of the idea than a deferral.", "proposed_by": "Françoise De Smedt (PTB) and Jan Busselen (PVDA/PTB)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "rejected", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00016/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"TFA": "for", "PTB": "for", "GRN": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "PS": "against", "MR": "against", "OVLD": "against", "NVA": "against", "LE": "abstain", "CDV": "abstain", "VOOR": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00016-v5", "date": "2025-03-21", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00016", "point": 5, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance (proposition d'ordonnance)", "topic": "Low Emission Zone", "headline": "Restore legal certainty for Brussels's LEZ postponement by replacing the defective October 2024 wording, confirming that Euro 5 diesel and Euro 2 vehicles may continue to access the zone until 31 December 2026.", "title": "La proposition d'ordonnance (A-79) remplace l'article 3.2.16 §6 du Cobrace par une formulation conforme aux exigences du Conseil d'État (avis 77.511/1), avec effet rétroactif au 1er janvier 2025. Elle consolide juridiquement le report au 1er janvier 2027 de la phase LEZ excluant les véhicules diesel Euro 5 et Euro 2, décidé par l'ordonnance du 17 octobre 2024, en ajoutant explicitement que les véhicules déjà autorisés à circuler conservent ce droit et en détaillant les mesures compensatoires exigées par le principe de standstill constitutionnel. Les échéances LEZ de 2028, 2030 et 2036 demeurent inchangées.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition d'ordonnance (A-79) remplace l'article 3.2.16 §6 du Cobrace par une formulation conforme aux exigences du Conseil d'État (avis 77.511/1), avec effet rétroactif au 1er janvier 2025. Elle consolide juridiquement le report au 1er janvier 2027 de la phase LEZ excluant les véhicules diesel Euro 5 et Euro 2, décidé par l'ordonnance du 17 octobre 2024, en ajoutant explicitement que les véhicules déjà autorisés à circuler conservent ce droit et en détaillant les mesures compensatoires exigées par le principe de standstill constitutionnel. Les échéances LEZ de 2028, 2030 et 2036 demeurent inchangées.", "source_brief": "In October 2024 the Brussels Parliament passed an ordinance (A-10) postponing the next phase of the Low Emission Zone — which would have banned Euro 5 diesel and Euro 2 vehicles from 2025 — by two years to 1 January 2027. The Conseil d'État subsequently identified a legal defect in the wording: the text did not explicitly state that vehicles already authorised to circulate would continue to be so, and it did not include the compensating measures required under the constitutional standstill obligation on environmental protection. This proposition d'ordonnance (A-79) is a corrective rewrite of article 3.2.16 §6 of the Brussels Air, Climate and Energy Code, replacing the October 2024 wording with a version the Conseil d'État considers compliant, with retroactive effect from 1 January 2025.\n\n- **What it changes:** The text restores legal certainty for more than 30,000 Brussels-registered vehicles and 8,000 light utility vehicles — plus over 700,000 vehicles nationally — that would have been banned under the original 2025 deadline.\n- **The compensating measures:** To satisfy the standstill condition, the text is accompanied by commitments including mobile enforcement teams to check foreign vehicles, anti-particle-filter-fraud detection, remote emissions sensing, and expansion of electric-vehicle charging infrastructure.\n- **Why again:** A MR amendment adopted in committee adds an explicit clause confirming that vehicles already permitted to enter the LEZ retain that right — the specific fix the Conseil d'État requested to avoid interpretation disputes.\n- **The rub:** Supporters say the postponement is a socially just transition measure in a difficult economic climate, that Brussels already meets all European air-quality norms, and that buildings (responsible for 60-75% of Brussels air pollution) are the bigger lever. Opponents argue that every LEZ delay chips away at the credibility of the phasing schedule, penalises the tens of thousands who already sold compliant vehicles in anticipation of the rule, and risks eroding the flexibility needed for the 2028, 2030 and 2036 milestones.", "deep_facts": "- The proposition d'ordonnance (dossier A-79/1, 2, 3 et 4 – 2024/2025) amends the ordonnance du 2 mai 2013 portant le Code bruxellois de l'Air, du Climat et de la Maîtrise de l'Énergie (Cobrace) by replacing article 3.2.16 §6 with a corrected formulation, with retroactive effect from 1 January 2025 (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- The first postponement ordinance (A-10, adopted 17 October 2024) had delayed the LEZ phase banning Euro 5 diesel and Euro 2 vehicles from 1 January 2025 to 1 January 2027; A-79 corrects its wording without reopening the date (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- The Conseil d'État (avis numéro 77.511/1, on a five-day referral from parliament and a 30-day referral from the Environment Minister) found that the wording of A-10 was not fully compliant with the constitutional standstill obligation under Article 23 of the Constitution and recommended two additions: explicit confirmation that already-authorised vehicles remain authorised, and a description of compensating measures to offset any air-quality regression (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- A MR amendment adopted in committee adds the explicit clause that vehicles already authorised to circulate within the LEZ will continue to be so — the specific textual fix requested by the Conseil d'État to prevent interpretation disputes (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- Figures cited in debate: without the postponement, the 2025 deadline would have banned more than 30,000 Brussels-registered vehicles and more than 8,000 Brussels-registered light utility vehicles, as well as more than 700,000 vehicles nationally seeking to enter the zone (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- Proposers cited Brussels Environnement data confirming that the Region has met all European air-quality norms since 2022 — including PM10, PM2.5 and nitrogen oxides — and is already below its 2030 targets for several pollutants; Environment Minister Maron confirmed in the 12 March 2025 committee meeting that a two-year delay would not prevent the Region from meeting its climate objectives (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- Compensating measures accompanying the postponement include: enhanced LEZ-enforcement conditions, mobile teams to control foreign vehicles, detection of particle-filter fraud, real-emissions evaluation via remote sensing, and expansion of public electric-vehicle charging infrastructure (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- Ikazban (PS) emphasised that buildings account for 60 to 75% of Brussels air pollution while road transport represents only 25%, arguing the LEZ cannot be the sole lever for air-quality improvement; the postponement is explicitly framed as a social-accompaniment measure, not an abandonment of the broader LEZ schedule (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- PTB (Oliver Rittweger de Moor) voted against both A-10 in October 2024 and A-79 in March 2025, arguing that the LEZ cannot be justified without genuine public-transport alternatives; they noted STIB fares had risen 10% over six months and service had been cut on dozens of bus lines (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- The 2028, 2030 and 2036 LEZ access restrictions and the Region's carbon-neutrality and greenhouse-gas-reduction targets remain unchanged; proposers repeated this commitment explicitly to reassure environmental groups and the Environment Minister (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).\n- The text passed by 68 votes in favour, 13 against and 1 abstention, out of 82 members present (CRI, 21 Mar 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- A-79 is structurally a clean-up bill: the political decision to postpone was taken in October 2024; this text is about making that decision legally watertight. That dynamic matters — a party that opposed the postponement on principle when A-10 was voted could still accept the technical fix without reversing course, producing an unusually broad majority.\n- The Conseil d'État's requirement to describe compensating measures is the substantive upgrade in A-79 over A-10: it forces the legislature to put on record what it intends to do to protect air quality during the extra two years, creating a traceable commitment that environmental groups can hold up against the 2028 milestone debate.\n- The 60-75% buildings / 25% transport framing, repeated by multiple proposers, is doing political work beyond the immediate text: it pre-positions the next fiscal battle over building-renovation budgets as the primary air-quality lever, potentially softening future resistance to further LEZ flexibility if building-renovation funding is secured.\n- The Flemish LEZ postponement — mentioned as a source of citizen confusion if Brussels had held to its original schedule — shows how cross-regional policy divergence is now a political argument for regional alignment downward, a precedent that could resurface in future LEZ debates at the 2028 deadline.", "proposed_by": "Jamal Ikazban (PS), Anne-Charlotte d'Ursel (MR), Mounir Laarissi, Ibrahim Dönmez, Sofia Bennani, Imane Belguenani, Clémentine Barzin", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00016/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "VB": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for", "DEFI": "against", "GRN": "against", "Ecolo": "against", "CDV": "against"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00018-v1", "date": "2025-04-04", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00018", "point": 1, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Horeca terraces", "headline": "Make permanent the Covid-era rules letting Brussels horeca operators install year-round public-space terraces without a planning permit, subject to the existing guidelines.", "title": "La proposition de résolution invite le gouvernement à modifier de manière permanente l'arrêté relatif aux dispenses de permis d'urbanisme, afin d'autoriser l'installation de terrasses horeca dans l'espace public toute l'année, dans le respect des lignes directrices existantes. Ces mesures, introduites en 2021 dans le contexte de la pandémie de Covid-19, ont été prorogées à plusieurs reprises à titre provisoire, créant une insécurité juridique préjudiciable aux investissements du secteur. Le texte maintient les obligations d'autorisation communale et d'occupation de voirie, ainsi que les prérogatives des communes en matière de conditions et de retrait d'autorisation.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition de résolution invite le gouvernement à modifier de manière permanente l'arrêté relatif aux dispenses de permis d'urbanisme, afin d'autoriser l'installation de terrasses horeca dans l'espace public toute l'année, dans le respect des lignes directrices existantes. Ces mesures, introduites en 2021 dans le contexte de la pandémie de Covid-19, ont été prorogées à plusieurs reprises à titre provisoire, créant une insécurité juridique préjudiciable aux investissements du secteur. Le texte maintient les obligations d'autorisation communale et d'occupation de voirie, ainsi que les prérogatives des communes en matière de conditions et de retrait d'autorisation.", "source_brief": "During the Covid lockdowns Brussels permitted café and restaurant owners to set up terraces in public space without a planning permit, as a lifeline for a sector forbidden from trading indoors. Those rules proved popular and were extended repeatedly by a caretaker government — most recently to 31 December 2025. This resolution urges the incoming government to write the exemption into the standing permit-dispensation decree permanently, so operators have the legal certainty to invest properly in their terrace infrastructure.\n\n- **Who it touches:** independent bar, café and restaurant owners across Brussels, particularly those on narrow streets or with small frontages who relied on the extra outdoor space to survive the pandemic and now fear a return to heavy administrative procedures.\n- **The legal mechanism:** operators would still need a communal horeca licence and a road-occupation authorisation; municipalities retain the power to impose conditions (including seasonal limits) and to withdraw authorisation. Only the planning-permit requirement for the structure itself would go.\n- **The nuance:** supporters note the existing circular included other benefits — wider footpaths, greening, cycle infrastructure — and some members call for those gains to be carried into the permanent framework, so the reform is not just about terraces.\n- **The rub:** whether entrenching year-round terrace rights strikes the right balance between supporting a fragile sector and protecting pedestrian access, wheelchair users, and genuinely shared public space.", "deep_facts": "- The Brussels government issued guidelines in 2021 authorising horeca operators to install public-space terraces year-round without a planning permit (permis d'urbanisme), as a response to the Covid-19 economic crisis (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- The measures were originally planned for two years; they were subsequently extended to 30 September 2024, then again — by the caretaker government — to 31 December 2025, creating a succession of temporary extensions that the sector found unsatisfactory for long-term investment planning (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- The resolution asks the government to amend the existing arrête on planning-permit dispensations to incorporate a permanent, year-round terrace authorisation, in conformity with the current guidelines, removing the need for further case-by-case extensions (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- Operators retaining obligations include: a communal horeca exploitation authorisation and a communal or regional road-occupation authorisation; municipalities may still impose seasonality conditions, financial guarantees, or other requirements, and may withdraw authorisation for non-compliance (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- Proponents argue that the temporary nature of successive extensions has deterred operators from investing in quality permanent terrace infrastructure; legal certainty through a permanent rule would unlock those investments (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- A speaker from Groen flagged that the 2021 circular contained wider benefits — simplified greening, widened footpaths and cycle paths, improved pedestrian crossings — that are not automatically carried over and should be integrated into the permanent urban-planning framework, specifically in the forthcoming Good Living regional regulation (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- Accessibility concerns were raised: expanding terraces in public space has at times made wheelchair navigation more difficult; the permanent framework must preserve an unobstructed pedestrian passage, and communes are urged to take their enforcement responsibilities seriously (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- The resolution was adopted unanimously in the committee on Territorial Development before being brought to plenary; a PS-tabled amendment by Pascal Smet contributed to that consensus (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- Speakers across groups cited the broader economic fragility of Brussels — the president of the French-language commercial court had just told the economics committee that business failures were accelerating across all sectors — as context for why legislative certainty for horeca mattered urgently (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The unanimity in committee, and near-unanimity in plenary, signals that this is one of the rare files where the left-right divide disappears: supporting small independent traders and removing red tape are framing devices that work across the ideological spectrum simultaneously.\n- The caretaker government's repeated one-year extensions are the practical driver: each extension creates a planning horizon too short for a terrace investment to pay back, so the sector kept lobbying. Making the rule permanent is less a policy choice than a bureaucratic fix everyone can agree on.\n- The Groen amendment folding in greening and pedestrian access is the only real tension point — it reframes the text from a pure deregulation measure into a modest public-space-quality requirement, which is why it found majority support but is framed as a long-term ask rather than a blocking condition.\n- The timing — voted the same morning as the far more divisive rent ordinance — underscores how different the political temperature can be on two housing-adjacent files decided on the same day.", "proposed_by": "Ludivine de Magnanville (MR), co-signed by Pascal Smet (Vooruit.brussels), Isabelle Emmery (PS), Lotte Stoops (Groen) and others across several groups", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00018/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "VOOR": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "NVA": "for", "PTB": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00018-v4", "date": "2025-04-04", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00018", "point": 4, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance (proposition d'ordonnance)", "topic": "Rent abuse remedy", "headline": "Activate, from 1 May 2025, the dormant 2021 articles empowering Brussels tenants to bring abusive-rent cases before a justice of the peace, backed by the advisory opinion of the joint rental commission.", "title": "La proposition d'ordonnance fixe au 1er mai 2025 l'entrée en vigueur des articles 8 à 13 de l'ordonnance du 28 octobre 2021, qui organisent une action en révision de loyer abusif devant le juge de paix, sur la base d'un avis de la Commission paritaire locative. Ces articles étaient restés sans date d'entrée en vigueur depuis 2021 faute d'accord politique. Le texte a été inscrit à l'ordre du jour en urgence le matin même, à la suite d'un vote procédural (45-33-4) ; un amendement DéFI–Open Vld proposant de reporter l'entrée en vigueur au 1er janvier 2027 a été rejeté ; la séance a été suspendue quarante minutes après une tentative de rupture du quorum, avant que le vote final ne soit acquis.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition d'ordonnance fixe au 1er mai 2025 l'entrée en vigueur des articles 8 à 13 de l'ordonnance du 28 octobre 2021, qui organisent une action en révision de loyer abusif devant le juge de paix, sur la base d'un avis de la Commission paritaire locative. Ces articles étaient restés sans date d'entrée en vigueur depuis 2021 faute d'accord politique. Le texte a été inscrit à l'ordre du jour en urgence le matin même, à la suite d'un vote procédural (45-33-4) ; un amendement DéFI–Open Vld proposant de reporter l'entrée en vigueur au 1er janvier 2027 a été rejeté ; la séance a été suspendue quarante minutes après une tentative de rupture du quorum, avant que le vote final ne soit acquis.", "source_brief": "In October 2021 the Brussels Parliament passed an ordinance creating a joint rental commission (Commission paritaire locative) to tackle abusive rents, but left articles 8–13 — the ones giving tenants a right of action before a justice of the peace — without an entry-into-force date. This ordinance sets that date as 1 May 2025. The session was one of the most dramatic in recent Brussels parliamentary history: an agenda-change vote that very morning (45-33-4) forced the text onto the day's schedule; opposition parties argued the supporting rent reference grid is too old and unrepresentative; a DéFI/Open Vld amendment to delay activation to 1 January 2027 was put to a vote; and after its defeat, MR, N-VA and Vlaams Belang MPs walked out in a quorum-breaking manoeuvre that suspended the sitting for 40 minutes.\n\n- **What articles 8–13 do:** a tenant whose rent exceeds the indicative reference rent by more than 20% AND who faces qualifying quality defects in the property can petition the Commission paritaire locative for a conciliation opinion, then use that opinion before a justice of the peace to seek a rent reduction.\n- **The grid dispute:** opponents argued the reference rent grid is built on data collected in 2017–2020, making it unrepresentative of 2025 market conditions; proponents cited updated indexation and a researcher-confirmed sample of 3,000-plus observations as sufficient.\n- **The walkout:** after the 2027-delay amendment was rejected 37-44, MR leader Barzin called a suspension on quorum grounds and MR, N-VA and Vlaams Belang deputies left the chamber; after a 40-minute suspension, quorum was restored and the vote proceeded.\n- **The rub:** whether activating a mechanism tied to a contested reference grid protects Brussels tenants — around 60% of the population — from abusive landlords, or whether it risks chilling investment in a market already starved of new supply.", "deep_facts": "- The 2021 ordonnance (28 October 2021) was adopted with only four votes against; articles 8 to 13, which organise the judicial action for rent revision before the justice of the peace, were left without an entry-into-force date pending a political agreement that the previous government never concluded (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- Articles 8–13 define the Commission paritaire locative's role as issuing a binding advisory opinion (avis) that a tenant can use as the basis for a judicial claim; the Commission is parity-based — equal representation of landlords and tenants — and speakers noted that of the three dossiers processed to date, two were rejected as not constituting abusive rents (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- The abusive-rent trigger has two cumulative criteria: the rent must exceed the indicative reference rent by more than 20%, AND quality defects in the property must be established; a high-quality property exceeding the median by 20% is not automatically classified as abusive (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- The indicative rent reference grid (grille des loyers) was originally built on data from 2017, 2018 and 2020; it has since been updated by indexation and a new statistical methodology developed by researcher Hugo Périlleux; Périlleux testified in committee that a sample of 3,000 to 4,000 observations is statistically sufficient for the new approach (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- Key statistics cited in the debate: median Brussels rents rose 143% since 2018 compared to a health-index rise of roughly 20-23%; 10% of Brussels residents with the lowest incomes spend an average of 45% of their income on housing (Baromètre social 2023); some households spend up to 70% (Rassemblement bruxellois pour le droit à l'habitat); the social housing waiting list exceeds 60,000 households; an estimated 7,000 to 10,000 people are homeless (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- MR speaker de Clippele cited estimates from the developer Hugo Périlleux of 70,000 potentially affected properties, and cited 2025 rental-advertisement data showing 95% of listings on Immoweb, Immovlan and Logic-Immo exceeding the reference rent by more than 20%, raising concern about the mechanism's effective scope (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- DéFI and Open Vld filed amendment no. 1 to article 2, replacing '1 mai 2025' with '1er janvier 2027', arguing that an additional 20 months would allow the regional lease-registration system — which had already collected more than 5,000 leases in three months — to produce a reliably representative grid; the amendment was rejected 37 votes for, 44 against (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- After the amendment was defeated, MR group leader Barzin called a suspension on quorum grounds; MR, N-VA and Vlaams Belang deputies then left the chamber; the sitting was suspended from 17h47 to 18h29; quorum was reached after the suspension and the final article-2 vote and whole-text vote each recorded 45 yes, 16 no, with 61 members present (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- The morning sitting (séance n° 17) had begun with PS leader Laaouej requesting an agenda change under article 52 of the standing orders to add the ordonnance to the same day's plenary; that procedural vote passed 45-33-4, with MR, N-VA, Les Engagés and cd&v voting against on grounds of undue haste and insufficient hearings (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).\n- An economics professor (Prof. Sven Damen) testified in committee that international studies on rent-control regimes consistently show four side effects: reduced supply, lower housing quality, reduced household mobility, and a halt in new construction; opposition speakers cited Berlin's 2020 rent cap, struck down by the Federal Constitutional Court in 2021, as a cautionary parallel (CRI, 4 Apr 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The 2027-delay amendment is the real strategic contest: it was not a wrecking amendment but a genuine offer to the swing votes (DéFI, Open Vld, Les Engagés), and the majority's refusal to accept it forced those groups to choose between a mechanism they helped design in 2021 and an entry-into-force they consider premature — most ended up voting no on the amendment but staying for the final vote, which is exactly the 'democratic courtesy' PS leader Laaouej thanked them for.\n- The quorum walkout is the most revealing moment: it signals that MR and allies judged the political cost of being recorded as voting against a tenant-protection measure at roll call to be higher than the reputational cost of a walkout, which they calculated would be framed as a procedural objection rather than an ideological one.\n- The grid-reliability debate is technically unresolved. Both sides cite credentialed experts; the underlying disagreement is whether a statistically adequate sample justifies activating a legally binding mechanism when the underlying rent data predate the pandemic. That question will likely return in any post-implementation litigation.\n- The absence of a full government for ten months is load-bearing context: the majority coalition acted precisely because they feared that a new government including parties hostile to rent regulation might repeal or indefinitely delay articles 8–13, making the parliamentary window a now-or-never moment.", "proposed_by": "Martin Casier (PS), Kalvin Soiresse Njall (Ecolo), Stijn Bex (Groen), Nadia El Yousfi (PS), Farida Tahar (Ecolo), Mohamed Ouriaghli (PS), Ilyas Mouani (Vooruit.brussels), Françoise De Smedt (PTB), Jan Busselen (PVDA), Fouad Ahidar (Team Fouad Ahidar)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00018/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "PTB": "for", "GRN": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "OVLD": "against", "LE": "against", "VB": "against", "DEFI": "against", "MR": "against"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00022-v4", "date": "2025-05-16", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00022", "point": 4, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Gaza aid", "headline": "Call on the Brussels government to press Belgium to ensure food and humanitarian aid reaches Gaza, including via EU-coordinated air drops if land corridors remain blocked.", "title": "La proposition de résolution demande au gouvernement de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale d'intervenir auprès du gouvernement fédéral pour qu'il agisse au niveau de l'Union européenne afin de garantir l'acheminement urgent d'aide alimentaire et humanitaire à Gaza, notamment en inscrivant ce point à l'ordre du jour des ministres européens compétents. Un amendement du MR, rejeté en séance, visait à remplacer la référence aux largages aériens par celle aux couloirs humanitaires terrestres et à élargir la notion d'aide alimentaire à l'aide humanitaire au sens large. Le texte originel a ensuite été adopté lors du vote final.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition de résolution demande au gouvernement de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale d'intervenir auprès du gouvernement fédéral pour qu'il agisse au niveau de l'Union européenne afin de garantir l'acheminement urgent d'aide alimentaire et humanitaire à Gaza, notamment en inscrivant ce point à l'ordre du jour des ministres européens compétents. Un amendement du MR, rejeté en séance, visait à remplacer la référence aux largages aériens par celle aux couloirs humanitaires terrestres et à élargir la notion d'aide alimentaire à l'aide humanitaire au sens large. Le texte originel a ensuite été adopté lors du vote final.", "source_brief": "Since 2 March 2025, no food or humanitarian aid has entered Gaza; the World Food Programme reported on 16 May that the emergency had never been greater, with half a million people — one in five — facing famine. This resolution asks the Brussels government to lobby the federal government to put urgent humanitarian aid to Gaza on the EU Foreign Affairs and Humanitarian Aid Ministers' agenda, and to call on the European Commission to enable aid delivery. An MR amendment sought to replace explicit references to air drops with humanitarian land corridors; supporters of the original text rejected it; the amendment was voted down, and the unamended text was then put to a final vote.\n\n- **What it asks:** the Brussels government to relay a request up the chain — to the federal government, then to the EU — for guaranteed, unblocked access to Gaza for food and humanitarian aid.\n- **The air-drop dispute:** left groups and Vooruit.brussels argued air drops are the last remaining route given Israel's blockade; MR countered that air drops are technically dangerous, require Israeli permission, and that land convoys already waiting at the border are the right mechanism.\n- **The competence objection:** MR and Open Vld repeatedly noted that foreign-policy levers belong to the federal level, not a regional parliament, and that three Brussels resolutions on Gaza in three months created false expectations among citizens.\n- **The rub:** whether a regional parliament's symbolic signal amplifies international pressure and reflects its Palestinian diaspora's concerns, or whether it oversteps competence, polarises Brussels society, and delivers no practical change.", "deep_facts": "- The text is a proposition de résolution (dossier NRS. A-122/1 and 2 — 2024/2025) tabled by Ilyas Mouani (Vooruit.brussels) and examined under urgency; it passed committee unanimously before the plenary session (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 21).\n- Since 2 March 2025 no food or humanitarian aid had entered Gaza; on the day of the debate, Israel's plan to demolish remaining infrastructure in Gaza was entering into force; the World Food Programme declared the emergency had never been so acute (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 21).\n- An IPC report dated 12 May 2025, cited by PS speaker Nadia El Yousfi, stated that the entire population of Gaza faced acute food insecurity; some 500,000 people — one in five — were at risk of famine (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 21).\n- In March 2024 the Belgian B-FAST cell, under then-Defence Minister Ludivine Dedonder (PS), had air-dropped 164 tonnes of humanitarian material over Gaza; speakers noted nothing comparable had occurred in the fifteen months since (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 21).\n- The Brussels Parliament had already adopted a resolution on 14 February 2025 calling for an arms export ban to Israel, a halt to subsidies to complicit companies and for sanctions; several speakers, notably PTB's Patricia Parga Vega, argued the federal parties present had done nothing to implement those measures at federal level (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 21).\n- Fouad Ahidar (Team Fouad Ahidar) stated he had visited Gaza eight times, most recently accompanying two trucks of supplies for which he was asked to pay 300 dollars per truck at the Egyptian border, despite thousands of trucks already waiting; he counted 15,000 children killed since 7 October 2023 (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 21).\n- MR's Ismail Luahabi filed amendment no. 1 to replace references to air drops with 'humanitarian corridors' and to widen 'aide alimentaire' to 'aide humanitaire' covering medicines and clothing; he noted that air drops require Israel's explicit permission, that a single military aircraft carries 15 tonnes requiring 120 daily flights, and that drops had previously killed or injured people (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 21).\n- Ecolo's Zakia Khattabi responded that she found it 'indigne' for MR to express concern about the danger of food drops while never having condemned the dropping of bombs on Gaza (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 21).\n- Gilles Verstraeten (N-VA) supported the MR amendment on practical grounds — preferring land convoys as the primary mechanism — but said N-VA's support for the resolution was not conditional on the amendment's adoption (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 21).\n- The federal Arizona coalition government (including MR, Les Engagés, cd&v, N-VA and Vooruit) had tabled a federal resolution on Gaza the previous day (15 May 2025), prompting some parties to argue the Brussels text was partly superseded (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 21).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The amendment battle over 'air drops vs. land corridors' is the visible debate but the deeper tension is procedural: the MR used a technical objection (dangers of drops, Israeli veto on airspace) to reframe the request in terms more palatable to a government that is simultaneously negotiating the federal resolution — a way to back the text without endorsing its most confrontational framing.\n- The timing — tabling the Brussels resolution the day after Vooruit abstained from the Gaza committee at the federal Parliament — gave PTB and left groups political ammunition to accuse proposers of symbolic gesture-politics while blocking concrete action at the level where it would actually matter.\n- A wide final consensus, if it emerges once the amendment falls, would obscure significant disagreement on the resolution's underlying logic: parties that thought it outside Brussels competence or symbolically insufficient still voted yes rather than split the plenary just before a sensitive second vote on the ICC.\n- For a regional parliament without foreign-policy competence, the vote's main function is reputational signalling toward Brussels's large Palestinian diaspora and toward EU-level actors, rather than any operational effect on aid delivery.", "proposed_by": "Ilyas Mouani (Vooruit.brussels) as lead author; co-signed by Zakia Khattabi (Ecolo), Fouad Ahidar (Team Fouad Ahidar) and others across several groups; rapporteurs Zakia Khattabi (Ecolo) and Ismail Luahabi (MR)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00022/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00022-v5", "date": "2025-05-16", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00022", "point": 5, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "ICC warrants", "headline": "Demand that Belgium honour its obligations under the Rome Statute and execute ICC arrest warrants, including those issued in the context of the conflict in Gaza.", "title": "La proposition de résolution demande à la Belgique de respecter ses engagements internationaux en matière de justice pénale, notamment l'exécution des mandats d'arrêt émis par la Cour pénale internationale. Déposé en urgence à la suite de déclarations du Premier ministre De Wever interprétées comme remettant en cause ces obligations, le texte a été débattu alors que le gouvernement fédéral avait entre-temps clarifiés sa position par la voix des ministres des Affaires étrangères et de la Justice, ainsi que par le dépôt d'une résolution fédérale réaffirmant le soutien de la Belgique à la CPI.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition de résolution demande à la Belgique de respecter ses engagements internationaux en matière de justice pénale, notamment l'exécution des mandats d'arrêt émis par la Cour pénale internationale. Déposé en urgence à la suite de déclarations du Premier ministre De Wever interprétées comme remettant en cause ces obligations, le texte a été débattu alors que le gouvernement fédéral avait entre-temps clarifiés sa position par la voix des ministres des Affaires étrangères et de la Justice, ainsi que par le dépôt d'une résolution fédérale réaffirmant le soutien de la Belgique à la CPI.", "source_brief": "This resolution was triggered by remarks made by Belgian Prime Minister Bart De Wever in April 2025, which were widely understood as signalling reluctance to execute a potential ICC arrest warrant, notably against Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu. The text asks the Brussels Parliament to call on Belgium to respect its international legal commitments, specifically the Rome Statute obligation to arrest and transfer persons subject to ICC warrants. By the time of the final vote, the federal government had issued clarifications — through the Foreign Affairs and Justice ministers and a federal resolution — affirming Belgium's commitment to international law.\n\n- **Why a Brussels vote?** Supporters argued the Prime Minister's remarks created genuine doubt about Belgium's rule-of-law reliability; they saw a regional signal as necessary and timely given pressure on the ICC from the US and Hungary's withdrawal.\n- **The selective-justice charge:** MR argued the text named only Israel-related mandates and omitted warrants against Vladimir Putin, Omar al-Bashir and others, questioning whether it reflected genuine universalism or political targeting.\n- **The competence and timing defence:** Les Engagés, cd&v and MR contended that since the federal government and prime minister had already clarified their position, the resolution had no further object; execution of ICC warrants is a judicial, not executive, matter.\n- **The rub:** whether reaffirming international-law obligations is a legitimate act of regional parliamentary expression or an out-of-competence move that imports geopolitical conflict into Brussels's social fabric.", "deep_facts": "- The text is a proposition de résolution (dossier NOS A-114/1, 2 and 3 — 2024/2025) with PS as first signatory; it was tabled as a matter of urgency following statements by Prime Minister De Wever that were interpreted as signalling possible non-compliance with ICC arrest warrants (CRI, 16 May 2025 — sessions nr. 21 and nr. 22).\n- Prime Minister De Wever had stated publicly that executing a possible ICC warrant against Netanyahu would not be straightforward, citing a scenario in which the Israeli PM might make an emergency landing in Belgium; N-VA's Gilles Verstraeten characterised the remarks as 'excessive intellectual honesty' from De Wever's earlier role as party chairman, inappropriate for a sitting premier (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 22).\n- Federal Foreign Affairs Minister Maxime Prévot and Justice Minister Annelies Verlinden issued clarifications in early April 2025 stating that the remarks had not been discussed in government and that Belgium would continue to support the ICC and execute its decisions; on 9 April, Verlinden confirmed before the Chamber that Belgium, as a Rome Statute party, is legally obliged to execute ICC arrest and transfer requests (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 22).\n- The federal Arizona coalition majority had, as of 12 May 2025, tabled a resolution in the Chamber reaffirming Belgium's support for the ICC and the execution of its decisions including arrest warrants; Les Engagés and N-VA cited this to argue the Brussels resolution was without object (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 22).\n- PS's Jamal Ikazban added new urgency to the debate by reporting that ICC Prosecutor Karim Khan had lost access to his email (Microsoft had suspended his account), that his bank accounts had been frozen, and that American ICC officials had been warned they risked arrest if they set foot in the United States — all in connection with the indictment of Netanyahu for war crimes and crimes against humanity (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 22).\n- MR's Clémentine Barzin argued the text was selective: it referenced Mohammed Deif (who had died) but did not characterise the 7 October attacks as terrorist, and made no mention of existing ICC warrants against Vladimir Putin (Ukraine war) or Omar al-Bashir (Darfur), among others (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 22).\n- Barzin also warned against importing the conflict into Brussels society, citing Unia data linking the rise of antisemitism in Brussels directly to the Gaza war, and argued the repeated passage of resolutions outside Brussels competence was damaging institutional credibility (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 22).\n- Ecolo's Farida Tahar and Groen's Celia Groothedde argued that the rule-of-law concern was locally relevant: Groothedde noted that the 'Free Gaza' inscriptions visible across Brussels reflected a feeling among communities of colour that their lives were treated as less valuable, making this an issue for Brussels residents as well as an international one (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 22).\n- DéFI's Joëlle Maison announced a personal abstention on the grounds that the resolution had lost its primary object following the federal clarifications, while colleagues Jonathan de Patoul and Cécile Jodogne voted in favour (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 22).\n- Open Vld's Imane Belguenani explained that her group had maintained a twenty-year practice of abstaining on international-affairs resolutions at Brussels level, but had made an exception for the previous A-122 vote 'to avoid breaking consensus' (CRI, 16 May 2025 — session nr. 22).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The resolution was essentially written into futility between drafting and vote: within days of tabling, the federal government's own clarifications removed the main factual premise, leaving PS to argue the value of belt-and-suspenders symbolic reaffirmation against parties that could plausibly reply 'the problem is already solved'.\n- The 'selective justice' charge (Putin missing from the text) is MR's strongest analytical point and hardest for supporters to rebut: a text framed as universal but operationally specific to one conflict opens itself to the accusation that international law is being deployed as a political instrument rather than a principle.\n- The separation-of-powers argument (execution of warrants is judicial, not executive) is constitutionally correct and provides a face-saving rationale for MR and cd&v to oppose or abstain without appearing to endorse non-compliance with the ICC — their objection is procedural, not substantive.\n- The Unia data on rising antisemitism gives MR an additional local-competence hook: social cohesion is a genuine Brussels competence, so opposing the resolution on those grounds recasts a foreign-policy dispute as a legitimate regional governance concern.", "proposed_by": "Jamal Ikazban (PS) as first signatory, and co-signers including Nadia El Yousfi (PS) and others across left and centrist groups; rapporteur Nadia El Yousfi (PS); dossier NOS A-114/1, 2 and 3 — 2024/2025", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00022/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "PTB": "for", "GRN": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "MR": "abstain", "CDV": "against", "OVLD": "abstain", "LE": "abstain", "VB": "abstain", "NVA": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00023-v1", "date": "2025-05-28", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00023", "point": 1, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance (projet d'ordonnance, assent)", "topic": "Aviation emissions", "headline": "Assent to the inter-regional cooperation agreement integrating aviation into the EU Emissions Trading System, progressively phasing out free allowances and extending the scheme to intra-European flights.", "title": "L'ordonnance donne l'assentiment de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale à l'accord de coopération du 4 avril 2024, qui modifie l'accord de 2013 relatif à l'intégration des activités aériennes dans le système communautaire d'échange de quotas d'émission (EU ETS), dans le cadre du paquet européen « ajustement à l'objectif 55 ». Les principales modifications portent sur la suppression progressive des quotas gratuits pour l'aviation, l'extension du système aux vols intraeuropéens et l'intégration du mécanisme international Corsia en droit belge. La transposition, qui aurait dû intervenir fin 2023, a été finalisée par la Commission nationale climat après examen des observations du Conseil d'État.", "raw_outcome": "L'ordonnance donne l'assentiment de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale à l'accord de coopération du 4 avril 2024, qui modifie l'accord de 2013 relatif à l'intégration des activités aériennes dans le système communautaire d'échange de quotas d'émission (EU ETS), dans le cadre du paquet européen « ajustement à l'objectif 55 ». Les principales modifications portent sur la suppression progressive des quotas gratuits pour l'aviation, l'extension du système aux vols intraeuropéens et l'intégration du mécanisme international Corsia en droit belge. La transposition, qui aurait dû intervenir fin 2023, a été finalisée par la Commission nationale climat après examen des observations du Conseil d'État.", "source_brief": "Aviation is one of the most carbon-intensive and hardest-to-decarbonise sectors: aircraft fuel is not subject to excise duties, low-cost and private jet traffic keeps growing, and electric or hydrogen aircraft remain years away. This ordinance gives the Brussels Parliament's assent to the revised inter-federal cooperation agreement that updates Belgium's implementation of the EU Emissions Trading System for aviation under the EU's Fit for 55 package.\n\n- **What changes:** free emissions allowances for airlines are progressively abolished, the ETS is extended to all intra-European flights, and the international CORSIA offset mechanism is integrated into Belgian law.\n- **The constitutional step:** because aviation is a shared federal-regional competence, all four Belgian entities must each pass an assent ordinance; Brussels is acting on the text agreed by the National Climate Commission.\n- **The delay:** the transposition was due by end of 2023; the parliament is catching up after the National Climate Commission finalised the texts and addressed remarks from the Council of State.\n- **The rub:** supporters see this as a necessary polluter-pays step and a level playing field for airlines; critics argue the ETS is a market mechanism that lets big polluters buy their way out of real change, that CORSIA offset projects almost never deliver genuine emission reductions, and that aviation still enjoys far cheaper travel costs than rail alternatives.", "deep_facts": "- The ordinance (dossier A-99, nos A-99/1, 2 et 3 – 2024/2025) gives Brussels Capital Region's assent to the cooperation agreement of 4 April 2024, which amends the original cooperation agreement of 2 September 2013 on aviation in the EU ETS; the texts were reported to the plenary by rapporteur Mounir Laarissi (CRI, 28 May 2025).\n- The reform is part of the EU Fit for 55 package targeting at least 55% reduction in greenhouse gas emissions by 2030 relative to 1990 levels; the Brussels ordinance is one of several parallel assent texts required across all Belgian federated entities because aviation straddles federal and regional competences (CRI, 28 May 2025).\n- Key changes introduced by the revised agreement: (1) progressive abolition of free CO2 allowances for the aviation sector; (2) extension of the EU ETS to intra-European flights; (3) integration of the CORSIA (Carbon Offsetting and Reduction Scheme for International Aviation) international offset mechanism into Belgian law (CRI, 28 May 2025).\n- The transposition should have been completed by end of 2023; the delay was attributed to the complexity of the inter-federal coordination; the National Climate Commission examined remarks from the Council of State before finalising the texts, and the minister stated those concerns were fully addressed in the environment committee (CRI, 28 May 2025).\n- Minister Alain Maron identified several reasons aviation is a climate policy weak point: no excise taxes on aviation fuel; continued sector growth with more flights and airports seeking expansion (including Zaventem, Liège and Charleroi); private jets; and the near-term unavailability of electric or hydrogen aircraft (CRI, 28 May 2025).\n- The PTB group opposed the text, arguing the ETS is a market mechanism allowing large multinationals to buy the right to keep polluting rather than structurally changing their practices; PTB cited studies suggesting only 2% of CORSIA offset projects result in genuine emission reductions, and called instead for massive rail investment, strict rules on short-haul flights, removal of aviation fiscal privileges and a ban on private jets (CRI, 28 May 2025).\n- Several speakers noted the contradiction between phasing in carbon pricing for aviation while Flanders and Wallonia simultaneously renew and expand airport environmental permits — the minister acknowledged that all policy levels should align their approaches (CRI, 28 May 2025).\n- Multiple groups (MR, Les Engagés, Ecolo, PS, DéFI, cd&v, Team Fouad Ahidar) stressed that making flights more expensive only makes climate sense if accompanied by massive investment in international and domestic rail, so that sustainable alternatives are available and affordable (CRI, 28 May 2025).\n- The ETS principle, as explained by the minister, is not simply to let industries buy pollution rights indefinitely: CO2 quotas decrease over time, making each tonne of emissions progressively costlier, which is designed to create a structural economic incentive to decarbonise; a number of previously free quotas are eliminated under this revised agreement (CRI, 28 May 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The broad cross-party yes vote (with only PTB's 15 voting against) reflects this being a compliance obligation rather than a choice: Belgium had no option but to transpose EU law, and Brussels could not unilaterally block or reshape the cooperation agreement, which meant most groups could vote yes without fully endorsing ETS as climate strategy.\n- The PTB dissent is structurally coherent — opposing market-based mechanisms on principle — but it also serves a political function of differentiating PTB from the rest when the practical outcome (the text passing) was never in doubt.\n- The airport-expansion contradiction raised by the minister points at the real political limit of the instrument: carbon pricing through ETS is a federal-European mechanism, while permitting decisions that drive traffic growth sit with Wallonia and Flanders, meaning Brussels can assent to pricing but cannot close the loop on volume.\n- CORSIA is the most contested element in the room: integrating a mechanism whose environmental integrity is heavily disputed into Belgian law while the EU and ICAO continue to debate its adequacy suggests the legal compliance frame is doing more work than the climate accountability frame in this vote.", "proposed_by": "Brussels Capital Region Government (minister Alain Maron); co-signed by the federal State, Walloon Region, Flemish Region", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00023/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "CDV": "for", "VB": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "GRN": "for", "DEFI": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for", "PTB": "against"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00027-v4", "date": "2025-06-19", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00027", "point": 4, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Motion (ordre du jour pur et simple)", "topic": "Antisemitism motion", "headline": "Adopt the pure-and-simple motion tabled by Hasan Koyuncu (PS), moving to the day's order without debate, thereby shelving Clémentine Barzin's motivated motion calling for a dedicated Brussels antisemitism action plan.", "title": "La séance plénière du 19 juin 2025 a été saisie de deux motions déposées à l'issue de l'interpellation de Mme Clémentine Barzin (MR) à la secrétaire d'État Nawal Ben Hamou sur les actions de lutte contre l'antisémitisme au regard des résolutions de 2005 et de 2023 : un ordre du jour motivé de Mme Barzin appelant à un plan bruxellois spécifique, et un ordre du jour pur et simple de M. Hasan Koyuncu (PS). Conformément à l'article 119.7 du règlement, l'ordre du jour pur et simple a la priorité de droit. Le débat a opposé ceux qui estiment que l'antisémitisme, en raison de ses racines historiques singulières et de ses manifestations propres, requiert une approche distincte, et ceux qui privilégient un cadre global de lutte antiraciste, jugeant la résolution d'octobre 2023 déjà adoptée suffisamment ciblée.", "raw_outcome": "La séance plénière du 19 juin 2025 a été saisie de deux motions déposées à l'issue de l'interpellation de Mme Clémentine Barzin (MR) à la secrétaire d'État Nawal Ben Hamou sur les actions de lutte contre l'antisémitisme au regard des résolutions de 2005 et de 2023 : un ordre du jour motivé de Mme Barzin appelant à un plan bruxellois spécifique, et un ordre du jour pur et simple de M. Hasan Koyuncu (PS). Conformément à l'article 119.7 du règlement, l'ordre du jour pur et simple a la priorité de droit. Le débat a opposé ceux qui estiment que l'antisémitisme, en raison de ses racines historiques singulières et de ses manifestations propres, requiert une approche distincte, et ceux qui privilégient un cadre global de lutte antiraciste, jugeant la résolution d'octobre 2023 déjà adoptée suffisamment ciblée.", "source_brief": "Following an interpellation by Clémentine Barzin (MR) to Secretary of State Nawal Ben Hamou on Brussels actions to combat antisemitism in light of the 2005 and 2023 resolutions, two competing motions were tabled. Barzin's motivated motion called for a specific Brussels antisemitism plan separate from the broader anti-racism framework; Koyuncu's pure-and-simple motion, which under parliamentary rules takes procedural priority, instead proposes to close the session without endorsing that call.\n\n- **What triggered the debate:** cited incidents — antisemitic remarks and assaults at a university, a swastika on a Second World War resistance memorial, named individuals' faces and addresses posted on Brussels streets — prompted MR to argue that antisemitism requires a distinct, targeted response beyond the existing Brussels anti-racism plan.\n- **The majority's counter-argument:** several groups argued that the Secretary of State had already demonstrated concrete action in committee, that a 2023 parliamentary resolution already addressed antisemitism specifically, and that a global cross-cutting anti-racism approach is more unifying than a motion focused on one form of discrimination.\n- **Gaza and context:** some speakers linked the resurgence of antisemitic acts to the aftermath of 7 October 2023 and the Gaza war; others argued the same period has also seen rising Islamophobia and racist violence, citing incidents in Brussels, and called for a working group covering all forms of racism together.\n- **The rub:** whether an explicit, standalone plan to fight antisemitism — with its historically specific roots and particular manifestations — is more effective and appropriate, or whether folding it into a universal anti-racism approach better serves solidarity and avoids selective indignation.", "deep_facts": "- The interpellation was addressed by Mme Clémentine Barzin (MR) to Mme Nawal Ben Hamou, Secretary of State for Housing and Equal Opportunities, concerning actions to combat antisemitism in light of the 2005 and 2023 resolutions; a motivated motion was filed by Barzin on 12 June 2025 and a pure-and-simple motion by M. Hasan Koyuncu (PS) on 17 June 2025 (CRI, 19 Jun 2025).\n- Under article 119.7 of the Brussels Parliament's rules of procedure, the pure-and-simple motion takes procedural priority over the motivated motion; by being adopted, it renders the motivated motion 'sans objet' (without effect), as confirmed by the president at the close of the vote (CRI, 19 Jun 2025).\n- Mme Barzin cited specific recent incidents: antisemitic statements and physical aggression at a Brussels university; a swastika painted on a memorial plaque honouring three young Second World War resistance fighters; personal attacks naming individuals on social media; and posters affixed in Brussels streets displaying the faces, names and addresses of prominent Jewish personalities (CRI, 19 Jun 2025).\n- Barzin's stated rationale for a dedicated plan: antisemitism shares characteristics with other racisms but has specific historical roots stretching back through centuries, including anti-Judaism in Christian tradition, the dissemination of conspiratorial narratives, and the 20th-century genocide; she argued these specificities justify a distinct response, not a dilution of the struggle into other battles (CRI, 19 Jun 2025).\n- PS speaker Jamal Ikazban argued the Secretary of State had already demonstrated she was acting — citing a October 2023 resolution specifically targeting antisemitism already adopted by a large majority — and proposed a cross-party working group (excluding far right) to combat all forms of hatred, while noting other serious racist violence including an averted mosque attack in Molenbeek and violence against Muslim residents on 4 May (CRI, 19 Jun 2025).\n- PTB questioned the political motivation behind the MR motion, noting MR had not voted for the October 2023 antisemitism resolution adopted by a large majority, and also citing MR for having equated criticism of Israeli state policy with antisemitism, which PTB argued was disputed by many Jewish organisations; PTB affirmed firm opposition to all antisemitism while supporting the pure-and-simple motion (CRI, 19 Jun 2025).\n- DéFI (Joëlle Maison) argued antisemitism has its own specificity warranting targeted attention while simultaneously calling for a working group to develop parallel plans — one targeting antisemitism, one targeting Islamophobia and violence against Muslims — to address both surges linked to the Gaza conflict and broader conflicts being imported into Brussels society (CRI, 19 Jun 2025).\n- N-VA (Gilles Verstraeten) voted against the pure-and-simple motion, arguing it should be possible to discuss specific forms of hatred and design targeted responses without importing every other issue into the same debate, and warning that the inability to have a calm, focused conversation about antisemitism is itself concerning (CRI, 19 Jun 2025).\n- Les Engagés (Marie Cruysmans) voted for the pure-and-simple motion with one abstention, expressing firm condemnation of antisemitism and all racist acts but arguing the motivated motion would be more divisive than unifying in the current geopolitical context; Ecolo (Farida Tahar) and Groen (Emile Luhahi) also supported the pure-and-simple motion while reaffirming commitment to fighting antisemitism (CRI, 19 Jun 2025).\n- The baroness Regina Sluszny, a former hidden child from the Second World War, had addressed the plenary chamber in the days before the vote, an appearance referenced by Barzin in her statement as a reminder of where silence and inaction lead (CRI, 19 Jun 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The procedural mechanism (pure-and-simple motion displacing the motivated motion) means the majority did not need to argue the substantive case against Barzin's plan on its merits — voting for the simple motion is procedurally a non-decision, which is precisely why it is politically attractive for groups who do not want to be seen as opposing anti-antisemitism action but also do not want to endorse MR's framing.\n- The 2023 resolution becomes the majority's shield: pointing to a text already adopted lets groups claim they have already acted specifically on antisemitism while declining to take up what MR frames as inadequate follow-through — the real dispute is over whether the 2023 resolution is being implemented, not over principle.\n- The Gaza dimension runs just below the surface throughout: several speakers linked rising antisemitism explicitly to 7 October 2023 and the war, while others linked rising Islamophobia to the same events; the tension between condemning antisemitism specifically and insisting on parallel treatment of Islamophobia reflects the way the Middle East conflict maps onto the composition and sensitivities of the Brussels Parliament.\n- Framing a standalone antisemitism plan as 'divisive' is the most revealing argument in the debate: it implies that in Brussels's specific demographic and political context, a motion that names one form of discrimination for a dedicated plan risks being read as taking a side in a broader conflict — which is itself a signal of how internationally freighted the local politics of antisemitism have become.", "proposed_by": "Hasan Koyuncu (PS); motivated motion it displaces: Clémentine Barzin (MR), filed 12 June 2025", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00027/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "PTB": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "TFA": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "MR": "against", "NVA": "against", "DEFI": "abstain", "OVLD": "abstain", "VB": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00029-v4", "date": "2025-06-27", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00029", "point": 4, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance (proposition d'ordonnance)", "topic": "Student housing tax", "headline": "Exempt non-profit university and college student housing from the regional property tax (precompte immobilier) from 1 January 2026, by rewriting an ambiguous 2017 clause of the Income Tax Code.", "title": "La proposition d'ordonnance modifie le Code des impots sur les revenus 1992 afin de clarifier que les logements etudiants proposes sans but lucratif par les universites et hautes ecoles sont exoneres du precompte immobilier. L'article 253, paragraphe 4, insere par une ordonnance de 2017 entree en vigueur le 1er janvier 2018, avait cree un doute sur le maintien de cette exoneration. Le texte n'est pas retroactif et entrerait en vigueur le 1er janvier 2026.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition d'ordonnance modifie le Code des impots sur les revenus 1992 afin de clarifier que les logements etudiants proposes sans but lucratif par les universites et hautes ecoles sont exoneres du precompte immobilier. L'article 253, paragraphe 4, insere par une ordonnance de 2017 entree en vigueur le 1er janvier 2018, avait cree un doute sur le maintien de cette exoneration. Le texte n'est pas retroactif et entrerait en vigueur le 1er janvier 2026.", "source_brief": "A 2017 rewrite of the Income Tax Code left it unclear whether student rooms (kots) owned by Brussels universities and colleges still escaped the regional property tax. The tax office read the ambiguity as meaning the exemption was gone and began billing; this text settles the question by writing the exemption back in, but only from 2026 onward.\n\n- **Who it touches:** students renting public, below-market rooms from institutions like the ULB and VUB, where any tax landing on the building eventually shows up in the rent.\n- **The money already in dispute:** an administration note put the 2018-2024 billing for the communes concerned at roughly 1.7 million euros, much of it already contested in administrative and court proceedings.\n- **The deliberate gap:** the fix is not retroactive, so the 2017-2025 period is left for the institutions, the Region and the courts to settle separately.\n- **The rub:** whether a tax break for university-owned housing is a sensible way to keep student rents down, or a carve-out that quietly costs communes revenue they were starting to collect.", "deep_facts": "- The text is a proposition d'ordonnance modifying the Code des impots sur les revenus 1992; the operative provision concerns article 253, paragraph 4, inserted by a 2017 ordinance that entered into force on 1 January 2018 (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- The stated problem: the 2017 wording created doubt over whether the property-tax (precompte immobilier) exemption for student housing owned by universities and hautes ecoles survived; such housing had previously been exempt without contestation, but Bruxelles Fiscalite read the new text as having removed the exemption and enrolled the buildings for the tax (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- Per an administration note circulated about two hours before the committee meeting, the enrolment for 2018-2024 across the communes concerned was of the order of 1.7 million euros [estime]; of that, 700.000 euros are the subject of pending administrative reclamations and more than 800.000 euros the subject of pending judicial recours, with the 800.000 figure not yet including the 2024 enrolment (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- The ordinance is prospective only: it is stated to take effect on 1 January 2026 and to change nothing for the past, deliberately, to avoid adding legal uncertainty while institutions' court cases against the Region proceed; for this reason proposers considered an avis of the Conseil d'Etat unnecessary (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- Context figures cited in debate: average kot rent of about 680 euros per month, rising roughly 5% per year, described as the highest in Belgium; more than 100.000 students in Brussels; a year of higher education in the Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles said to cost between 12.000 and 15.000 euros, with housing the largest share (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- Mechanism cited: where the tax is billed, paid and immediately contested then frozen, the Region currently must later repay the sums with interest of 4% on the total annual amount; proposers argue the exemption is therefore cheaper for Region and communes than the status quo (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- A speaker reported that one institution must cover about 1 million euros a year on student housing, of which 200.000 euros goes to loan repayments and 800.000 euros to advancing and paying rent students cannot themselves pay; another reported the VUB had to raise its own kot rents by 10% after the fiscal change (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- One original co-signer (Olivier Willocx, MR) announced he was withdrawing his name from the proposal \"for reasons you are aware of\" while still supporting the text's fiscal-clarity principle (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- Proposers note that in 2018 the Mesa is not at issue here; rather, they cite a 2007 Flemish circular treating such student housing as an essential education service warranting property-tax exemption, and argue other Regions exempt this housing, so taxing it in Brussels would create unfair competition pushing students toward Ghent, Antwerp or Leuven (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The non-retroactivity is the crux of the bargain: it lets the assembly affirm the exemption going forward without conceding the 2018-2025 bills, which would directly hit some communes' budgets and pre-empt live litigation — so the text buys consensus precisely by not touching the contested money.\n- Pitching it as revenue-neutral or even cheaper (the 4% repayment-with-interest argument) is what lets fiscally cautious groups vote yes without owning a 'tax cut for universities' framing.\n- The competition-with-Flanders argument reframes a social-housing measure as a defensive economic one, broadening the coalition beyond left groups to include market-minded members.\n- Skipping the Conseil d'Etat avis trims weeks off the timetable but leaves the prospective-only design as the main shield against a later legality challenge.", "proposed_by": "Celia Groothedde (Groen), Martin Casier (PS) and co-signers across several groups", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00029/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for", "OVLD": "abstain", "CDV": "abstain", "VB": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00029-v5", "date": "2025-06-27", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00029", "point": 5, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Amendment to an ordinance", "topic": "Public-fund transparency", "headline": "Replace the Guarantee Fund ordinance's reporting article (art. 23) with a single rewritten transparency clause requiring the Fund's accounts and board report to reach the government and Parliament by 30 June each year.", "title": "Un amendement n° 1, depose par M. Olivier Willocx, remplace l'article 3 afin de reecrire integralement l'article 23 de l'ordonnance de 1999 : le conseil d'administration arrete chaque 31 decembre le bilan et le compte de resultat du Fonds, transmis directement, avec le rapport du conseil d'administration et celui du reviseur, au gouvernement et au Parlement au plus tard le 30 juin de l'annee suivante. L'auteur invoque une mise en conformite et la modernisation d'un article juge confus et redondant.", "raw_outcome": "Un amendement n° 1, depose par M. Olivier Willocx, remplace l'article 3 afin de reecrire integralement l'article 23 de l'ordonnance de 1999 : le conseil d'administration arrete chaque 31 decembre le bilan et le compte de resultat du Fonds, transmis directement, avec le rapport du conseil d'administration et celui du reviseur, au gouvernement et au Parlement au plus tard le 30 juin de l'annee suivante. L'auteur invoque une mise en conformite et la modernisation d'un article juge confus et redondant.", "source_brief": "Bolted onto the ordinance raising the Guarantee Fund's ceiling is this rival amendment over how the Fund reports to Parliament. It would scrap and rewrite the whole reporting article (art. 23), folding in useful bits of a separately added clause, and set a clear annual deadline — but, critics note, it swaps Parliament's prior say on extra credit tranches for a mere notification.\n\n- **What it changes:** the board would have to send the balance sheet, results and audit report directly to the government and Parliament by 30 June each year — later than the existing 30 April deadline, which the mover argues is impossible to meet since accounts are approved in May.\n- **The oversight catch:** the mover frames it as fixing a drafting muddle and modernising one clean clause; opponents say it strips out the essential parliamentary-control element kept in a near-identical rival text.\n- **The backdrop charge:** the mover claims Parliament has received no Fund report since 2018 and that the board is absent from the legally required UBO register.\n- **The rub:** whether this is a genuine tightening of transparency over a near-100-million-euro public guarantee, or a watered-down copy that quietly trades real parliamentary control for after-the-fact notification.", "deep_facts": "- Amendment no. 1, tabled by Olivier Willocx (MR), replaces article 3 of the ordinance so as to rewrite article 23 of the 1999 ordinance in full (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- Under the amendment's text, the board (conseil d'administration) draws up the Fund's balance sheet and results account each 31 December and transmits them directly, with the board report and the report of the government-appointed auditor (reviseur), to the government and the Parliament, at the latest on 30 June of the following year (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- The mover's stated grounds: the existing 30 April transmission deadline is materially impossible because accounts are systematically approved in May; and a committee-added article 19.1 created parallel reporting logic with article 23 without articulating or removing the old obligations, described as confused and redundant — hence a proposal to modernise article 23 as a single clear provision (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- Difference from a near-identical earlier amendment (Team Fouad Ahidar, supported in committee by PTB and MR): the MR version asks for a notification to Parliament rather than an avis (opinion) of Parliament before adding a credit tranche; opponents characterise this as removing the essential parliamentary-control element (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- The mover's transparency charges: no annual report, balance sheet or results account has been transmitted to the assembly directly from the Guarantee Fund since 2018 (last report 2018; amount cited 61 million euros), contrary to article 23 of the 1999 ordinance; and the Fund's administrators are not found in the UBO register, a legal obligation; he also notes article 11 requires Parliament to designate three representatives to the Fund, which he says has not happened (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- Opponents' reply: the requested information (encours des engagements, default rate, premiums received) already appears in the Fund's annual accounts; activity reports are on the finance&invest.brussels site (2023 report pages 38-39); the board already informs the competent minister past a 75% threshold under the arrete of 25 May 2022; and a Fund official answered all committee questions — so the amendment was judged redundant and without added value (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- Procedural note: a near-identical Team Fouad Ahidar amendment was tabled to the same text; the dispute on this amendment turned substantially on the MR having supported that earlier amendment in committee days before, then tabling its own (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The fight is only partly about reporting dates: the load-bearing change is avis -> notification, which converts a parliamentary veto over extra credit tranches into a courtesy heads-up, materially weakening control even as the wrapper is sold as 'modernising transparency'.\n- Tabling a near-verbatim copy of a rival's amendment, minus the control element, lets the mover claim the transparency high ground while declining to hand the rival group a co-authorship win — the procedural theatre is the substance.\n- The 'no report since 2018 / absent UBO entry / undesignated representatives' charges, if borne out, undercut the majority's core defence that existing controls make new ones redundant — the existing controls may simply not be operating.\n- A 30 April-to-30 June deadline shift is defensible on accounting calendars, but bundling it with the notification downgrade means a member who agrees on dates cannot vote the fix without also accepting the weaker control.", "proposed_by": "Olivier Willocx (MR)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "rejected", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00029/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"MR": "for", "LE": "for", "NVA": "for", "DEFI": "against", "PS": "against", "TFA": "against", "PTB": "against", "GRN": "against", "Ecolo": "against", "VOOR": "against", "OVLD": "abstain", "CDV": "abstain", "VB": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00029-v7", "date": "2025-06-27", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00029", "point": 7, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance (proposition d'ordonnance)", "topic": "SME loan guarantees", "headline": "Raise the intervention ceiling of the Brussels Guarantee Fund from 62 million to 90 million euros, so it can keep guaranteeing bank loans to local SMEs and self-employed, with no extra regional subsidy.", "title": "La proposition d'ordonnance vise a accroitre le plafond d'intervention du Fonds bruxellois de garantie, qui passerait de 62 millions d'euros a 90 millions d'euros (objet de l'article 2). Cree en 1999, le Fonds facilite l'acces au credit professionnel pour les PME et les independants et est concu pour fonctionner a l'equilibre, sans accroissement des subsides regionaux. Rien qu'en 2024, le fonds a permis d'activer 42 millions d'euros de credits au benefice de 153 entreprises.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition d'ordonnance vise a accroitre le plafond d'intervention du Fonds bruxellois de garantie, qui passerait de 62 millions d'euros a 90 millions d'euros (objet de l'article 2). Cree en 1999, le Fonds facilite l'acces au credit professionnel pour les PME et les independants et est concu pour fonctionner a l'equilibre, sans accroissement des subsides regionaux. Rien qu'en 2024, le fonds a permis d'activer 42 millions d'euros de credits au benefice de 153 entreprises.", "source_brief": "The Brussels Guarantee Fund, created in 1999, stands surety so that small firms and self-employed people who would otherwise be refused can still get a bank loan. Its ceiling has not moved in 25 years and is close to being hit; this text lifts it from 62 to 90 million euros so the Fund can keep backing new credit.\n\n- **Who it touches:** entrepreneurs, shopkeepers and self-employed people who lack family backing or an easy bank yes, and rely on the public guarantee to get financed.\n- **The cost claim:** backers stress the rise needs no extra subsidy — the Fund is built to run at balance, funded by premiums from banks and beneficiary firms, and only pays out on default or bankruptcy (about 13-15% of amounts granted).\n- **Why now:** the ceiling was about 90% used in March and projected to hit 100% in September, which is why the text was brought urgently.\n- **The rub:** whether expanding public exposure backing private loans is smart leverage for the local economy at near-zero budget cost, or a near-100-million-euro commitment waved through with thin oversight of how the Fund actually performs.", "deep_facts": "- The text (proposition d'ordonnance, dossier A-116) raises the Guarantee Fund's intervention ceiling (plafond d'intervention / d'engagement) from 62 million euros to 90 million euros; this is the object of article 2 (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- The Fonds bruxellois de garantie was created in 1999 to support entrepreneurship by facilitating SME and self-employed access to professional credit; the ceiling has not changed since 1999, cited as nearly 62 million euros (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- Budget framing: the Fund is designed to run at equilibrium without increased regional subsidy; the subsidy is fixed at 1.125.000 euros; backers state no fund increase is needed for the ceiling rise, current budget plus rising premiums from banks and beneficiary firms being sufficient; over the last two years the Fund reportedly partly repaid its subsidy to the Region (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- The guarantee intervenes only in last resort, on default or bankruptcy, concerning 13 to 15% of amounts granted (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- Activity figures cited: in 2024 the Fund activated 42 million euros of credit benefiting 153 firms; over seven years, 4.164 jobs generated, 90 million euros granted and 621 firms supported (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- Urgency rationale: the existing ceiling was about 90% reached in March and projected to reach 100% in September, prompting urgent submission (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- The text allows raising the ceiling further by up to four additional tranches of 12,5 million euros each; under the version proposed, activating a tranche requires notification to (not prior approval of) the Parliament (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- An oversight clause (article 19.1) added in committee and obligations under article 23 of the 1999 ordinance provide for parliamentary reporting; an arrete of 25 May 2022 already requires the Fund's board to inform the competent minister when a 75% threshold is exceeded (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- A safeguard cited: if the average default rate across active engagements exceeds 20% in a budget year, the Government suspends any new tranche activation and submits a thorough evaluation to the Parliament with a view to possible revision of the engagement ceiling (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- A contested point: one group stated the Parliament has received no annual report, balance sheet or accounts directly from the Guarantee Fund since 2018 (the last report dating from 2018, on amounts cited as 61 million euros), and that the Fund's administrators do not appear in the UBO register; proposers replied that activity reports are published on the finance&invest.brussels website (e.g. pages 38-39 of the 2023 report) and that a Fund official answered all questions in committee (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).\n- An initial version reportedly proposed a higher ceiling (figures of 100 million and even 125 million euros were referenced in debate) before being brought down to 90 million euros (CRI, 27 Jun 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The decisive selling point is 'budget-neutral': in a tight fiscal context, a measure that expands support without new spending is the rare lever almost every group can back, which is why the debate is about oversight, not about the ceiling itself.\n- The 90-million figure reads as a negotiated landing point — down from 100-125 million — calibrated to projections plus a safety margin, trading maximal headroom for broad buy-in.\n- The tranche mechanism (four x 12,5 million, notification not approval) hands the government future capacity to keep raising exposure without returning for a full vote, which is exactly the slippage the transparency fight is really about.\n- The 'no report since 2018 / no UBO entry' charge, if accurate, matters beyond this vote: it questions whether the existing controls the majority leans on to call new oversight redundant are actually being honoured.", "proposed_by": "Farida Tahar, Zakia Khattabi (Ecolo) and co-signers", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00029/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00031-v1", "date": "2025-07-10", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00031", "point": 1, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Invasive species", "headline": "Commit Brussels Region to a coordinated prevention and control plan against the invasive Asian hornet, designating a single lead agency to manage nest removal and protecting bees, biodiversity and public safety.", "title": "La proposition de resolution demande au gouvernement bruxellois d'etablir un plan de prevention et de lutte contre la proliferation du frelon asiatique (Vespa velutina nigrithorax), apparu pour la premiere fois en Region bruxelloise en 2018. Les interventions des pompiers pour des nids sont passees de 20 en 2020 a pres de 670 en 2023. Le texte reclame l'inscription de l'espece sur la liste bruxelloise des especes invasives, la designation d'un acteur de reference unique pour la neutralisation des nids, des campagnes de sensibilisation et la clarification des conditions d'intervention gratuite.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition de resolution demande au gouvernement bruxellois d'etablir un plan de prevention et de lutte contre la proliferation du frelon asiatique (Vespa velutina nigrithorax), apparu pour la premiere fois en Region bruxelloise en 2018. Les interventions des pompiers pour des nids sont passees de 20 en 2020 a pres de 670 en 2023. Le texte reclame l'inscription de l'espece sur la liste bruxelloise des especes invasives, la designation d'un acteur de reference unique pour la neutralisation des nids, des campagnes de sensibilisation et la clarification des conditions d'intervention gratuite.", "source_brief": "The Asian hornet (Vespa velutina nigrithorax) arrived in Brussels in 2018 and has spread exponentially: fire-brigade callouts for nests jumped from 20 in 2020 to 670 in 2023, and each nest devours an average twelve kilos of insects per year — 60% of them bees. This resolution asks the government to put a proper plan in place.\n\n- **What it asks for:** listing the hornet on the Brussels invasive-species register, designating a single coordinating body (proposed candidate: Bruxelles Environnement) for nest neutralisation, launching awareness campaigns, and clarifying when fire-brigade removal is free of charge.\n- **Why bees matter:** speakers stress that honeybees and solitary bees pollinate the majority of our food crops; their collapse would cascade through the food chain. Urban solitary bees in Brussels are already under pressure from hard surfaces and density.\n- **Who pays today:** removing a nest costs 250–1,000 euros; the total regional cost was estimated between 170,000 and 700,000 euros; current fire-brigade rules are unclear about the threshold for free intervention, which risks under-reporting.\n- **The rub:** whether a non-binding resolution will actually unlock resources and a named coordinating agency, or whether it becomes another unfunded commitment while nests keep multiplying.", "deep_facts": "- The Asian hornet (Vespa velutina nigrithorax) was first detected in Brussels Region in 2018; it has been on the EU list of invasive exotic species of concern since 13 July 2016, and is cited as having been accidentally introduced in France in 2004 (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Fire-brigade interventions for Asian-hornet nests in Brussels rose from 20 in 2020 to approximately 670 in 2023; one speaker noted 89 nests were counted in a single 4-square-kilometre stretch of Woluwe-Saint-Pierre alone (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Each nest consumes an average of twelve kilos of insects per year, of which 60% are bees; a single founding queen can produce 200 to 500 new founding queens annually, creating an exponential reproduction curve (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- About 30% of nests are located less than ten metres from the ground, creating direct public-safety risks; a nest fell from a cornice in Etterbeek onto a public street near a grocery shop; in France fatalities from swarm attacks have been recorded (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The cost of destroying a single nest ranges from 250 to 1,000 euros; the total regional bill was estimated between 170,000 and 700,000 euros per year; some residents still bear removal costs themselves, which discourages reporting (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- An existing convention limits free Siamu (Brussels fire service) intervention to cases meeting a 'serious immediate danger' criterion, but the threshold is unclear — e.g., whether a nest at 30 metres qualifies as 'less than seven metres from a school' criterion (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The resolution calls for the hornet to be added to the Brussels invasive-species list, an action plan to be drawn up, a single reference body for nest management to be designated, and awareness campaigns to be launched including guidance for municipal officials (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The PTB, while voting for the resolution, argued the Siamu already lacks the staffing and equipment for this additional environmental mission and called for a dedicated public-sector unit — such as a specialised Bruxelles Environnement brigade — rather than shifting costs to private contractors (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Team Fouad Ahidar called for nest removal to be free for low-income households and proposed spring queen-trapping citizen initiatives as a low-cost complementary tool (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The MR withdrew its co-signature from the text, citing a party decision not to co-sign with certain other groups, but confirmed its substantive support and voted for the resolution (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The 20-to-670 intervention curve in three years is the political engine: it turns an environmental niche issue into a public-order and budget issue, broadening the coalition to parties otherwise indifferent to biodiversity.\n- Naming 'a single coordinating body' is the load-bearing ask — diffuse responsibility across communes, fire service and Bruxelles Environnement is precisely why the problem has grown unchecked; the resolution's value depends entirely on whether government follows through with a named entity and a budget line.\n- The free-removal question carries a social-equity dimension: residents who cannot afford 250–1,000 euros per nest will under-report, concentrating infestations in lower-income neighbourhoods with older housing stock and fewer green-space managers.\n- A non-binding resolution has low enforcement teeth; the real test is whether the incoming government treats it as an instruction or a decoration.", "proposed_by": "Jonathan de Patoul (DéFI) and co-signers across multiple groups", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00031/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for", "CDV": "for"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00031-v5", "date": "2025-07-10", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00031", "point": 5, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Genocide commemoration", "headline": "Formally recognise 11 July as an official day of reflection and commemoration of the Srebrenica genocide in Brussels Region, and commit regional institutions to annual remembrance actions, educational initiatives and solidarity with survivors.", "title": "La proposition de resolution invite la Region bruxelloise a appliquer la resolution de l'Assemblee generale des Nations unies proclamant le 11 juillet comme journee internationale de reflexion et de commemoration du genocide commis a Srebrenica en 1995. Elle demande que le 11 juillet soit integre dans les actions de sensibilisation regionales, que les initiatives de memoire soient soutenues et que soit exprimee la solidarite avec les familles des victimes et les survivants. Un amendement no 2, soutenant l'initiative de la commune de Saint-Gilles d'inaugurer une plaque commemorative rue de Bosnie le lendemain, a ete adopte a l'unanimite.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition de resolution invite la Region bruxelloise a appliquer la resolution de l'Assemblee generale des Nations unies proclamant le 11 juillet comme journee internationale de reflexion et de commemoration du genocide commis a Srebrenica en 1995. Elle demande que le 11 juillet soit integre dans les actions de sensibilisation regionales, que les initiatives de memoire soient soutenues et que soit exprimee la solidarite avec les familles des victimes et les survivants. Un amendement no 2, soutenant l'initiative de la commune de Saint-Gilles d'inaugurer une plaque commemorative rue de Bosnie le lendemain, a ete adopte a l'unanimite.", "source_brief": "Thirty years after more than 8,372 Bosnian Muslim men and boys were executed at Srebrenica in July 1995 — the worst mass atrocity on European soil since World War II — the UN General Assembly in 2024 proclaimed 11 July as an International Day of Reflection and Commemoration. This resolution asks Brussels Region to apply that commitment locally.\n\n- **What it asks for:** official recognition of 11 July as a regional commemoration day; encouragement for communes and the government to organise annual ceremonies, expositions and educational actions; solidarity with the Mothers of Srebrenica and survivor associations; and support for a new commemorative plaque in the Rue de Bosnie in Saint-Gilles, unveiled the day after the plenary vote.\n- **The controversy:** some speakers used the debate to draw a parallel with the ongoing conflict in Gaza; MR abstained in committee on those grounds, filed an amendment (later withdrawn) to strengthen the territorial link, and in plenary cited objections to the Gaza references — though it ultimately withdrew its amendment and a near-unanimous assembly adopted a second amendment with full applause.\n- **Amendment 2:** adopted unanimously (79–0), adding support for the Saint-Gilles commemorative plaque initiative; Amendment 1 (MR, proposing the Rue de Bosnie as a formal memorial space) was withdrawn before the vote.\n- **The rub:** whether a regional parliament should go beyond territorial competences to make sweeping solidarity statements on active conflicts, or whether the duty of memory obligates exactly that.", "deep_facts": "- The UN General Assembly proclaimed 11 July as the International Day of Reflection and Commemoration of the Genocide Committed in Srebrenica in 1995 in a resolution adopted in 2024; the Brussels text applies that recognition at the regional level (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The massacre took place between 11 and 16 July 1995; 8,372 men and boys are named on the memorial at Potocari; the youngest victim, Fatima Muhic, was born and died on the same day at the Potocari school, a UN-designated safe zone (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The Srebrenica massacre has been recognised as genocide by the International Court of Justice and the International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia (ICTY); Belgium recognised it early, aligning with the EU and UN position (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The broader Balkan wars produced more than 100,000 dead in Bosnia, 20,000 in Croatia, over 13,000 in Kosovo, an estimated 20,000 women subjected to sexual violence, and hundreds of thousands of displaced persons and refugees (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- An estimated 7,600 persons remain listed as missing in Bosnia-Herzegovina; psychological trauma, described as trans-generational, continues across Balkan societies (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The MR filed Amendment 1 to point 1 of the dispositif, proposing to identify the Rue de Bosnie in Saint-Gilles as a memorial site and provide for a commemorative artwork; this amendment was withdrawn before the vote, with the chair thanking Mme Bytyçi (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Amendment 2 — tabled by the PS, supporting the Saint-Gilles commune initiative to unveil a commemorative plaque in the Rue de Bosnie on 11 July 2025 — was adopted unanimously by 79 votes to 0, with general applause (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The whole-text vote saw 78 yes and 1 abstention; the lone abstainer (MR, Mme Clémentine Barzin) cited two grounds: the tenuous link with regional competences, and objection to the method of drafting, specifically the inclusion of a Gaza parallel in a text about a recognised past genocide (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Several speakers (PS, Ecolo, Team Fouad Ahidar, Groen) explicitly connected the Srebrenica duty of memory to the current situation in Gaza; Ecolo stated 'tout acte de violence de masse et toute atteinte disproportionnee a des populations civiles doivent etre denonces' (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The day after the plenary, 11 July 2025, marked the first official international commemoration day; the PTB argued genuine peace comes not through military alliances but through social justice and solidarity (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The Gaza sub-debate is the political fault line: linking a recognised historical genocide to an active, contested conflict is factually and legally fraught, and the MR's single abstention — rather than a no vote — is a calibrated signal that it supports the memory obligation but objects to the editorial choices.\n- Withdrawing Amendment 1 (MR's memorial-site proposal) reads as a de-escalation to avoid a fracture on what should be a unifying text; it let the assembly end on unanimous applause for Amendment 2 — a classic parliamentary face-saving move.\n- The timing — eve of the 30th anniversary, day before the Saint-Gilles plaque unveiling — transforms the abstract resolution into a concrete civic moment, giving the vote the resonance a purely symbolic text would lack.\n- Drafting a regional commemoration text that stays within territorial competences while satisfying groups that view the resolution's value as precisely its universalist reach is structurally impossible — the abstention proves the tension is real.", "proposed_by": "Leila Agic (PS), Ahmed Laaouej (PS), Zakia Khattabi (Ecolo), Stijn Bex (Groen), Fouad Ahidar (Team Fouad Ahidar) and co-signers", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00031/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00031-v7", "date": "2025-07-10", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00031", "point": 7, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance (proposition d'ordonnance)", "topic": "Disability employment", "headline": "Replace Brussels Region's fragmented disability employment supports with a single unified ordinance — consolidating wage subsidies, self-employment grants and traineeships under Actiris — and restore equal access for Dutch-speaking Brusselers with disabilities.", "title": "La proposition d'ordonnance instaure un regime unifie d'aides a l'emploi pour les personnes en situation de handicap en Region bruxelloise, transferant les competences du service Phare vers Actiris. Elle cree cinq mecanismes d'aide — prime d'insertion, prime pour independants, stages de decouverte et de formation, aides auxiliaires — calcules sur la base du pourcentage de perte de rendement estimee. Elle remedie egalement a l'inegalite d'acces qui prive depuis 2019 les Bruxellois neerlandophones en situation de handicap de ces dispositifs.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition d'ordonnance instaure un regime unifie d'aides a l'emploi pour les personnes en situation de handicap en Region bruxelloise, transferant les competences du service Phare vers Actiris. Elle cree cinq mecanismes d'aide — prime d'insertion, prime pour independants, stages de decouverte et de formation, aides auxiliaires — calcules sur la base du pourcentage de perte de rendement estimee. Elle remedie egalement a l'inegalite d'acces qui prive depuis 2019 les Bruxellois neerlandophones en situation de handicap de ces dispositifs.", "source_brief": "Only 33% of Brusselers with a disability are employed, against a 50% European average. This text transfers competences from the Phare service (under the French Community Commission) to Actiris, the regional employment body, unifying two previously separate aid streams — the Phare insertion premium and the Activa 'reduced capacity' premium — into a single coherent scheme applicable to French and Dutch speakers alike.\n\n- **What changes:** a single wage-subsidy calculated on estimated productivity loss (10–75% of a reference salary), a new self-employment creation grant, discovery and training internships, and auxiliary aids for workplace adaptations; public employers that have not met their disability-quota obligations are excluded from the new benefits.\n- **The NL equity fix:** since 2019, Dutch-speaking Bruxellois with a disability have had no access to these aids — the ordinance closes that gap.\n- **The critics' objection (PTB/PVDA — abstentions):** the core logic of measuring a person's eligibility against their 'productivity loss' forces those already excluded by society to prove their worth against the very standard that marginalises them; the text also lacks binding quotas with sanctions, no formal appeals mechanism, and was voted less than two weeks after tabling without formal consultation of the Brussels Council for Persons with Disabilities.\n- **The rub:** whether incentive-based wage subsidies conditional on productivity assessments are the right lever for inclusion, or whether only enforceable quotas with sanctions — as in the French public-sector model where 6% of staff have a disability — will close the gap.", "deep_facts": "- Article 22ter of the Belgian Constitution, inserted on 12 March 2021, guarantees every person with a disability the right to full inclusion in society, including the right to reasonable accommodations; the ordinance is presented as a regional implementation of that constitutional right (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The employment rate of Bruxellois with a disability is cited at 33% (one speaker cites 27.7% for the Brussels-specific figure against a 57.3% overall rate in the Region, a gap of nearly 30 percentage points), against a 50% European average; the poverty-risk rate for a Belgian with a disability is 22%, compared to 12% for the general population (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Since 2019, Dutch-speaking Bruxellois with a disability have had no access to employment aids; the ordinance restores equal access by bringing all Brussels residents under a single Actiris-administered scheme (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Five aid mechanisms are created: (1) a wage intervention calculated on estimated productivity loss (10–75% of a reference salary); (2) a self-employment creation and maintenance premium; (3) discovery internships (unpaid); (4) training internships and auxiliary aids covering workplace adaptations, equipment and travel costs; (5) a transitional support for those combining both former schemes (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The expression 'perte de rendement' (productivity loss) was acknowledged by the main proposer to be uncomfortable but legally required: EU state-aid rules demand that a subsidy compensates for a measurable economic loss; 'loss of autonomy' alone does not suffice as justification under those rules (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The Brussels Council for Persons with Disabilities (CBPH) issued an emergency advisory opinion the day before the plenary vote stating it had not been formally consulted; proposers replied that multiple contacts occurred in 2023 and 2025 and committed to formal consultation on implementing decrees (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Public-sector employers that have not met their statutory disability-quota obligations (adopted unanimously in parliament about a decade ago but widely unmet due to lack of sanctions) are excluded from eligibility for the new subsidies (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The NGO Diversicom estimated the public-finance gain of moving a person with a disability into employment at slightly more than 30,000 euros (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- PTB criticised the text for being tabled under urgency: the Conseil d'Etat gave no opinion (as it was a proposition, not a projet), the CBPH gave no formal opinion, and the interval between tabling and plenary vote was under two weeks; one PTB speaker argued this violated the disability-rights principle 'nothing about us without us' (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- N-VA expressed concern that the existing Actiris budget transferred from Phare (slightly over 1 million euros, previously underused) may be insufficient once the scheme becomes better known and more accessible — a budget risk given that no government with full powers exists to attach a financial plan (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The vote result was 65 yes, 0 no, 14 abstentions; the abstainers included PTB, PVDA and N-VA (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The NL-equity fix is the political accelerant: it transforms a technical subsidy reform into a rights-restoration story, generating cross-party support that a mere consolidation of Francophone instruments would never have achieved.\n- The PTB abstention is ideologically coherent, not obstructionist: their critique (productivity-loss framework penalises the already marginalised) is a legitimate structural objection, not a procedural complaint — they are voting on principle, not in protest at the process.\n- Piloting the scheme through Actiris rather than a new body is institutionally pragmatic but carries a competence-transfer risk: Phare has decades of specialised knowledge that Actiris staff may not replicate quickly, and the lack of an appeals mechanism means service failures will be harder to challenge.\n- The 'no formal government = no implementing decrees' problem is the real procedural tension: the ordinance's operational value is zero without the government arretes, and the parliament must pass a separate resolution to nudge a caretaker government into action.", "proposed_by": "Joëlle Maison (DéFI), Emile Luhahi (Groen), Imane Belguenani (Open Vld), Farida Tahar (Ecolo), Fadila Laanan (PS), Pascal Smet (Vooruit.brussels), Stéphanie Lange (Les Engagés), Sadik Köksal (MR)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00031/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for", "PTB": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00031-v9", "date": "2025-07-10", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00031", "point": 9, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Proposal for a decision", "topic": "MP pay", "headline": "Cut Brussels MPs' parliamentary indemnity by 5% on a permanent basis, applying the reduction not only to the base pay but also to the extra indemnities paid for special functions.", "title": "Proposition de decision A-103/1 - 2024/2025 visant a reduire les indemnites des parlementaires bruxellois de 5 %. Au-dela d'une baisse de 5 % de l'indemnite de base, le texte propose d'aller un cran plus loin en l'appliquant aussi aux indemnites pour fonctions speciales et en supprimant le caractere temporaire de la mesure. Les deux propositions (A-103 et celle du Bureau) ont fait l'objet d'une discussion conjointe mais d'un vote separe.", "raw_outcome": "Proposition de decision A-103/1 - 2024/2025 visant a reduire les indemnites des parlementaires bruxellois de 5 %. Au-dela d'une baisse de 5 % de l'indemnite de base, le texte propose d'aller un cran plus loin en l'appliquant aussi aux indemnites pour fonctions speciales et en supprimant le caractere temporaire de la mesure. Les deux propositions (A-103 et celle du Bureau) ont fait l'objet d'une discussion conjointe mais d'un vote separe.", "source_brief": "On the table is a standalone proposal to reduce the indemnity Brussels regional MPs draw by 5% as a lasting measure, and to push that cut further than the parallel cross-party text by also shaving 5% off the top-up indemnities paid to members who hold special functions (such as Bureau seats).\n\n- **What it changes:** a 5% reduction on the base parliamentary indemnity, framed as permanent rather than time-limited, plus the same 5% applied to special-function indemnities.\n- **The money cited:** speakers describe a Brussels MP's net monthly pay as more than 7,000 euros, and special-function holders receiving roughly 2,500 euros extra.\n- **The wider frame:** it lands amid a regional budget described as a 1.6 to 1.9 billion euro deficit, with cuts cited to associations (15%) and family allowances.\n- **The rub:** whether elected members should cut their own pay deeply and permanently as a credibility signal, or whether a 5% trim risks underpaying the job and should align with other parliaments rather than go furthest.", "deep_facts": "- The text is proposal for a decision A-103/1 - 2024/2025, tabled to reduce the indemnities of Brussels MPs by 5%; it was deposited before the parallel Bureau text and is described by its backers as going further (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- A competing text, A-150/1 - 2024/2025, was brought by the president on behalf of the Bureau, introducing a 'retenue temporaire de 5%' (temporary 5% retention) on the parliamentary indemnity from the first payment of the 2025-2026 session; the two were debated jointly but voted separately (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Backers of A-103 argued for two differences from the Bureau text: removing the 'temporary' character (making the cut lasting), and extending the 5% reduction to indemnities for special functions, not just the base indemnity (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- A Brussels MP's pay is described in debate as 'plus de 7.000 euros nets par mois', characterised by one speaker as making them 'les parlementaires les mieux payes de Belgique' (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Special-function indemnities are described as an extra figure of about '2.500 euros supplementaires' per holder; A-103's backers favour abolishing them and at minimum applying the 5% cut to them (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Backers note the Chamber of Representatives and the Flemish Parliament temporarily reduced their members' indemnity to 95% of the base salary of a 'conseiller d'Etat sans anciennete'; the Bureau text aligns Brussels on a 100% base, applying its 5% in that logic (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Backers state that by renouncing their end-of-term departure indemnities in June 2024, their group allowed the Brussels Parliament to save 730.000 euros (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The debate situates the measure within a regional deficit cited at '1,6 a 1,9 milliard d'euros' for 2025 and a consolidated debt that 'avoisinerait les 16,5 milliards d'euros'; cited parallel cuts include a 15% reduction in association budgets and the removal of a 10-euro top-up in family allowances for children born before 1 December 2019 (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- A Bureau majority (cited as MR, Groen, Les Engages, Open Vld, N-VA) had a month earlier tabled a note to modernise the Parliament and cut its spending by 10%, bringing its budget to a figure cited at 62 million euros (from 57-59 million in 2024, having reached 14 twelfths / about 69 million in 2025 with provisional twelfths) (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Already-applied Bureau measures cited include scrapping the chauffeured car for the presidency, removing advantages for honorary MPs, ending staff provision for the outgoing president, and ending phone-cost reimbursement for Bureau members and group chairs (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The PTB's longstanding stated position is to halve MPs' salaries; the 5% cut and the special-functions extension are presented as steps short of that demand (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Several groups (Ecolo, Team Fouad Ahidar, DeFI) voiced support for the substance of A-103 as more coherent or 'linear' than the Bureau text, since many members sit on neither the Bureau nor the enlarged Bureau; others (Groen) signalled they could abstain to respect the broader cross-party accord (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Tied debates flagged for future work: a 2014 rule change letting a single member form (and chair) a group with the attached costs; remunerating special functions on the basis of effective attendance; and a DeFI proposal to cut the number of MPs from 89 to 60 (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference - not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- Two texts on the same 5% let members separate the gesture from its depth: backing the consensus Bureau cut while voting down the 'further' text lets a member claim the headline reduction without touching special-function pay or making it permanent.\n- 'Temporary vs permanent' is the real wedge: a time-limited retention can quietly lapse, so insisting on permanence converts a symbolic signal into a structural pay change - which is precisely why it draws more resistance.\n- Extending the cut to special-function indemnities reallocates pain toward members who hold Bureau and leadership seats, i.e. often the very people negotiating the Bureau consensus - a redistribution within the chamber, not just a cut.\n- Framing pay against a billion-euro deficit and cuts to associations and family allowances builds an equity argument (the chamber should bleed before citizens do); opponents counter that an attractive indemnity keeps the mandate open to non-wealthy candidates and avoids a 'race to the bottom'.\n- Voting against this text is not the same as opposing any cut, since a parallel consensus reduction exists - it can read instead as preferring alignment with other parliaments over going furthest.", "proposed_by": "Francis Dagrin, Oliver Rittweger de Moor, Francoise De Smedt, Jan Busselen (PTB/PVDA)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "rejected", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00031/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"TFA": "for", "PTB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "PS": "against", "MR": "against", "OVLD": "against", "LE": "against", "VB": "against", "VOOR": "against", "NVA": "against", "GRN": "abstain", "CDV": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2425-00031-v13", "date": "2025-07-10", "session_code": "PB_2024-25_00031", "point": 13, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Decision", "topic": "MP pay", "headline": "Introduce a temporary 5% retention on Brussels MPs' base parliamentary indemnity from the first payment of the 2025-2026 session, mirroring the federal Chamber and Flemish Parliament.", "title": "La proposition de decision A-150/1 – 2024/2025, soumise par le Bureau au nom d'un large consensus entre groupes, introduit une retenue temporaire de 5 % sur l'indemnite parlementaire de base des membres du Parlement bruxellois a partir du premier paiement de la session 2025-2026. Elle s'inscrit dans un effort plus large de reduction du budget du Parlement de 10 % et aligne Bruxelles sur la Chambre des representants et le Parlement flamand, qui avaient deja reduit temporairement leurs indemnites a 95 % du traitement de reference.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition de decision A-150/1 – 2024/2025, soumise par le Bureau au nom d'un large consensus entre groupes, introduit une retenue temporaire de 5 % sur l'indemnite parlementaire de base des membres du Parlement bruxellois a partir du premier paiement de la session 2025-2026. Elle s'inscrit dans un effort plus large de reduction du budget du Parlement de 10 % et aligne Bruxelles sur la Chambre des representants et le Parlement flamand, qui avaient deja reduit temporairement leurs indemnites a 95 % du traitement de reference.", "source_brief": "The Brussels Parliament's Bureau — after weeks of negotiations — agreed unanimously to introduce a 5% retention on every MP's base parliamentary indemnity from September 2025, matching the temporary reduction already applied by the federal Chamber and the Flemish Parliament. It is part of a wider package cutting the Parliament's own budget by 10%.\n\n- **What it does:** removes 5% from the base indemnity from the first payment of the 2025-2026 session; the savings go to the Parliament's general running costs; this follows an already-applied series of Bureau measures (no chauffeured car for the president, no phone reimbursement for Bureau members, no benefits for honorary MPs).\n- **The institutional backdrop:** Brussels MPs are cited as the best-paid regional legislators in Belgium at over 7,000 euros net per month; the regional budget runs a deficit of 1.6–1.9 billion euros; the Parliament's own 2025 budget reached 14 provisional twelfths (approx. 69 million euros), up from 57 million in 2024.\n- **The political story:** the opposition (PTB) had been demanding a 5% cut for seven months; the Bureau brought its own version on the same day; the PTB text (A-103) was put to the vote first, then this Bureau text — two near-identical cuts, two separate roll-calls.\n- **The rub:** whether a 5% temporary reduction on base pay — excluding special-function indemnities — is a meaningful gesture of solidarity or a carefully scoped minimum that leaves the best-paid chamber in Belgium largely untouched.", "deep_facts": "- The text is proposition de decision A-150/1 – 2024/2025, tabled by the president on behalf of the Bureau after a unanimous Bureau agreement reached on 9 July 2025, the day before the plenary (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The 5% retention applies to the parliamentary indemnity as defined in the 1989 Council of Brussels-Capital Region decision and its subsequent amendments; the product of the retention is allocated to the Parliament's general expenditure (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The Bureau's framing aligns Brussels on the federal Chamber and Flemish Parliament, both of which temporarily reduced their members' indemnity to 95% of the base salary of a 'conseiller d'Etat sans anciennete'; Brussels MPs' base indemnity had been set at 100% of that reference salary (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Brussels MPs' net monthly indemnity is cited as 'plus de 7.000 euros', described by several speakers as the highest parliamentary pay in Belgium; the Parliament's 2025 budget on provisional twelfths reached approximately 69 million euros, compared to 57–59 million in 2024 (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The wider Bureau savings package — supported by a majority of MR, Groen, Les Engages, Open Vld and N-VA — targets a 10% reduction in Parliament spending, bringing the budget toward 62 million euros; it includes measures already applied: no chauffeured presidential car, no honorary-MP benefits, no phone reimbursements for Bureau members and group chairs, no support for the outgoing president (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- PTB's two amendments to A-150 were voted separately: Amendment 1 (removing the 'temporary' qualifier from the text) was voted and rejected (vote 10); Amendment 2 (applying the 5% cut also to special-function indemnities) was voted and rejected (vote 12); both were defeated before the Bureau text itself came to its final vote (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The competing PTB/PVDA text A-103 — which would have made the cut permanent (definitive) and extended it to special-function indemnities — was put to the vote in the same sitting, before the Bureau text A-150 (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- The regional deficit is cited at 1.6 to 1.9 billion euros for 2025, with a consolidated regional debt described as 'avoisinant les 16,5 milliards d'euros'; cited parallel austerity measures include a 15% cut in association budgets and removal of a 10-euro supplement in family allowances for children born before 1 December 2019 (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- Ecolo and Team Fouad Ahidar stated they considered the PTB text more coherent and linear but indicated they would support the Bureau text; Groen said it was not opposed to A-103 but chose to respect the broader cross-party Bureau accord; cd&v, not represented on the Bureau, also backed the 5% cut while regretting it did not extend to supplementary indemnities (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).\n- DeFI noted it is not represented on the Bureau, raised the question of the 2014 rule change allowing a single member to constitute a group and draw group-chair special-function pay, and called for a wider debate on the financing of parliamentary activity (CRI, 10 Jul 2025).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- The sequence — reject A-103, then pass A-150 unanimously — is the cleanest illustration of consensus politics: the majority uses the opposition's own language (5% cut) while stripping out the elements it finds inconvenient (permanence, special functions), then claims the moral credit for unanimity.\n- 'Temporary' is doing significant political work here: it leaves the door open for the cut to quietly expire, or for a later interparlementary negotiation to reset the baseline — a flexibility the Bureau majority clearly values more than the PTB's structural permanence.\n- The exclusion of special-function indemnities is a material choice: it protects the pay of Bureau members and group chairs — precisely the people who negotiated the deal — while presenting the cut as universal.\n- The unanimous 79–0 final vote after a heated debate is not consensus on substance; it is consensus on the symbol — every party can tell voters it supported a parliamentary pay cut, while the disagreement about its scope disappears from the record.", "proposed_by": "Bertin Mampaka Mankamba (president of the Parliament, on behalf of the Bureau — broad cross-party consensus: MR, Groen, Les Engagés, Open Vld, N-VA and others)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2024-25/00031/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00014-A-29", "date": "2026-02-06", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00014", "point": 8, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Women's health", "headline": "Have the Brussels Region formally recognise gynaecological and obstetric violence and require official data-gathering, training for health staff, patient information, and a clear route to lodge complaints.", "title": "La proposition vise à faire reconnaître par la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale les violences gynécologiques et obstétricales et à mettre en place la collecte de données, la sensibilisation du personnel soignant, l'information des patientes et un dispositif de plainte. Des amendements ajoutent la mise à disposition et le financement d'interprètes en langue des signes pour les femmes sourdes ainsi que des outils d'information accessibles.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition vise à faire reconnaître par la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale les violences gynécologiques et obstétricales et à mettre en place la collecte de données, la sensibilisation du personnel soignant, l'information des patientes et un dispositif de plainte. Des amendements ajoutent la mise à disposition et le financement d'interprètes en langue des signes pour les femmes sourdes ainsi que des outils d'information accessibles.", "source_brief": "Belgium keeps no official national figures on gynaecological and obstetric violence — no public database, no structured reporting system — so the scale is known only from citizen surveys. This resolution would have the Brussels Region put a name to the problem and act on it: collect data, train health staff, inform patients of their rights, and open a clear path to complain. It covers acts such as examinations or procedures done without consent or explanation, pressure during labour, and the so-called \"husband stitch\". Late additions ask hospitals to provide sign-language interpreters for deaf women and to fund that interpretation so cost is no barrier.\n\n- **What it asks for** — formal recognition by the Region, a data-collection mechanism, staff training, patient-rights information, and an accessible complaints route.\n- **Why now** — a citizen-platform survey put a number on something the official statistics ignore, and a Senate report flagged the missing consolidated data.\n- **Disability angle** — added asks oblige health establishments to supply French- and Flemish-sign-language interpreters across prenatal, birth and postnatal care, financed so it doesn't fall on the patient.\n- **The rub:** supporters say a region that names the problem and counts it can finally tackle it; opponents argue maternal care and hospital norms sit largely with the federal and Community levels, so a Brussels resolution adds paperwork to already-stretched medical staff without real power to act.", "deep_facts": "- The resolution concerns gynaecological and obstetric violence: acts experienced as abusive or non-consented during gynaecological care, childbirth and perinatal care, including procedures without effective anaesthesia, abdominal (fundal) pressure, episiotomy, and the \"husband stitch\" (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; doc A-29).\n- Stated context: Belgium has no specific official national statistics on these violences, no public database and no structured reporting system; the Senate report flags missing consolidated figures and data-collection mechanisms (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).\n- Cited survey figures (estimated, from a citizen platform for respected birth / \"naissance respectée\"): one woman in five reports such violence during childbirth or perinatal care; one woman in three among racialised women (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).\n- A second 2021 study cited yields a figure of two women in five when verbal, physical and psychological violence are added to the \"à vif\" acts, abdominal pressure and husband stitch (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).\n- The operative text (the committee's text) seeks, as its first request, recognition of these violences by the Brussels-Capital Region, plus data collection, staff training/awareness, patient information and a complaints route (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; doc A-29).\n- Two amendments tabled in plenary (am. 1, two new tirets; am. 2, one new tiret) add: requiring health establishments to make available French Belgian (LSFB) and Flemish (VGT) sign-language interpreters at all contacts with deaf women — notably prenatal consultations, delivery and postnatal follow-up — via complementary non-organic hospital norms; financing sign-language interpretation so its cost is not a barrier to gynaecological/obstetric care; and promoting accessible information and awareness tools in sign language on patients' rights, forms of violence and available support, plus improved staff training on disability specifics (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; am. 1 and am. 2, A-29).\n- Levels-of-power references: a federal interfederal working group on follow-up of professional recommendations was foreseen under the National Action Plan against gender-based violence 2021–2025, supervised by the Institute for the Equality of Women and Men, with the federated entities represented; the proposers also cite a parallel training proposal lodged in the Federation Wallonia-Brussels based on Senate recommendations (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- **What it enables:** a regional act of naming plus a data mandate — once a phenomenon is officially counted, it becomes governable (budgets, norms, follow-up questions). The first ask, \"recognition,\" is the lever: it converts a contested experience into an administrative fact the Region must then track.\n- **Second-order effects:** the sign-language amendments quietly create a financing obligation (\"interpretation cost is not a barrier\") and reach into hospital norms — a concrete, costed lever bolted onto an otherwise declaratory text.\n- **The wedge:** the competence question. Much of maternal care and hospital norms is federal/Community, so the fight is less about whether the violence exists than about whether a Brussels resolution is the right instrument or symbolic overreach that loads administration onto clinicians. The proposers pre-empt this by stressing \"normes hospitalières\" the Region can touch and the precedent of guaranteeing IVG access in Brussels hospitals.", "proposed_by": "Ecolo, PS, PTB, DéFI (De Ré, Stoops, Tahar, De Smedt, El Yousfi, Cruysmans, Mandaila)", "doc_ref": "A-29", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00014/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "PTB": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for", "MR": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00014-A-192", "date": "2026-02-06", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00014", "point": 9, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Gender violence", "headline": "Encourage all nineteen Brussels communes to declare themselves \"safe spaces for women\" by adopting charters and proactive policies against sexist and sexual violence in local political life.", "title": "La proposition invite les dix-neuf communes bruxelloises à se déclarer « lieux sûrs pour les femmes » et à lutter contre les violences sexistes et sexuelles dans l'exercice des fonctions politiques locales, en s'inspirant d'une charte adoptée à Schaerbeek. Le plan s'articule autour de six axes : chartes et politiques proactives, formation et sensibilisation, procédures de signalement claires et indépendantes, prévention, suivi démocratique et intégration transversale de la dimension de genre.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition invite les dix-neuf communes bruxelloises à se déclarer « lieux sûrs pour les femmes » et à lutter contre les violences sexistes et sexuelles dans l'exercice des fonctions politiques locales, en s'inspirant d'une charte adoptée à Schaerbeek. Le plan s'articule autour de six axes : chartes et politiques proactives, formation et sensibilisation, procédures de signalement claires et indépendantes, prévention, suivi démocratique et intégration transversale de la dimension de genre.", "source_brief": "Sexist and sexual violence reaches into political life: one survey found 18% of women elected officials had faced sexual harassment during their mandate, and in 2022 more than 120 Belgian women politicians signed an open letter denouncing it inside the institutions. This resolution would push all nineteen Brussels communes to declare themselves \"safe spaces for women\" — modelled on a charter first adopted in Schaerbeek — and to build out a six-part plan around it. The text is a regional resolution encouraging the communes; it does not bind them.\n\n- **What it does** — invites every commune to adopt a charter and proactive policy, train and raise awareness, set up clear independent reporting procedures, act in prevention, ensure democratic follow-up, and mainstream gender across the plan.\n- **Where it comes from** — a charter adopted in Schaerbeek, presented as the first Belgian commune to commit to one, scaled up to the whole Region.\n- **The rub:** supporters call it a low-cost way to spread a proven good practice to the level closest to citizens; sceptics note it is a non-binding nudge to communes over their own internal conduct, with the substantive legal protections (Istanbul Convention, the 2023 femicide law) already existing — so the question is whether a regional charter changes behaviour or mainly signals.", "deep_facts": "- The resolution invites the nineteen Brussels communes to declare themselves \"safe spaces for women\" (\"lieux sûrs pour les femmes\") and to combat sexist and sexual violence in the exercise of local political functions (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; doc A-192).\n- It is presented as inspired by a motion adopted unanimously in Schaerbeek, cited as the first Belgian commune to adopt a charter of commitment and actions against sexist and sexual violence in politics; the aim stated is to extend those good practices to all communes (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).\n- The plan is articulated around six axes (CRI, 6 Feb 2026): (1) create safe spaces for women by encouraging all communes to adopt charters and proactive policies and to exchange good practices; (2) increase training and awareness; (3) put in place clear, independent and protective reporting procedures; (4) act in prevention; (5) ensure democratic follow-up; (6) mainstream the gender dimension transversally across the plan.\n- Cited figures (estimated/surveyed): a 2018 survey found 18% of women elected officials reported sexual harassment during their mandate and 34% outside it; in 2022 more than 120 Belgian women politicians across political families signed an open letter denouncing sexist behaviour and sexual or psychological harassment within the institutions (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).\n- Existing legal framework referenced (not created by this text): the Istanbul Convention, a Council of Europe resolution, and the 2023 law on prevention of and combating femicides, gender-based homicides and violence; the proposers state the gap is operational implementation at local level, not the absence of legislation (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).\n- Process note: amendments originating from PS, MR and N-VA were folded into the text (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; doc A-192).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- **What it enables:** a regional template that communes can adopt off the shelf, lowering the cost of acting and creating peer pressure (a commune that declines a \"safe space\" charter has to explain why). The instrument is soft — \"encourage,\" not \"oblige\" — so its bite depends entirely on uptake.\n- **Second-order effects:** the \"clear, independent reporting procedures\" axis is the operative one — it points at internal complaint channels for elected officials and local staff, the part with real institutional consequences; the rest (charters, awareness, mainstreaming) is largely declaratory.\n- **The tension:** competence and scope. The proposers concede the legal protections already exist federally/internationally and frame the value as \"operationalising\" them locally — which is also the line of attack: a non-binding regional resolution about communes' own conduct can be read as signalling rather than change. The cross-party amendment uptake suggests a deliberate consensus play (broad sign-on lowers the cost of voting for it).", "proposed_by": "Ecolo, PS, DéFI (Tahar, Khattabi, De Ré, Luhahi, Vidal, Agic, Mandaila)", "doc_ref": "A-192", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00014/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00014-A-145", "date": "2026-02-06", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00014", "point": 36, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Unemployment policy", "headline": "Reform Actiris's jobseeker availability-check procedure: abolish the three-evaluator college, check 100% of jobseekers' availability (instead of today's partial coverage), and extend from two to four months the deadline for Actiris to offer training, an internship or a job.", "title": "La proposition vise à réformer la procédure de contrôle de la disponibilité des chercheurs d'emploi à Actiris : supprimer le collège d'évaluation composé de trois évaluateurs, réaffecter le personnel ainsi libéré, atteindre un taux de couverture de 100 % des chercheurs d'emploi et contrôler leur disponibilité deux fois (à l'analyse du dossier puis, en cas d'évaluation négative, lors d'un entretien). Le texte prévoit un plan d'action individuel dans les 30 jours et porte de deux à quatre mois le délai dans lequel Actiris doit proposer une formation, un stage d'insertion ou une offre d'emploi. Le texte soumis au vote a été amendé en séance, des amendements concurrents en modifiant la portée.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition vise à réformer la procédure de contrôle de la disponibilité des chercheurs d'emploi à Actiris : supprimer le collège d'évaluation composé de trois évaluateurs, réaffecter le personnel ainsi libéré, atteindre un taux de couverture de 100 % des chercheurs d'emploi et contrôler leur disponibilité deux fois (à l'analyse du dossier puis, en cas d'évaluation négative, lors d'un entretien). Le texte prévoit un plan d'action individuel dans les 30 jours et porte de deux à quatre mois le délai dans lequel Actiris doit proposer une formation, un stage d'insertion ou une offre d'emploi. Le texte soumis au vote a été amendé en séance, des amendements concurrents en modifiant la portée.", "source_brief": "Today only about 20% of Brussels jobseekers get an accompaniment, and a college of three evaluators sits in the availability-control chain. This resolution reworks that procedure at Actiris: scrap the three-evaluator college, redeploy those staff to counselling, and aim to check the availability of 100% of jobseekers — once when the file is analysed, and again in an interview if that first check is negative. It also stretches from two to four months the window in which Actiris must offer training, an internship or a job, and sets a 30-day target for an individual action plan. The text put to the vote was reworked in session, with competing amendments pulling it in different directions.\n\n- **What changes** — the evaluator college goes; freed-up staff move to counselling; availability is checked for everyone, twice if needed; the offer deadline goes from two to four months; an individual action plan within 30 days.\n- **The numbers behind it** — ~92,000 jobseekers, ~21,000 vacancies in the Actiris database; ten jobseekers for every available low-skilled job; Brussels unemployment 21.2% (2014) to 15.4% (2025).\n- **The fight over the amendments** — one bloc frames the redeployed staff as straight \"control\" of availability; a competing bloc would instead fold availability assessment into a capabilities-based accompaniment, bar any unfavourable decision without a real, adapted job-offer first, and build in procedural safeguards and appeals.\n- **The rub:** is checking everyone's availability a fairer \"nudge\" that frees staff to actually help — or does tightening control do little when there aren't enough jobs (ten seekers per low-skilled post), risking sanctions and transfers to the CPAS without creating work?", "deep_facts": "- The resolution reforms the procedure for controlling jobseekers' availability at Actiris (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; doc A-145). It was discussed jointly with related texts A-162, A-165 and A-168.\n- Core operative asks (as argued by the proposers and reflected in the cosigned amendments n° 1–4): abolish the college of three evaluators; redeploy the freed human resources to jobseeker evaluation/counselling; reach a coverage target of 100% of jobseekers; check availability twice — first at file analysis and, if the assessment is negative (\"suite à l'analyse sur dossier\"), a second time in an interview; an individual action plan within 30 days; and, by amending the seventh tiret, replace \"two\" with \"four\" months as the deadline within which Actiris must propose a training, an insertion internship or a job offer — aligning Brussels with the Walloon Region (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; am. n° 1, 2, 3, 4, A-145).\n- Amendment n° 3 also changes the competence-balance (bilan de compétences) wording and replaces the figure \"15\" with \"30\" (days) for the individual action plan (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; am. n° 3).\n- A competing set of amendments (n° 5–17, tabled by Tahar/Laanan and, for several, Luhahi) would instead: integrate active-availability assessment into the accompaniment process via a common methodology validated by Actiris's management committee, based on a \"capabilities\" approach; redeploy agents from active-availability control to employment-counsellor roles while keeping a specialised passive-availability service (about 8 FTE) and the dispensations cell; set up a methodological-support mechanism of referents (including former evaluators) to ensure consistency and validate signalements before transmission to passive availability; bar any unfavourable active-availability decision without a real, adapted, duly-notified accompaniment offer; add procedural safeguards (motivation in fact and law, reasonable deadlines, accessible information, right to assistance, effective internal appeals); ensure continuity for jobseekers reaching end-of-rights in liaison with the CPAS; and extend an evaluation reporting period from one year to two with named indicators (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; am. n° 5–17, A-145).\n- Cited figures: about 92,000 jobseekers and 21,000 job offers in the Actiris database; a BECI study reports 100,000 vacancies; roughly ten jobseekers per available low-skilled job; only about 20% of jobseekers currently benefit from accompaniment under the current procedure (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).\n- Cited labour-market figures (stated): Brussels employment rate 54% (2014) rising to 64% (2025); unemployment rate 21.2% (2014) falling to 15.4% (2025); comparison cities — Antwerp 14.9%, Liège 23.3%, Charleroi 22.8%; youth unemployment cited at about 25% (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).\n- Procedural references: the current availability-control device is organised by the arrêté of 16 February 2017; the proposers cite a recent Brupartners opinion and federal measures pushing a more demanding follow-up; the competing bloc references articles 51 to 53bis of the royal decree of 25 November 1991 for passive-availability handling (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- **What it enables:** removing the evaluator college and pushing toward 100% coverage reframes availability control from a specialised gatekeeping body into a routine, universal check folded into counselling — which can be read as \"more help\" (more agents counselling) or \"more control\" (everyone screened), and the text is deliberately ambiguous between the two.\n- **The wedge that defines the vote:** two rival visions sit on the same skeleton. One bloc keeps the language of control (check whether you found a job / attended an interview) and budget-neutral redeployment; the rival bloc rewires the same machinery toward a capabilities approach, makes any unfavourable decision conditional on a genuine prior offer, and adds appeals and safeguards. A voter's instinct on \"control vs. support\" is the real fault line, not the abolition of the college, which both broadly accept.\n- **Second-order effects:** stretching the offer deadline from two to four months loosens the timer in the jobseeker's favour but also delays the point at which a negative assessment can bite; the \"no unfavourable decision without a real offer\" amendment, if it lands, shifts the burden onto Actiris to have delivered first — a structurally different regime from a pure coverage target.\n- **Tension:** the macro argument (ten seekers per low-skilled job; ~92,000 seekers vs ~21,000 listed vacancies) cuts against the premise that tighter checking yields more hires — raising whether the reform mainly accelerates transfers to the CPAS rather than to work.", "proposed_by": "DéFI, Anders (de Patoul, Belguenani); in-session amendments from MR and CD&V, with competing amendments from PS and Ecolo", "doc_ref": "A-145", "outcome": "rejected", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00014/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"DEFI": "against", "PS": "against", "TFA": "against", "MR": "against", "PTB": "against", "OVLD": "against", "LE": "against", "CDV": "against", "Ecolo": "against", "GRN": "against", "VB": "against", "VOOR": "against", "NVA": "against"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00014-A-236", "date": "2026-02-06", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00014", "point": 42, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Foreign affairs", "headline": "Have the Brussels Parliament call on the federal government to condemn the Iranian regime's repression, support the Iranian people's aspirations, protect dissidents and journalists, and break off diplomatic normalisation with the Islamic Republic of Iran.", "title": "La proposition demande au gouvernement fédéral et au ministre des Affaires étrangères de condamner les exactions du régime théocratique autoritaire iranien, de soutenir les aspirations légitimes du peuple iranien à la liberté et à l'autodétermination, de soutenir le droit à la communication et la levée du blocus numérique, de garantir une protection internationale renforcée aux dissidents, journalistes, avocats et activistes, et de rompre toute normalisation diplomatique avec la République islamique d'Iran. Des amendements ajoutent un appel à éviter l'escalade militaire et l'expression de la solidarité avec la population iranienne.", "raw_outcome": "La proposition demande au gouvernement fédéral et au ministre des Affaires étrangères de condamner les exactions du régime théocratique autoritaire iranien, de soutenir les aspirations légitimes du peuple iranien à la liberté et à l'autodétermination, de soutenir le droit à la communication et la levée du blocus numérique, de garantir une protection internationale renforcée aux dissidents, journalistes, avocats et activistes, et de rompre toute normalisation diplomatique avec la République islamique d'Iran. Des amendements ajoutent un appel à éviter l'escalade militaire et l'expression de la solidarité avec la population iranienne.", "source_brief": "Since 28 December 2025, Iran has violently repressed mass peaceful protests that grew out of an economic crisis into a challenge to the regime. This resolution has the Brussels Parliament address the federal government and the foreign-affairs minister: condemn the theocratic regime's abuses, back the Iranian people's aspirations to freedom and self-determination, support the right to information and lifting the digital blackout, guarantee reinforced international protection for dissidents, journalists, lawyers and binational activists, and end diplomatic normalisation with Iran. It is a position statement, addressed to the federal level, on a matter where the Region's international powers are limited.\n\n- **What it asks** — federal condemnation of the regime, support for the protesters' aspirations and for communication rights, protection for dissidents and journalists, and a break in diplomatic normalisation.\n- **Where it points** — to the federal government and foreign-affairs minister; it cites the EU Council's 29 January 2026 listing of the Revolutionary Guards as a terrorist organisation and a European Parliament resolution of 22 January 2026.\n- **The rub:** is a regional parliament right to take a clear, hard line (condemn, protect, break ties) on an international crisis where Brussels has real symbolic weight — or do automatic sanctions and a diplomatic rupture risk adding to the population's suffering, the line that drives the debate over softer, dialogue-first wording?", "deep_facts": "- The resolution asks the federal government (and the minister of foreign affairs) to: condemn the abuses of Iran's authoritarian theocratic regime against its population; support the Iranian people's legitimate aspirations to freedom of expression and to democratically decide their future; support the right to communication and information and favour lifting the digital blackout; guarantee reinforced international protection to dissidents, journalists, lawyers and binational activists; and break off diplomatic normalisation with the Islamic Republic of Iran, preventing the regime from harming Belgium and the EU by all legal, diplomatic and financial means (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; doc A-236).\n- Trigger stated: the repression of peaceful protests since 28 December 2025, which began amid an economic crisis (collapse of the rial) and turned into contestation of the theocratic regime; the regime has been in place since 1979 (cited as 47 years) (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).\n- Cited figures (stated in debate, attributed to speakers): a population of about 90–92 million; claims of \"more than 30,000\" dead and of executions \"in the tens of thousands\" (figures presented as not yet precisely established); particularly intense repression cited on 8, 9 and 10 January; a cited STEM female-graduate rate approaching 70% (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).\n- External decisions referenced (not enacted by this text): the European Council decision of 29 January 2026 listing the Revolutionary Guards as a terrorist organisation; a European Parliament resolution of 22 January 2026 on the repression; condemnations by UN bodies including the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).\n- The base text put to the vote comprised considerants 1 to 21 and points 1 to 4 of the dispositif (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; doc A-236).\n- Two amendments tabled in plenary (am. n° 1 and n° 2, by Parga Vega) would add: a new point calling on all parties to the conflict to avoid military escalation and seek a peaceful solution through diplomatic dialogue in respect of international law; and a new point expressing solidarity with the Iranian population suffering from the economic crisis, political repression, executions and arbitrary state violence, as well as from international sanctions and geopolitical tensions (CRI, 6 Feb 2026; am. n° 1 and n° 2, A-236).\n- Competence note stated: the Brussels-Capital Region has international powers limited to its areas of autonomy; the proposers stress Brussels as host to EU and NATO institutions (CRI, 6 Feb 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- **What it enables:** a sub-national parliament adding its voice to a federal/EU foreign-policy track it cannot itself execute — the asks are addressed upward (to the federal government and foreign minister), so the resolution's force is symbolic and agenda-setting, leveraging Brussels' status as a diplomatic capital.\n- **The wedge:** \"hard line vs. de-escalation.\" The base text leans toward condemnation, protection and a diplomatic break; the tabled amendments would graft on a call to avoid military escalation and a solidarity point that explicitly names international sanctions and geopolitical tensions among the population's burdens — a softer, sanctions-sceptical framing. The vote is partly about how punitive vs. how dialogue-first the message should be.\n- **Second-order tension:** the proposers' own \"break all diplomatic normalisation … by all financial means\" sits in tension with the amendment-line worry that sanctions add suffering to suffering — the same humanitarian concern pulls in opposite operational directions.\n- **Read on motive:** broad cross-party sponsorship signals a consensus-symbolism play; the contested amendments are where genuine values divergence (interventionist condemnation vs. anti-sanction caution) surfaces.", "proposed_by": "PS, DéFI, Ecolo, Team Fouad Ahidar (Aït Baala, Ghyssels, Ikazban, Maison, Khattabi, Ahidar)", "doc_ref": "A-236", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00014/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "PTB": "abstain", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for", "OVLD": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00019-v1", "date": "2026-02-27", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00019", "point": 1, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Motion of confidence", "topic": "Government confidence", "headline": "Grant the newly sworn-in Brussels regional government Parliament's confidence so it can take office and govern for the rest of the term.", "title": "L'ordre du jour appelle le vote nominatif sur la motion de confiance déposée par le gouvernement à l'issue de sa déclaration. Après plus de six cents jours, les Bruxellois ont enfin un gouvernement. Quelqu'un demande-t-il la parole pour une explication de vote ?", "raw_outcome": "L'ordre du jour appelle le vote nominatif sur la motion de confiance déposée par le gouvernement à l'issue de sa déclaration. Après plus de six cents jours, les Bruxellois ont enfin un gouvernement. Quelqu'un demande-t-il la parole pour une explication de vote ?", "source_brief": "After what the chair counted as more than 600 days — 614 by some tallies — without a formed government since the June 2024 election, a coalition finally took the oath before the King and is now asking Parliament to confirm it in office. The motion is the formal trigger: backing it installs this team for the roughly three years left in the term; withholding leaves Brussels without a full-powers executive.\n\n- **What a yes actually does** — invests *this* government and ends the longest formation deadlock in the Region's history, letting it leave the provisional-twelfths budget regime behind.\n- **The 24-page declaration** — the policy text runs 24 pages including the cover, and the GGC/Cocom budget is not slated for a vote for another three months, so confidence is granted before the spending plan is seen.\n- **A heavy ledger comes with it** — a hiring freeze, lower discretionary subsidies for associations, no STIB fleet renewal, tighter housing investment, and higher water and STIB tariffs plus a planned car vignette.\n- **The rub:** do you hand a government a vote of confidence on a thin, still-unbudgeted declaration just to end the paralysis — or refuse to write what critics call a blank cheque after 600 days of crisis?", "deep_facts": "- The vote applies article 120 of Parliament's rules; the declaration was read on Monday 23 February 2026 and the confidence vote held Friday 27 February 2026 (CRI n°19, p.6).\n- The chair stated Brussels had been without a formed government for 'more than six hundred days — six hundred and fourteen by some counters' since the June 2024 election (CRI n°19, p.6).\n- The government was legally constituted before the vote: oath taken before the King, portfolios distributed, ministers and state secretaries appointed (Verstraeten, N-VA, p.10).\n- The policy declaration runs 24 pages including the cover (de Patoul, DéFI, p.9; De Brabandere, Vlaams Belang, p.10).\n- The Cocom/GGC budget is to be submitted to a vote only in roughly three months (Khattabi, Ecolo, p.6).\n- Measures cited from the declaration: a moratorium on public-sector hiring; reduced facultative (discretionary) subsidies for associations; status quo on STIB service with ageing rolling stock not replaced; disinvestment in public housing; tariff rises including water and STIB; a planned car vignette (De Smedt, PTB, pp.8-9).\n- PTB framed it as 'one billion less' in the regional budget — '80% cuts and 20% drawn from workers' pockets' (PTB, p.9).\n- Following the vote the chair fixed the attributions and naming of eight permanent commissions under article 23.1 (CRI n°19, p.11): Environment; Spatial Planning; Finance and General Affairs; Housing; Internal Affairs; Mobility; Economic Affairs; Equal Opportunities and Women's Rights.\n- Several opposition speakers (DéFI, N-VA, Vlaams Belang) criticised the absence of an electoral-system reform from the declaration, citing it as a cause of the 614-day blockage (de Patoul, p.9).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- Investiture votes are near-formalities once a coalition is sworn in; the real content here is the speeches, which preview the term's battle lines (austerity, Good Move/mobility, the unvoted GGC budget) rather than the office question itself.\n- Sequencing the vote *before* the GGC budget — three months out — front-loads the political risk onto critics: a yes endorses spending nobody has yet seen, which is precisely the 'blank cheque' charge being lodged.\n- The opposition's repeated framing — 'what are we even voting on?', 'a government on paper' — is an attempt to delegitimise the mandate rather than contest a policy, a posture that costs little and positions them for 2029.\n- Pairing the vote with the immediate re-fixing of eight commissions signals the machinery is being restarted at speed after the provisional-twelfths limbo.", "proposed_by": "The Brussels regional government (minister-president Boris Dilliès), tabled at the close of its policy declaration of 23 February 2026; majority of PS, MR, Open VLD/Anders., Les Engagés, Groen, cd&v and Vooruit.brussels.", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00019/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "PTB": "against", "VOOR": "for", "DEFI": "against", "TFA": "against", "VB": "against", "Ecolo": "against", "NVA": "against"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00020-A-215", "date": "2026-02-27", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00020", "point": 2, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Resolution", "topic": "Sudan ceasefire", "headline": "Call for an immediate and permanent ceasefire in Darfur and Sudan and for strengthening and securing humanitarian aid and arms-transfer controls in the conflict.", "title": "Notre proposition de résolution appelle à un cessez-le-feu immédiat et permanent. Elle exige un accès humanitaire sûr et sans entraves, soutient la lutte contre l'impunité et rappelle la nécessité d'un contrôle strict des transferts d'armes, afin que nos politiques économiques ne puissent alimenter directement ou indirectement les logiques miliciennes.", "raw_outcome": "Notre proposition de résolution appelle à un cessez-le-feu immédiat et permanent. Elle exige un accès humanitaire sûr et sans entraves, soutient la lutte contre l'impunité et rappelle la nécessité d'un contrôle strict des transferts d'armes, afin que nos politiques économiques ne puissent alimenter directement ou indirectement les logiques miliciennes.", "source_brief": "Brussels has no formal power over foreign policy, so this resolution is a political statement: it asks the Region to put its voice — as a host of the EU capital — behind an immediate, permanent ceasefire in the Sudan war and to press for safe, unobstructed humanitarian access. The text also calls for tight control of arms transfers and says the Region's own economic policy must not, directly or indirectly, feed the militias.\n\n- **What it commits to** — a ceasefire demand, secured humanitarian corridors, support for international-justice mechanisms, and a call on the federal foreign minister to act.\n- **The scale invoked** — speakers cite up to ~25 million people facing hunger, ~21 million in acute food insecurity, civilian deaths more than doubling in 2025, and ~75% of health facilities no longer functioning.\n- **The Region's one real lever** — arms-export licensing: speakers argue Brussels must block any weapon exports usable to commit war crimes, and extend the embargo now limited to Darfur.\n- **The rub:** does a regional parliament with no foreign-affairs competence add weight by speaking out — or is a symbolic text that 'won't end the war' a distraction, especially when some argue it stays silent on European and Belgian responsibility for the crisis?", "deep_facts": "- The conflict is dated to April 2023, opposing the Sudanese Armed Forces and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF); described as one of the world's gravest humanitarian crises (Diallo, Les Engagés, p.15).\n- Figures cited in debate: nearly 25 million people suffering hunger and nearly 75% of health structures no longer functioning (Ahidar, p.19); about 21 million people in acute food insecurity (Maison, DéFI, p.20); 15 million children needing urgent humanitarian aid (Parga Vega, PTB, p.23); civilian deaths reported to have more than doubled during 2025, per the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights before the Human Rights Council in Geneva (Maison, p.20).\n- Documented violence cited: Al-Geneïna and El-Facher/Al-Fashir besieged, bombed and starved; hospitals destroyed; sexual violence used as a weapon of terror (against women and girls, and noted also against boys and men by Groothedde, Groen, p.22); drone strikes on vital infrastructure intensifying since the start of 2026 (Maison, p.20).\n- Belgian/federal actions referenced: foreign minister Maxime Prévot announced in August 2025 a release of €5 million to the UN Humanitarian Fund for Sudan, Belgium's first contribution to that fund; a Belgian contribution of US$20 million to the Central Emergency Response Fund (CERF); Belgium co-signed on 18-19 February 2026 a joint declaration condemning attacks on civilians and hospitals in Kordofan and Darfur (Diallo, pp.15-16).\n- Operative asks of the text: immediate and permanent ceasefire; safe, unobstructed humanitarian access; support for international-justice mechanisms and the fight against impunity; strict control of arms transfers; and that the Region's economic policy not arm the militias (Diallo, p.15).\n- The arms-export point invokes Amnesty International secretary-general Agnès Callamard urging expansion of the Darfur arms embargo to the rest of the country; the Region holds competence over arms trade (Groothedde, Groen, pp.22-23).\n- The text emerged from a cross-group working group that integrated amendments from nearly all groups (Ikazban, PS, p.16-17).\n- PTB/PVDA stated reservations that the text is superficial on the deep causes and on European and Belgian responsibility (citing the 2014 Khartoum Process and a 2017 collaboration with the al-Bashir regime to return refugees), while indicating support out of solidarity (Parga Vega, PTB, pp.23-24).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The Region candidly lacks foreign-policy competence, so the resolution's real function is reputational and signalling — speakers explicitly tie it to 'restoring Brussels' image' and to consistency with prior texts on Iran and Gaza.\n- The single binding lever is arms-export licensing, a genuine regional competence; the rest is an appeal to the federal minister, which makes the text strong on symbolism and weak on enforcement.\n- Building it through a cross-party working group with near-unanimous amendments is designed to maximise moral weight and pre-empt the 'why is a regional parliament doing foreign policy?' objection raised by N-VA-adjacent critics.\n- The PTB/PVDA stance — backing the text while attacking it for ignoring European/Belgian culpability — lets them claim solidarity without endorsing what they read as a sanitised account of the war's causes.", "proposed_by": "Elhadj Moussa Diallo (Les Engagés, lead author), Jamal Ikazban (PS), Benjamin Dalle (cd&v), Gladys Kazadi, Alain Deneef (Les Engagés), Mounir Laarissi, Fouad Ahidar, Gisèle Mandaila; later co-signed by Zakia Khattabi (Ecolo). Resolution A-215.", "doc_ref": "A-215", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00020/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "DEFI": "for", "CDV": "for", "VB": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for", "OVLD": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00021-A-264", "date": "2026-03-13", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00021", "point": 1, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Cooperation agreement", "topic": "Litter, polluter-pays", "headline": "Give assent to an interregional cooperation agreement making producers of packaging, tobacco, wipes and similar products fund the waste-management and street-cleaning costs their products create.", "title": "Le projet d'ordonnance concrétise le principe du pollueur-payeur : les producteurs d'emballages, de produits de tabac, de lingettes, etc., devront assumer le financement de la gestion des déchets issus de leurs produits ainsi que les coûts liés à la propreté publique. Cet accord permettra à la Région de percevoir des recettes estimées à 50 millions d'euros par an, dont 54 % alloués aux communes pour le volet des déchets sauvages.", "raw_outcome": "Le projet d'ordonnance concrétise le principe du pollueur-payeur : les producteurs d'emballages, de produits de tabac, de lingettes, etc., devront assumer le financement de la gestion des déchets issus de leurs produits ainsi que les coûts liés à la propreté publique. Cet accord permettra à la Région de percevoir des recettes estimées à 50 millions d'euros par an, dont 54 % alloués aux communes pour le volet des déchets sauvages.", "source_brief": "Today, when a discarded cigarette butt, wipe or food wrapper is cleaned off a Brussels street, the public pays. This agreement flips that under the polluter-pays principle: makers of packaging, tobacco, wipes, balloons, chewing gum and textiles would have to reimburse the cost of managing their products' waste and of keeping public space clean — money the Region estimates at about €50 million a year, with 54% of the litter share going to the 19 municipalities and 46% kept by the Region.\n\n- **Why now** — it transposes the EU Single-Use Plastics (SUP) directive; speakers warned that not adopting it risks EU financial penalties and loses another year of revenue.\n- **The model chosen** — a 'financial' model: producers reimburse public bodies rather than run the cleaning themselves, which proponents say rules out privatising street-cleaning.\n- **The long delay's price tag** — one speaker estimated Belgium's institutional slowness let industry save roughly €465 million at the public's expense; new revenue is earmarked for the regional waste prevention/sorting/recycling fund.\n- **The rub:** does forcing producers to pay finally relieve municipal budgets and your local taxes — or, as sceptics warn, will firms simply pass the new charge on to households and small shops while big online platforms slip through?", "deep_facts": "- The text gives assent to the interregional cooperation agreement of 9 February 2026 on Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR/REP) for certain waste streams and litter ('déchets sauvages'), and amends the ordinance of 12 December 1991 creating budgetary funds (CRI n°21, p.18; A-264).\n- It is the outcome of five years of negotiation between the federated entities; presented by secretary of state Ans Persoons (rapporteur Laarissi, p.19).\n- Estimated new revenue for the Region: ~€50 million per year (rapporteur, p.19).\n- Litter-share split: 54% to the municipalities, 46% to the Region (rapporteur, pp.18-19); the exact inter-municipal distribution key is to be set by a government decree still in preparation (Persoons, p.20).\n- Mechanism: a 'financial' model — producers reimburse costs borne by public bodies, which proponents say avoids privatising public cleaning (rapporteur, p.19).\n- Revenue is assigned to the Fund for the prevention, sorting, reuse, recycling and valorisation of waste, which already collects the incineration-tax receipts (rapporteur, p.20).\n- Producers covered include packaging, tobacco products, wipes, balloons, chewing gum and the textile sector (rapporteur/de Patoul, pp.18-21).\n- Chewing gum is not covered by the EU SUP directive, so a flat-rate ('forfaitaire') contribution was decided for it, with a budget of €3.5 million cited and an evaluation within two years (de Patoul / Persoons, pp.20-21).\n- Cost comparison cited: clearing illegal dumping/litter costs ~€51 million in Brussels versus ~€71 million in Flanders — about four times more per inhabitant, attributed by the state secretary to population density and tourist influx (Vanden Borre, N-VA / Persoons, pp.20-21).\n- DéFI estimated Belgium's institutional slowness let industry save ~€465 million at the collectivity's expense (de Patoul, p.21).\n- Driver: transposition of the EU Single-Use Plastics (SUP) directive; the text was treated as urgent to avoid EU financial penalties for SUP infringements and to avoid losing another year of revenue (rapporteur, p.19).\n- Online commerce: foreign platforms are to be made responsible via a mandatary ('mandataire') system, obliging them to assume EPR if their sellers do not (Persoons, p.20).\n- A deposit-return scheme ('consigne'/statiegeld) on cans is neither imposed nor excluded; the coalition agreement provides for working on it during the term (Persoons, pp.20-21).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The 'financial' model (producers reimburse, public bodies keep doing the cleaning) is the politically safest design: it captures the money while neutralising the privatisation fear that an operational-EPR model would trigger from unions and the left.\n- The €465 million 'savings' figure reframes five years of institutional delay as a direct transfer from the public purse to industry — a useful frame for proponents to argue urgency and for critics to attack Belgium's federal complexity.\n- The unresolved inter-municipal distribution key (left to a future decree) is where the real money fight will play out: 54% sounds settled, but who among the 19 communes gets what is not, so the headline split can still be hollowed in implementation.\n- The deposit-return scheme being explicitly left open is a deliberate fudge papering over a real majority split (Groen/Ecolo pro-consigne, MR wary of disrupting the blue-bag chain), deferred to avoid blocking the broader text.\n- The cost-pass-through worry (households/small shops paying instead of polluters) is the genuine equity hinge: whether EPR bites producers or just relabels a consumer charge depends on enforcement detail the ordinance does not itself guarantee.", "proposed_by": "Brussels regional government project of ordinance (A-264), defended in the Environment commission by secretary of state Ans Persoons; assent to the interregional cooperation agreement of 9 February 2026.", "doc_ref": "A-264", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00021/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "PTB": "abstain", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for", "TFA": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00023-A-267", "date": "2026-03-27", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00023", "point": 3, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Budget", "topic": "Public finance", "headline": "Adopt the Brussels-Capital Region's 2026 revenue budget (\"voies et moyens\"), fixing forecast receipts and the year's borrowing authorisations to fund regional spending.", "title": "Projet d'ordonnance contenant le budget des voies et moyens de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale pour l'année budgétaire 2026. Après les douzièmes provisoires, la Région se dote enfin d'un véritable budget initial, fixant le solde SEC à moins de 957 millions d'euros et amorçant une trajectoire de retour à l'équilibre à l'horizon 2029.", "raw_outcome": "Projet d'ordonnance contenant le budget des voies et moyens de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale pour l'année budgétaire 2026. Après les douzièmes provisoires, la Région se dote enfin d'un véritable budget initial, fixant le solde SEC à moins de 957 millions d'euros et amorçant une trajectoire de retour à l'équilibre à l'horizon 2029.", "source_brief": "This is the income side of the Region's budget for 2026 — the receipts it expects to collect and the borrowing it is authorised to take on. It matters because, for the first time in over a year, the Region is setting a full initial budget rather than improvising.\n\n- **First real budget in 615 days.** Brussels had been running on five successive sets of provisional \"twelfths\" — monthly stopgaps that kept the lights on but rolled deficits forward with no clear plan.\n- **A deep hole to fill.** The Region starts from a structural deficit cited in debate at around 1.5 billion euros, with a stated goal of reaching balance by 2029.\n- **Heavy reliance on borrowing.** Interest on the existing debt alone is set to cost about 500 million euros in 2026; the revenue side has to be read against that load.\n- **The rub:** balancing the books means either raising more revenue or borrowing more — and critics argue the receipts here are optimistic and the real adjustment is being pushed onto future years.", "deep_facts": "- The text is the \"projet d'ordonnance contenant le budget des voies et moyens de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale pour l'année budgétaire 2026\" (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- It follows a period in which the Region operated on provisional twelfths; the budget minister referred to \"615 jours\" and \"cinq budgets provisoires successifs\" before this initial budget (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- The 2026 budget fixes the SEC/ESR balance at under -957 million euros (\"le solde SEC à moins de 957 millions d'euros\"), with a stated trajectory back to balance by 2029 (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- The minister stated the budget improves by 591 million euros versus the previous year, citing the Court of Auditors (Cour des comptes), equal to about 9% of revenues (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- Interest on the debt is forecast at around 500 million euros in 2026; a 1-percentage-point rise in rates would add about 150 million euros (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- A debt ceiling of 3 billion euros was set; direct debt is estimated at 14.6 billion euros in 2026, rising to 15.6 (2027), 16.2 (2028) and 16.4 billion euros in 2029 (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- Regional debt was cited as having risen from 6 billion euros in 2019 to about 15 billion euros (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- Opposition members put the underlying deficit and off-budget operations differently, citing 400 million euros for the regional housing company (SLRB), 180 million for Vivaqua and 60 million for Kanal-Centre Pompidou, and a projected cumulative debt of 19.1 billion euros by 2029 (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The revenue budget is the lever that lets the Region keep borrowing: voting it sets how much new debt the executive can legally raise this year.\n- The fight is less about the numbers on the page than about whether the receipts are realistic — if they are over-stated, the gap re-appears as deeper borrowing later.\n- Keeping large operations (housing, water, Kanal) off-budget flatters the headline balance, which is precisely the transparency point the Court of Auditors keeps flagging.", "proposed_by": "Brussels-Capital Government", "doc_ref": "A-267", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00023/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "PTB": "against", "VOOR": "for", "DEFI": "against", "TFA": "against", "VB": "against", "Ecolo": "against", "NVA": "against"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00023-A-268", "date": "2026-03-27", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00023", "point": 11, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Budget", "topic": "Public finance", "headline": "Adopt the Brussels-Capital Region's 2026 general expenditure budget (\"budget des dépenses\"), authorising the year's spending and running a budget deficit.", "title": "Projet d'ordonnance contenant le budget général des dépenses de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale pour l'année budgétaire 2026. Les primes du régime des agents contractuels subventionnés vont être réduites, Activa va être supprimé, les moyens financiers dédiés à l'emploi vont être diminués.", "raw_outcome": "Projet d'ordonnance contenant le budget général des dépenses de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale pour l'année budgétaire 2026. Les primes du régime des agents contractuels subventionnés vont être réduites, Activa va être supprimé, les moyens financiers dédiés à l'emploi vont être diminués.", "source_brief": "This is the spending side of the Region's 2026 budget — the credits each policy area is authorised to spend. Paired with the revenue text, it is the Region's first full initial budget after a long run on provisional twelfths.\n\n- **It sets the deficit.** The 2026 budget is planned to close in the red, with the SEC/ESR balance fixed at under -957 million euros.\n- **From stopgap to plan.** For more than a year the Region spent through monthly provisional twelfths, which guaranteed continuity of public services but rolled deficits forward without a clear direction.\n- **Savings land on real programmes.** Debate flagged cuts touching employment measures — reduced subsidised-contract premiums and the end of the Activa scheme among them.\n- **The rub:** the only ways to shrink a 1.5-billion-euro deficit are to cut spending or raise revenue — and where the axe falls (employment, social programmes) is exactly what the budget is fought over.", "deep_facts": "- The text is the \"projet d'ordonnance contenant le budget général des dépenses de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale pour l'année budgétaire 2026\" (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- It is the companion expenditure text to the 2026 revenue budget, the Region having previously operated on provisional twelfths (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- The 2026 budget fixes the SEC/ESR balance at under -957 million euros, with a stated return to balance targeted for 2029, from a deficit cited at about 1.5 billion euros (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- The minister stated the budget improves by 591 million euros versus the prior year, per the Court of Auditors, about 9% of revenues (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- Debate cited spending cuts in employment policy: reduced premiums for subsidised contractual agents (ACS), suppression of the Activa scheme, and a lower employment budget; one figure given was 16,500 insertion pathways concerned (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- Several amendments were tabled to the expenditure budget, including ones touching the totals of Bruxelles Environnement and STIB programmes (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- Investment in housing in 2026 was cited at around 150 million euros (compared to the debt-interest charge of about 500 million euros) (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The expenditure budget is where the abstract \"reduce the deficit\" becomes concrete: every credit line cut is a programme that shrinks, which is why employment measures dominated the debate.\n- Pairing a -957-million-euro planned deficit with a 2029 balance target signals front-loaded ambition — the political risk is that the cuts needed to hit that path arrive faster than the affected sectors can absorb.\n- Reading this text against the revenue text shows the real choice: the same gap can be closed by spending less here or by borrowing/taxing more there.", "proposed_by": "Brussels-Capital Government", "doc_ref": "A-268", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00023/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "PTB": "against", "VOOR": "for", "DEFI": "against", "TFA": "against", "VB": "against", "Ecolo": "against", "NVA": "against"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00023-A-270", "date": "2026-03-27", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00023", "point": 12, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Budget", "topic": "Public finance", "headline": "Enact the formal regulation carrying the 2026 revenue budget of the Brussels Agglomeration, the companion text to the Region's own revenue budget.", "title": "Projet de règlement contenant le budget des voies et moyens de l'Agglomération de Bruxelles pour l'année budgétaire 2026.", "raw_outcome": "Projet de règlement contenant le budget des voies et moyens de l'Agglomération de Bruxelles pour l'année budgétaire 2026.", "source_brief": "This is the formal regulation (\"règlement\") setting the 2026 revenue budget of the Brussels Agglomeration, the small legacy entity whose budget travels alongside the Region's. It carries the same 2026 context, the Region having spent over a year on provisional twelfths.\n\n- **A procedural twin.** It mirrors the main revenue budget for the Agglomeration's separate accounts.\n- **The rub:** it is technical, but voting it through is part of finally closing the door on budgeting by monthly stopgap.", "deep_facts": "- The text is the \"projet de règlement contenant le budget des voies et moyens de l'Agglomération de Bruxelles pour l'année budgétaire 2026\" (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- It is the Agglomeration's revenue companion to the Region's 2026 voies-et-moyens budget; the regulation is submitted for sanction by the government (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- It shares the same 2026 initial-budget context that followed the period of provisional twelfths (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- This is a near-mechanical companion vote: the Agglomeration's accounts must move in lockstep with the Region's, so the substantive choices sit in the main revenue budget, not here.", "proposed_by": "Brussels-Capital Government", "doc_ref": "A-270", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00023/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "PTB": "against", "VOOR": "for", "DEFI": "against", "TFA": "against", "VB": "against", "Ecolo": "against", "NVA": "against"}, "curator_drop": true}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00023-A-271", "date": "2026-03-27", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00023", "point": 13, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Budget", "topic": "Public finance", "headline": "Enact the formal regulation carrying the 2026 general expenditure budget of the Brussels Agglomeration, the companion text to the Region's own expenditure budget.", "title": "Projet de règlement contenant le budget général des dépenses de l'Agglomération de Bruxelles pour l'année budgétaire 2026.", "raw_outcome": "Projet de règlement contenant le budget général des dépenses de l'Agglomération de Bruxelles pour l'année budgétaire 2026.", "source_brief": "This is the formal regulation (\"règlement\") setting the 2026 expenditure budget of the Brussels Agglomeration, the legacy entity whose accounts run alongside the Region's. It carries the same 2026 context, after a long stretch of provisional twelfths.\n\n- **A procedural twin.** It mirrors the main expenditure budget for the Agglomeration's separate accounts.\n- **The rub:** procedural, but it completes the set of texts that replace budgeting by monthly stopgap with a full 2026 budget.", "deep_facts": "- The text is the \"projet de règlement contenant le budget général des dépenses de l'Agglomération de Bruxelles pour l'année budgétaire 2026\" (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- It is the Agglomeration's expenditure companion to the Region's 2026 expenditure budget; the regulation is submitted for sanction by the government (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- It shares the same 2026 initial-budget context that followed the period of provisional twelfths (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- A companion vote with no independent stake of its own: the spending choices live in the Region's expenditure budget, which this text simply tracks for the Agglomeration.", "proposed_by": "Brussels-Capital Government", "doc_ref": "A-271", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00023/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "PTB": "against", "VOOR": "for", "DEFI": "against", "TFA": "against", "VB": "against", "Ecolo": "against", "NVA": "against"}, "curator_drop": true}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00023-A-263", "date": "2026-03-27", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00023", "point": 15, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance", "topic": "Pensions", "headline": "Amend the pension rules for former Brussels MPs to let their pensions be indexed again, lifting the freeze that follows from the federal salary-indexation pause.", "title": "Proposition de décision visant à permettre l'indexation des pensions des anciens députés bruxellois. Comme le montant de ces pensions est calculé sur la base du salaire des députés de la Chambre des représentants, et que le niveau fédéral a décidé de ne plus indexer ces salaires jusqu'en 2029, les pensions des députés bruxellois à la retraite sont automatiquement bloquées, sauf si une décision permet le contraire.", "raw_outcome": "Proposition de décision visant à permettre l'indexation des pensions des anciens députés bruxellois. Comme le montant de ces pensions est calculé sur la base du salaire des députés de la Chambre des représentants, et que le niveau fédéral a décidé de ne plus indexer ces salaires jusqu'en 2029, les pensions des députés bruxellois à la retraite sont automatiquement bloquées, sauf si une décision permet le contraire.", "source_brief": "This changes the rules on pensions paid to former members of the Brussels Parliament so they can be indexed — raised in line with the cost of living — again. The catch is a chain reaction from the federal level.\n\n- **Why they are frozen.** These pensions are calculated on the salary of federal Chamber MPs. The federal level decided not to index those salaries until 2029, so the Brussels pensions are automatically blocked unless a separate decision says otherwise.\n- **What it does.** It creates the legal basis to index the pensions despite that federal freeze, with an amendment making it take effect from 1 March 2026.\n- **The numbers in play.** Debate cited an average former-MP pension of about 2,000 euros a month, with older mandates reaching far higher.\n- **The rub:** indexing protects retirees against inflation — but applied here it lifts pensions already described as several times the average, raising the fairness question of who gets shielded from a freeze that hits everyone else.", "deep_facts": "- The text is a \"proposition de décision visant à modifier le règlement fixant le régime des pensions attribuées aux anciens membres du Parlement de la Région de Bruxelles-Capitale en vue de l'indexation des pensions\" (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- Former Brussels MPs' pensions are calculated on the salary of federal Chamber of Representatives MPs; the federal level decided not to index those salaries until 2029, which automatically freezes the Brussels pensions \"sauf si une décision permet le contraire\" (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- An amendment by two members gives the change retroactive effect from 1 March 2026 (\"L'article 1er produit ses effets le 1er mars 2026\"), with the pension fund (Caisse de retraite) to execute it without delay (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- Debate cited the average former-MP pension at about 2,000 euros per month, corresponding to nine years of parliamentary mandate (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- For a five-year mandate after 2019, the pension was cited at roughly 800 euros gross per month; for earlier mandates it could reach about 7,800 euros gross per month (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- The Wijninckx ceiling was cited at 8,329 euros gross per month; debate noted past cases of MPs receiving up to 20% above it, since ended, and that fourteen former Brussels MPs who litigated to exceed the ceiling were all dismissed (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).\n- For comparison, Statbel figures cited put the 2025 average pension at 2,252 euros for men and 1,853 euros gross for women (CRI, 27 Mar 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The mechanism is the real story: by pegging its pensions to a federal salary the Region cannot control, Brussels imported a freeze it never voted for — this text is the manual override.\n- Framing it as \"indexation\" makes it sound like routine inflation protection, but applied to pensions cited at multiples of the national average it is politically a top-up, which is the wedge the opposition presses.\n- The 1 March 2026 retroactivity means the override is designed to bridge the freeze seamlessly, leaving no gap month — a sign the drafters wanted continuity, not a one-off catch-up.", "proposed_by": "Brussels-Capital Parliament (cross-party)", "doc_ref": "A-263", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00023/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "PTB": "against", "VB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00024-A-274", "date": "2026-04-24", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00024", "point": 1, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Ordinance", "topic": "Crypto tax reporting", "headline": "Update the legal definition of a \"distributed-ledger address\" (a blockchain/crypto wallet address) in Brussels tax law, so the Region's reporting rules track the EU framework for taxing crypto-assets.", "title": "Projet d'ordonnance modifiant la définition de l'adresse de registre distribué dans l'article 5, 31°, de l'ordonnance du 26 juillet 2013 transposant la directive 2011/16/UE du Conseil du 15 février 2011 relative à la coopération administrative dans le domaine fiscal et abrogeant la directive 77/799/CEE. M. Gilles Verstraeten, rapporteur, se réfère au rapport écrit.", "raw_outcome": "Projet d'ordonnance modifiant la définition de l'adresse de registre distribué dans l'article 5, 31°, de l'ordonnance du 26 juillet 2013 transposant la directive 2011/16/UE du Conseil du 15 février 2011 relative à la coopération administrative dans le domaine fiscal et abrogeant la directive 77/799/CEE. M. Gilles Verstraeten, rapporteur, se réfère au rapport écrit.", "source_brief": "This is a narrow, technical fix to Brussels tax law: it rewrites the definition of an \"adresse de registre distribué\" — in plain terms, a blockchain or crypto wallet address — inside article 5, 31° of the 26 July 2013 ordinance that transposed EU Directive 2011/16/UE on administrative cooperation between tax authorities. The point is to keep the Region's crypto-asset reporting rules aligned with the EU's tax framework, so that holdings and transfers tied to wallet addresses can be reported and exchanged between tax administrations.\n\n- **What it actually changes:** one definition in an existing 2013 ordinance — not blockchain regulation in general, just the term the tax-reporting rules hang on.\n- **Why now:** it brings Brussels into line with the EU's expanding cooperation framework for taxing crypto-assets, where wallet addresses are the hook for identifying who owes what.\n- **Who it touches:** people and platforms holding or moving crypto-assets, whose wallet data feeds cross-border tax-information exchange.\n- **The rub:** is keeping tax authorities able to trace crypto wallets a sensible plug of an avoidance gap, or an extension of fiscal surveillance into a space people expected to stay private? Voting for adopts the update; voting against leaves the older definition in place.", "deep_facts": "- The text is a projet d'ordonnance modifying the definition of \"adresse de registre distribué\" (distributed-ledger / blockchain address) in article 5, 31° of the ordinance of 26 July 2013 (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- That 2013 ordinance transposed Council Directive 2011/16/UE of 15 February 2011 on administrative cooperation in the field of taxation, repealing Directive 77/799/CEE (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- The amendment concerns the definition used for tax-reporting/cooperation purposes; it does not regulate blockchain technology generally (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- In the plenary, the rapporteur (M. Gilles Verstraeten) referred to the written report; the general discussion was opened and closed without floor debate (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- Articles 1, then 2 to 3, were examined without observation in the article-by-article discussion ahead of the nominal vote on the whole (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- Reference numbers: A-274/1, 2 et 3 – 2025/2026 (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The change reads as a DAC8-type alignment: the EU has been extending Directive 2011/16/UE to cover crypto-assets, and a stale wallet-address definition would leave Brussels' reporting rules out of step with that exchange of tax information.\n- The absence of floor debate signals this was treated as a consensual technical transposition rather than a contested policy choice — the substantive arguments (crypto avoidance vs. fiscal privacy) sit at the EU level, not in this single-definition tweak.\n- For a citizen, the live question is less about this clause than about the direction it serves: tighter traceability of crypto holdings for tax purposes.", "proposed_by": "Brussels-Capital Government", "doc_ref": "A-274", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00024/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "TFA": "for", "MR": "for", "PTB": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "DEFI": "for", "CDV": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "VB": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00024-A-273", "date": "2026-04-24", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00024", "point": 2, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Motion", "topic": "Police-zone merger", "headline": "Decline to raise a conflict-of-interest objection against federal bill 56-1342, letting the federal reform that merges Brussels' six local police zones into one proceed without the Region forcing a formal pause for inter-parliamentary talks.", "title": "Conclusions de la commission tendant au rejet de la proposition de motion relative à un conflit d'intérêts avec la Chambre des représentants concernant le projet de loi n° 56-1342 modifiant la loi du 7 décembre 1998 organisant un service de police intégré structuré à deux niveaux. Quelle que soit votre position à propos de la fusion des zones de police, si vous voulez faire en sorte que Bruxelles soit entendue, il faut voter en faveur de cette motion.", "raw_outcome": "Conclusions de la commission tendant au rejet de la proposition de motion relative à un conflit d'intérêts avec la Chambre des représentants concernant le projet de loi n° 56-1342 modifiant la loi du 7 décembre 1998 organisant un service de police intégré structuré à deux niveaux. Quelle que soit votre position à propos de la fusion des zones de police, si vous voulez faire en sorte que Bruxelles soit entendue, il faut voter en faveur de cette motion.", "source_brief": "Federal bill 56-1342 would merge Brussels' six local police zones into a single zone. A conflict-of-interest motion was tabled to force a formal pause and oblige the federal and regional parliaments to talk before the bill is finalised — a procedural lever, not an outright veto, meant to make Brussels' voice heard on a reform that reshapes how its policing is organised. What is on the table now is the committee's recommendation to **not pursue** that objection.\n\n- **What you are voting on:** the committee's conclusion to set the conflict-of-interest motion aside. Voting **for** = decline to raise the objection and let the federal police-zone reform run its course. Voting **against** = pursue the objection and trigger the inter-parliamentary pause.\n- **The case to pursue it:** supporters argue no Brussels security actor — the 19 mayors, the public prosecutor, the chief commissioners, the police unions — backs the forced merger, that Brussels police are underfunded by an estimated 80 million euros a year, and that the Region is being reshaped without a say.\n- **The case to let it proceed:** others point to Brussels' security record — 62 shootings in 2023, 92 in 2024, 101 in 2025, and 19% of residents feeling often or always unsafe — and treat the merger as an overdue, necessary response.\n- **The rub:** should Brussels spend its institutional lever to stall a federal security reform it had little hand in, or step aside and let it happen? The direction matters — a \"for\" here clears the merger's path; an \"against\" puts it on hold.", "deep_facts": "- The item is the committee's \"conclusions tendant au rejet\" of the proposition de motion relative to a conflict of interest with the Chamber of Representatives concerning federal bill n° 56-1342 (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- Bill 56-1342 modifies the law of 7 December 1998 organising an integrated, two-level police service and the law of 26 April 2002 on essential elements of the status of police-service personnel; it provides for merging Brussels' six local police zones into one (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- The conflict-of-interest mechanism is described as an institutional tool to make institutional loyalty function: when one entity of the federal state takes a decision affecting another, the latter may request to be heard and to open discussion between parliaments — a pause, not a veto (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- Proponents stated that all 19 Brussels mayors had criticised the merger, including some now in government (Henry, Laaouej, Dilliès), and that the public prosecutor (M. Moinil), chief commissioners and police unions did not advocate for it (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- Figures cited for underfunding: federal dotation of about 37,000 euros per year per Brussels officer versus 50,000 euros per federal officer (KUL norm); Bruxelles Pouvoirs locaux estimated the shortfall at about 80 million euros per year (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- Federal minister Quintin was cited as proposing 55 million euros over five years (about 11 million per year) for the police reform, characterised by proponents as insufficient against an 80-million-per-year structural deficit (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- The KUL norm dates from 2002, based on 1999 population figures; proponents noted Brussels' population had since grown by roughly 25% without a corresponding revision (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- Arguments for the merger cited the Flemish Peace Institute ranking Brussels among Europe's top three cities for gang violence and shootings, alongside Naples and Marseille; shootings reported as 62 in 2023, 92 in 2024 and 101 in 2025; 19% of residents feeling often or always unsafe in their neighbourhood, 42% fearing aggression, 30% theft (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- The federal bill on the merger was reported as adopted in federal committee less than two days before this sitting, with no accompanying text on a KUL-norm reform (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).\n- Reference numbers: A-273/1, 2 et 3 – 2025/2026 (CRI, 24 Apr 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The framing as \"conclusions tending to reject the motion\" inverts the intuitive direction: a vote in favour does not endorse the conflict-of-interest objection — it endorses setting it aside, clearing the path for the federal merger. Voters who care about the substance must read past the procedural wrapper.\n- The conflict-of-interest motion is as much about institutional leverage as about policing: proponents repeatedly framed it as a test of whether Brussels MPs answer to their region or to their (largely Walloon/Flemish-led) federal party lines.\n- The funding figures (37k vs 50k per officer, an 80M/year gap, 55M over five years) are the proponents' core argument that a merger without refinancing changes the org chart but not the resourcing — while the security statistics (rising shootings, fear indicators) are the counter-frame for treating the merger as urgent.\n- That the federal bill cleared committee two days earlier, without the promised KUL-norm reform, is the timing wedge proponents used to argue the pause is the Region's last realistic moment to be heard.", "proposed_by": "C. Jodogne, B. Clerfayt, J. Maison, J. De Patoul, Z. Khattabi, F. Maingain (DéFI / Ecolo)", "doc_ref": "A-273", "outcome": "approved", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00024/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PS": "for", "MR": "for", "OVLD": "for", "LE": "for", "GRN": "for", "CDV": "for", "VOOR": "for", "NVA": "for", "DEFI": "against", "TFA": "against", "PTB": "against", "Ecolo": "against", "VB": "against"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00025-v1", "date": "2026-05-22", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00025", "point": 1, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Urgency request", "topic": "Housing inquiry", "headline": "Vote to fast-track onto today's agenda a proposal opening a parliamentary commission of inquiry into how the Anderlecht public social-housing company is run.", "title": "« Je demande donc non seulement la prise en consideration en urgence de ce texte, mais aussi l'instauration en urgence de la commission d'enquete. Nous ne pouvons pas nous permettre d'attendre trois mois. » La demande porte sur l'instauration d'une commission d'enquete parlementaire concernant la gestion du Foyer anderlechtois (n° A-293/1 – 2025/2026), a la suite d'un reportage de l'emission « Pano ».", "raw_outcome": "« Je demande donc non seulement la prise en consideration en urgence de ce texte, mais aussi l'instauration en urgence de la commission d'enquete. Nous ne pouvons pas nous permettre d'attendre trois mois. » La demande porte sur l'instauration d'une commission d'enquete parlementaire concernant la gestion du Foyer anderlechtois (n° A-293/1 – 2025/2026), a la suite d'un reportage de l'emission « Pano ».", "source_brief": "The request would suspend the normal timetable and put a proposal to open a parliamentary commission of inquiry into the management of the Foyer anderlechtois — a public social-housing company — straight onto today's agenda, rather than waiting for a committee meeting weeks away.\n\n- **What triggered it:** a VRT *Pano* broadcast alleging \"a system of abuse of power and clientelism\" inside the company, including irregular allocation of social housing.\n- **What an inquiry would do:** a parliamentary commission of inquiry has investigative powers to examine the facts; backers stress it is not in itself a verdict of guilt.\n- **The clock:** if not fast-tracked, the text would go to the Finance and General Affairs Committee for its sitting on 1 June.\n- **The rub:** whether grave allegations justify expediting an inquiry the same day, or whether the case is better served by the normal committee route a few days later — especially with a judicial investigation already open.", "deep_facts": "- The text is proposal A-293/1 – 2025/2026, seeking a parliamentary commission of inquiry into the management of the Foyer anderlechtois, a public social-housing company (societe immobiliere de service public, SISP) (CRI, 22 May 2026).\n- The request is to modify the order of the day to add the urgent taking-into-consideration AND immediate urgent debate of the text; absent urgency, it would be referred to the Finance and General Affairs Committee meeting of 1 June (CRI, 22 May 2026).\n- The trigger cited is a VRT *Pano* report broadcast that week describing, per speakers, \"clientelisme, interventionnisme, non-respect des regles et violation de la vie privee\" and \"un systeme d'abus et de clientelisme\" at the Foyer anderlechtois (CRI, 22 May 2026).\n- Speakers state a judicial investigation (\"une enquete judiciaire\") has already been opened into the matter (CRI, 22 May 2026).\n- Some speakers additionally asked that the company's president and Anderlecht housing alderman, Lotfi Mostefa, be suspended from his functions while the inquiry runs; backers underline an inquiry does not equal a condemnation (CRI, 22 May 2026).\n- Procedurally the president put the question of referral to the 1 June committee first; the proposers insisted on a vote on urgency instead, so the urgency was put to a vote of the 77 members present (CRI, 22 May 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- Fast-tracking the same day is about control of the narrative as much as the calendar: a media scandal loses momentum within days, so backers want the inquiry visibly launched while attention is high.\n- The parallel demand to suspend the company president folds a personnel sanction into a procedural urgency vote, raising the stakes well above scheduling.\n- The 1 June committee date functions as the off-ramp: opposing urgency does not require opposing an inquiry, which lets reluctant members back the substance while resisting the rushed format.\n- An open judicial probe gives sceptics a ready argument that a parliamentary inquiry the same day risks cutting across the courts.", "proposed_by": "Kalvin Soiresse Njall, Zakia Khattabi, Alain Maron, Joelle Maison (Ecolo)", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "rejected", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00025/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PTB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "VB": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "NVA": "for", "PS": "against", "MR": "against", "OVLD": "against", "LE": "against", "GRN": "against", "CDV": "against", "VOOR": "against", "TFA": "abstain"}, "curator_drop": false}, {"id": "BXL-2526-00025-v2", "date": "2026-05-22", "session_code": "PB_2025-26_00025", "point": 2, "organ": "Parlement bruxellois", "type": "Urgency request", "topic": "Tuition conflict-of-interest", "headline": "Vote to fast-track onto the agenda a conflict-of-interest motion against a French Community draft decree that would raise university tuition toward 1,250 euros, before that assembly votes next week.", "title": "« Je demande egalement l'urgence pour un deuxieme texte, qui consiste en une motion en conflit d'interets a la suite du debat en Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles sur l'augmentation du minerval a 1.250 euros (n° A-294/1 – 2025/2026). » L'urgence est invoquee parce que le vote en seance pleniere du Parlement de la Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles doit avoir lieu mercredi, le but etant d'evaluer l'impact sur les finances communales et les CPAS.", "raw_outcome": "« Je demande egalement l'urgence pour un deuxieme texte, qui consiste en une motion en conflit d'interets a la suite du debat en Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles sur l'augmentation du minerval a 1.250 euros (n° A-294/1 – 2025/2026). » L'urgence est invoquee parce que le vote en seance pleniere du Parlement de la Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles doit avoir lieu mercredi, le but etant d'evaluer l'impact sur les finances communales et les CPAS.", "source_brief": "The request would put a conflict-of-interest motion straight onto the agenda now, so Brussels could act before the Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles (the French Community) holds its own plenary vote the following Wednesday.\n\n- **The target:** a draft programme-decree of the French Community that includes raising the university registration fee (minerval) toward 1,250 euros.\n- **The Brussels angle:** backers argue the measure would shift extra costs onto Brussels municipalities and their public welfare centres (CPAS), on top of other cost transfers.\n- **Why now:** the French Community's plenary vote was due the following Wednesday, leaving a narrow window to register a conflict-of-interest objection.\n- **The rub:** whether to drop this onto today's agenda to beat a neighbouring assembly's clock, or hold to the normal process even at the risk of missing that window.", "deep_facts": "- The text is a conflict-of-interest motion, proposal A-294/1 – 2025/2026, concerning a debate in the Federation Wallonie-Bruxelles (French Community) on raising the minerval (university registration fee) \"a 1.250 euros\" (CRI, 22 May 2026).\n- The stated ground for urgency is that the plenary vote in the French Community Parliament was due the following Wednesday; backers wanted time \"d'evaluer l'impact de cette nouvelle mesure sur les finances communales, singulierement celles des CPAS\" (CRI, 22 May 2026).\n- Backers frame the measure as a potential \"report de charges\" (cost transfer) onto Brussels municipalities and CPAS, adding to transfers attributed to federal government decisions (CRI, 22 May 2026).\n- In debate, figures cited for the fee impact varied by speaker: one stated current fees \"entre 175 et 600 euros\" rising \"jusqu'a 1.200 euros\"; the originating request referenced 1.250 euros (CRI, 22 May 2026).\n- The request was to add the urgent taking-into-consideration of the text to the order of the day; absent urgency it would follow the normal procedure rather than being taken up the same day (CRI, 22 May 2026).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n\n- A conflict-of-interest motion is a delaying lever: its value depends almost entirely on timing, so urgency is not a detail here but the whole point — miss the window and the instrument is spent.\n- Framing a French-Community education measure as a Brussels budget problem (municipalities, CPAS) reroutes a debate the region cannot directly settle into one it can vote on.\n- The differing fee figures (1.200 vs 1.250 euros) reflect the measure being mid-process in another assembly, so the exact number was still moving when urgency was sought.\n- Voting against urgency need not mean endorsing the fee hike — it can simply reject using this assembly's agenda to intervene in another's pending vote.", "proposed_by": "Ecolo", "doc_ref": null, "outcome": "rejected", "source_url": "http://weblex.brussels/data/crb/cri/2025-26/00025/images.pdf", "party_votes_canon": {"PTB": "for", "DEFI": "for", "Ecolo": "for", "TFA": "for", "VB": "for", "NVA": "for", "PS": "against", "MR": "against", "OVLD": "against", "LE": "against", "GRN": "against", "CDV": "against", "VOOR": "against"}, "curator_drop": false}]};